ve that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\forall a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\;:\\;(a,b)\\text{ lies on at least one of the lines}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nmust contain at least one *sunny* line (i.e. a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThe proof is by mathematical induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe points required by (1) are  \n\n\\[\nS_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\) and can contain at most the two points \\((1,c)\\) and \\((2,c)\\); thus it can cover at most two points of \\(S_{3}\\).  \nSimilarly, a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)) can cover at most two points of \\(S_{3}\\).  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); because all points of \\(S_{3}\\) have distinct values of \\(x+y\\) (namely \\(2,3,3\\)), such a line can contain at most one point of \\(S_{3}\\).\n\nSince we are allowed only three distinct lines, covering the three points of \\(S_{3}\\) with only non‑sunny lines would require at least one line to contain two points and the remaining two lines to contain the third point each. The only way a non‑sunny line could contain two points of \\(S_{3}\\) is if those two points share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate (vertical line) or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate (horizontal line). The set \\(S_{3}\\) contains the pairs \\((1,1),(1,2)\\) (same \\(x\\)) and \\((1,1),(2,1)\\) (same \\(y\\)), but whichever of these two pairs we choose, the third point is \\((2,1)\\) or \\((1,2)\\) respectively, which is not collinear with the chosen pair on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Consequently at least one of the three lines must have a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. at least one sunny line is necessary.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### Inductive hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the