on is that the set \\(S_n\\) can be covered by the three families \\(\\{y=j\\},\\{x=j\\},\\{x+y=j\\}\\) using at most \\(n\\) lines: simply take all horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\), all vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) and the diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,n+1-\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\). This yields a covering with exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\n   To obtain a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace any \\(k\\) of the non\u2011sunny lines by the sunny lines \\(L_i\\). Because each sunny line \\(L_i\\) passes through the origin, it contains the point \\((1,1)\\); moreover, by choosing the slopes \\(m_i\\) rational with denominator at most \\(n\\) we can guarantee that each sunny line also passes through a distinct point of \\(S_n\\) that is not already covered by the remaining non\u2011sunny lines. Consequently the removal of the replaced non\u2011sunny lines does not create uncovered points.  \n\n   A concrete algorithm is:\n\n   1. Set \\(h=v=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{3}\\right\\rfloor\\) and \\(d=n-k-2h\\) (so \\(h+v+d=n-k\\)).  \n   2. Use the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,h\\), the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,v\\) and the diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,d+1\\).  \n   3. For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=i+1\\) by the sunny line \\(L_i:y=m_i x\\) where \\(m_i=\\frac{i}{n}\\) (a rational slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\n   The replaced diagonal line \\(x+y=i+1\\) originally covered all points with \\(a+b=i+1\\). The new sunny line \\(L_i\\) contains the point \\((i,n-i)\\) (since \\(n-i = m_i i\\)), which also satisfies \\(i+(n-i)=n\\le n+1\\). Hence every point that was previously covered by the removed diagonal line is still covered either by another diagonal line (if its sum is larger) or by the new sunny line. All lines are distinct because the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct and none coincides with the slopes of the remaining horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines.\n\n   Thus we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that satisfies the covering condition.\n\nCombining the necessity and sufficiency parts yields the complete characterisation of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nWe must determine, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), the set  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\;\\text{a family }\\mathcal L\\text{ of }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }S_n\n\\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  No configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nWe prove that \\(0\\notin K_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Base case \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\); a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most one.  \nSince we have only three lines, covering the three points with only non\u2011sunny lines would require one of the lines to contain two points and the other two lines each to contain the remaining point. The only way a non\u2011sunny line can contain two points of \\(S_3\\) is if those points share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate (vertical) or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate (horizontal). Whichever pair we choose, the third point is not collinear with the pair on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Hence at least one line must have a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. a sunny line is unavoidable.\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) every family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) contains at least one sunny line.\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nConsider \\(S_{n+1}\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}=S_n\\cup\\{(1,n+1),(n+1,1),(1,1)\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and containing **no** sunny line.  \nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are therefore parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nRemove from \\(\\mathcal L\\) the three lines (if they appear) that are forced solely by the three extra points in (1):\n\n* the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\) (covers \\((1,n+1)\\));\n* the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) (covers \\((n+1,1)\\));\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=2\\) (covers \\((1,1)\\)).\n\nAt most three lines are removed, leaving a collection \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of at least \\((n+1)-3=n-2\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are still covered, because the only points that could have become uncovered are the three removed ones, which do not belong to \\(S_n\\).  \n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L'|<n\\) we may adjoin arbitrary new non\u2011sunny lines (for instance, vertical lines \\(x=n+2,n+3,\\dots\\)) until we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines; this does not affect the covering of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\), contradicting the inductive hypothesis.  \nTherefore any covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines must contain at least one sunny line.\n\nBy induction, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines necessarily includes a sunny line. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n0\\notin K_n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\ge1\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThe construction proceeds in two stages.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  A covering with only non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nh=v=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Big\\rfloor,\\qquad d=n-2h .\n\\]\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* Horizontal lines \\(H_j:\\; y=j\\) for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,h\\);\n* Vertical lines   \\(V_j:\\; x=j\\) for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,v\\);\n* Diagonal lines   \\(D_j:\\; x+y=j+1\\) for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,d\\).\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nWe claim that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nIndeed, let \\(s=a+b\\).  \nIf \\(s\\le h+1\\) then \\(b\\le h\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nIf \\(s\\le v+1\\) then \\(a\\le v\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nOtherwise \\(s\\ge \\max\\{h+2,v+2\\}\\). Since \\(s\\le n+1\\) and \\(d=n-2h\\ge s-(h+1)\\), we have \\(s-1\\in\\{2,\\dots ,d+1\\}\\); consequently the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\{H_j\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\}\\cup\\{D_j\\}\\) cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Replacing diagonal lines by sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nFrom the covering above we will replace exactly \\(k\\) diagonal lines by \\(k\\) sunny lines, keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nChoose distinct rational numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{i}{n}\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nso that each \\(m_i\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nare sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\nReplace the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\; x+y=i+1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nby the sunny lines \\(L_i\\).  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of\n\n* the \\(h\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,h\\);\n* the \\(v\\) vertical lines   \\(x=1,\\dots ,v\\);\n* the remaining \\(d-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=j+1\\) for \\(j=k+1,\\dots ,d\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because the slopes \\(m_i\\) are different from each other and from the slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) of the other families.\n\nIt remains to verify that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) still covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\) was covered by a horizontal or vertical line* in the original covering, it is still covered, as those lines have not been altered.\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\) was covered by a diagonal line \\(D_j\\) with \\(j>k\\)*, that diagonal line is still present, so the point remains covered.\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\) was covered by a diagonal line \\(D_i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)*, then \\(a+b=i+1\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(L_i\\) with slope \\(m_i=i/n\\).  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nm_i a = \\frac{i}{n}\\,a \\le \\frac{i}{n}\\,n = i .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b=i+1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb = i+1-a = i - (a-1) \\ge i - (n-1) .\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb = m_i a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a\\bigl(1+m_i\\bigr)=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m_i=i/n\\), the equality holds precisely when \\(a = n\\) and \\(b = 1\\), i.e. for the point \\((n,1)\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)=(n,1)\\) then it lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\) (because \\(1 = m_i n\\)).  \n\nFor all other points with sum \\(i+1\\) we note that they are also covered by either a horizontal line (if \\(b\\le h\\)) or a vertical line (if \\(a\\le v\\)).  \nIndeed, because \\(h=v=\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\) and \\(i\\le k\\le n\\), at least one of the coordinates of any point with \\(a+b=i+1\\le n+1\\) does not exceed \\(\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\); otherwise both would be larger than \\(\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\) and their sum would exceed \\(2\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\ge n\\), contradicting \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence every such point is still covered by a horizontal or vertical line, or by the sunny line when it is \\((n,1)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) remains on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nConsequently, for each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subseteq K_n . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final characterisation\n\nFrom (2) we know \\(0\\notin K_n\\); from (3) we know every integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) belongs to \\(K_n\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
