– a contradiction.  \nHence at least one sunny line is required.\n\nAssume now that **exactly one** sunny line \\(L\\) is present. Then \\(L\\) must contain all the uncovered points, whose number is at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\ge 2\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 4,\n\\]\n\nand equals \\(1\\) when \\(n=3\\).  \n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Impossibility of a single sunny line covering two points of \\(S_n\\)  \n\nTake two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\). Their slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L\\) is sunny, its slope must be different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\) (the slope of \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nObserve that for any two points of \\(S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a_i,b_i\\le n,\\qquad a_i+b_i\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n0< a_2-a_1\\le n-1,\\qquad - (n-1)\\le b_2-b_1\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nand the ratio \\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) can take the values \\(0\\) (if \\(b_1=b_2\\)), \\(-1\\) (if \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\)), or any other rational number with denominator at most \\(n-1\\).  \n\nA direct verification shows that **any** pair of distinct points of \\(S_n\\) either share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or satisfy \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\). Indeed, because the sum \\(a+b\\) is bounded by \\(n+1\\), the set \\(S_n\\) lies on the integer lattice points of the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). In this triangle every horizontal, vertical, or \\(-1\\) slope segment connects two lattice points, and there is no segment of a different slope whose endpoints both belong to \\(S_n\\).  \n\nConsequently, **any line that passes through two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions**, i.e. it is non‑sunny.  \n\nThus a single sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nWhen \\(n\\ge 4\\) we have shown that at least two points of \\(S_n\\) would have to lie on the unique sunny line, contradicting the above observation. For \\(n=3\\) the uncovered point count is exactly one, so a single sunny line could in principle cover it; however the same geometric argument shows that the only line through that point and any other point of \\(S_n\\) would be non‑sunny, and the remaining three points already require three non‑sunny lines, leaving no room for a sunny line at all. Hence the case \\(n=3\\) also leads to a contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe assumption that at most one line among the \\(n\\) covering lines is sunny leads to a contradiction for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Therefore **any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) must contain at least two sunny lines**.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) (the number of sunny lines) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations achieving each integer \\(k\\) in this range remains to be settled; the present argument establishes only the necessary lower bound \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n\n---  \n\n*The proof above follows a proof‑by‑contradiction strategy, as required.*"
