\\(k\\) is \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\n\\[\nd_1:\\;y=0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\nd_2:\\;x=0\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\nd_3:\\;x+y=0\\quad(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nAll other directions are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) at most one is sunny.  \nHence there are at least \\(n-1\\) **non\u2011sunny** lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  Geometry of a non\u2011sunny line  \n\n*Horizontal.*  A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\) is a positive integer, the only point of \\(S_n\\) on it is \\((1,c)\\) (any larger \\(x\\) would make \\(x+y>n+1\\)).  \nIf \\(c\\) is not a positive integer, the line contains no point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Vertical.*  A line \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\) contains exactly the point \\((c,1)\\); otherwise none.\n\n*Diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)).*  A line \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\) (and \\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) contains exactly the point \\((1,c-1)\\); otherwise none.\n\nConsequently **each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  Counting points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWith at least \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by them is at most \\(n-1\\).  \nFrom (1),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>n-1\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus after the non\u2011sunny lines are placed, at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\ge 1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\) remain uncovered. These points must lie on the (possible) sunny line.\n\nIf there is **no** sunny line, (2) shows that the points of \\(S_n\\) cannot all be covered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nHence at least one sunny line is required.\n\nAssume now that **exactly one** sunny line \\(L\\) is present. Then \\(L\\) must contain all uncovered points, whose number is at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\ge 2\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 4,\n\\]\n\nand equals \\(1\\) when \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  No sunny line can pass through two points of \\(S_n\\)  \n\nTake two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\).  \nBecause both satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) and \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n0<|a_2-a_1|\\le n-1,\\qquad |b_2-b_1|\\le n-1.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the line through them is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection of all possibilities for the integer differences shows that the only slopes that can arise are  \n\n\\[\nm=0,\\qquad m=\\text{undefined (vertical)},\\qquad m=-1.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(a_2-a_1\\neq 0\\) and \\(b_2-b_1\\neq -(a_2-a_1)\\), then the sum of the two points would exceed \\(n+1\\), contradicting their membership in \\(S_n\\).  \nHence **any line containing two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions**, i.e. it is non\u2011sunny.\n\nTherefore a single sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\) we have seen that at least two points would have to lie on the unique sunny line \u2013 impossible.  \nFor \\(n=3\\) the uncovered point count is exactly one, but the three points already require three distinct non\u2011sunny lines (one horizontal, one vertical, one diagonal), leaving no line to be sunny. Hence the case \\(k\\le 1\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nConsequently **any admissible configuration must contain at least two sunny lines**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\ge 2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction showing that every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable  \n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  The basic set of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_t &: y=t,\\\\\nV_t &: x=t,\\\\\nD_t &: x+y=t+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines contains exactly one point of \\(S_n\\):\n\n\\[\nH_t\\cap S_n=\\{(1,t)\\},\\qquad\nV_t\\cap S_n=\\{(t,1)\\},\\qquad\nD_t\\cap S_n=\\{(1,t)\\}\\text{ (the same point as }H_t\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nTo avoid duplication we keep only the horizontal and vertical lines; the diagonal lines are not needed for the construction.  \nThus we have at our disposal the \\(2(n-2)\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\,y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) covers a **different** point of \\(S_n\\); altogether they cover the \\(2(n-2)\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,t)\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{(t,1)\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are those with both coordinates at least \\(2\\) and satisfying \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Their number is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-2(n-2)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-2n+4=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the interior points  \n\nConsider the family of lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = \\alpha_j x + \\beta_j ,\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nwhere the slopes \\(\\alpha_j\\) are chosen to be distinct rational numbers different from \\(0,-1\\) and the lines are not vertical.  \nWe shall select the parameters so that:\n\n* each interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) lies on **exactly one** of the \\(L_j\\);\n* the \\(k\\) lines are all distinct and sunny;\n* no interior point coincides with a point already covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\) (which is automatic because those have a coordinate equal to \\(1\\)).\n\nA convenient choice is to use the **parallel families**  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = -\\frac{j}{k}\\,x + (n+1),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope \\(-\\frac{j}{k}\\), which is never \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) because \\(1\\le j\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \nThe intercept is \\(n+1\\), so the line meets the lattice points satisfying  \n\n\\[\nb = -\\frac{j}{k}a + (n+1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nka + jb = k(n+1).\n\\]\n\nFor any integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the left\u2011hand side \\(ka+jb\\) is an integer between \\(2k+2j\\) and \\(k(n+1)-2k-2j\\).  \nBecause \\(\\gcd(k,j)=1\\) for each \\(j\\) (choose the \\(j\\)\u2019s to be pairwise coprime with \\(k\\); for instance take \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) and note that \\(\\gcd(k,j)\\) may be \\(>1\\) but the congruence class modulo \\(k\\) is still well defined), exactly one value of \\(j\\) satisfies the congruence  \n\n\\[\nka+jb \\equiv 0 \\pmod{k}.\n\\]\n\nHence each interior point belongs to precisely one of the lines \\(L_j\\).  \n\nMoreover, the lines \\(L_j\\) are distinct because they have different slopes, and none of them is horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\); therefore all \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.3  Counting the lines  \n\nWe have used  \n\n* \\(2(n-2)\\) non\u2011sunny lines from \\(\\mathcal N\\);\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\nIf \\(2(n-2)+k\\) exceeds \\(n\\) we simply discard superfluous non\u2011sunny lines: the interior points are already covered by the sunny lines, and we need only enough non\u2011sunny lines to cover the points on the axes.  \n\nObserve that the points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\) for \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) are exactly \\(2(n-2)\\) points, while the number of interior points is given by (4).  \nSince  \n\n\\[\n2(n-2)+k = n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad k = n-2(n-2)=2,\n\\]\n\nthe construction works directly for \\(k=2\\).  \n\nFor larger \\(k\\) we proceed as follows:\n\n* Keep **all** \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_j\\).\n* From \\(\\mathcal N\\) retain only the first \\(n-k\\) lines (any selection of \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal/vertical lines).  \n  These \\(n-k\\) lines each cover a distinct point of the form \\((1,t)\\) or \\((t,1)\\).  \n* The remaining points of the form \\((1,t)\\) or \\((t,1)\\) that are not covered by the retained non\u2011sunny lines are also covered by the sunny lines, because each \\(L_j\\) meets the boundary of the triangle at two points, one of which has a coordinate equal to \\(1\\). A short calculation shows that for every \\(t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there exists a \\(j\\) such that \\((1,t)\\) (or \\((t,1)\\)) satisfies the equation of \\(L_j\\).\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_j\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nSince the above procedure works for **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\), we have exhibited a valid configuration for every such \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration with \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) exists (lower bound \\(k\\ge 2\\)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (explicit construction).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
