ar number  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  How many points can a line contain?\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor such a line the admissible points are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) lie on it.  \nThe same bound holds for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)) and for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)), because in each case the set of admissible points is a segment of length at most \\(n\\).\n\nThus **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).**  \n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nAssume a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \nIf \\(L\\) contained three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\), then the slopes of the segments joining them would be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\\qquad \n\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2},\\qquad \n\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(a_i,b_i\\) are positive integers and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), none of these slopes can be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) unless the line itself is parallel to one of the forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  \nConsequently **a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Pigeonhole inequality\n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nUsing the bounds above, the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2k}_{\\text{sunny}}+\\underbrace{n\\,t}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n=2k+n(n-k). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, so by (3) and (4)\n\n\\[\n2k+n(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInequality (5) is the pigeonhole condition that any admissible configuration must satisfy.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Solving the inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nRewrite (5):\n\n\\[\n2k+n^{2}-nk\\ge\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nn^{2}+4k-2nk\\ge n\n\\Longrightarrow\n2k(2-n)\\ge -n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), the factor \\(2-n\\) is negative; dividing by it reverses the inequality:\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)} . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, a **necessary condition** for the existence of a configuration is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct pairs of points of \\(S\\) that are not collinear with any third point of \\(S\\).  \n   For each pair draw the unique line through them; by the discussion in §1 each such line is sunny and contains exactly those two points.  \n   This uses \\(2k\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   Let \\(U\\subseteq S\\) be the set of points not yet covered; \\(|U|=|S|-2k\\).  \n   Because of (5) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |U|\\le n(n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   We now cover \\(U\\) with the \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Proceed greedily: at each step pick a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (or, if no vertical line contains any uncovered point, a horizontal line \\(y=c\\), or finally a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\)) that contains the largest possible number of points of \\(U\\).  \n   Such a line always contains at most \\(n\\) points, and after adding it we delete those points from \\(U\\).  \n   Since we have at most \\(t\\) lines to use and the initial size of \\(U\\) does not exceed \\(n t\\), the greedy process succeeds: after at most \\(t\\) steps all points of \\(U\\) are removed.\n\n   The lines chosen in this way are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence they are non‑sunny, and they are distinct because each uses a different constant \\(c\\).\n\nThus we have produced \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines that together cover every point of \\(S\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completeness of the answer\n\n- **Necessity:** inequality (7) follows from the pigeonhole principle applied to the maximal capacities of sunny and non‑sunny lines.\n- **Sufficiency:** the construction in §4 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\big|\\;0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\right\\}} .\n\\]"
