ue non‑sunny line has slope \\(-1\\)*, we may take it to be the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which covers all points of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   The next diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points.  None of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), so by Lemma 1 each of them can meet \\(D_{n}\\) in at most one point; consequently every point of \\(D_{n}\\) must lie on a different one of those lines.  Hence each of those \\(n-1\\) lines contains exactly one point of \\(D_{n}\\).  Any line that contains a point of \\(D_{n}\\) and also a point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. must have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption that there is only one non‑sunny line.  \n   Therefore at least **two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines (two fixed ones).*\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_2:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   \\(L_1\\) is vertical, \\(L_2\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  Together they cover all points of the two longest diagonals \\(D_{n+1}\\) and \\(D_{n}\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick a point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P\\) that is **not** on \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\) and that has not been used before, and draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-b_i=\\alpha_i (x-a_i).\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct and none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are sunny.  \n   Lemma 1 guarantees that each \\(S_i\\) meets any diagonal in at most one point, so the greedy choice can always be continued until \\(k\\) sunny lines have been drawn.  After this step every diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\le n-1\\) still has at most one uncovered point.\n\n   *Horizontal lines (the remaining non‑sunny lines).*  \n   Let \\(B\\) be the set of \\(y\\)-coordinates of the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that are still uncovered after the previous step.  \n   Because we have already used \\(2+k\\) lines, the number of uncovered rows is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   n-(2+k)=n-k-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(b\\in B\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\;y=b .\n   \\]\n\n   Horizontal lines are non‑sunny; together with \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) they give a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{fixed})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;=\\;n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   - If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on \\(L_2\\).  \n   - If \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(L_1\\).  \n   - Otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\).  If the point was selected as the defining point of some sunny line \\(S_i\\) it lies on that line.  If it was not selected, then its row \\(y=b\\) belongs to the set \\(B\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 together with the argument of step 3 shows that at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  The explicit construction of step 4 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realized.  Therefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
