east two lines that are *not* sunny. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Two fixed non‑sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non‑sunny (vertical and horizontal).  Together they cover the whole column \\(C\\) and the whole row \\(R\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick a point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P\\) that is not on \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\) and that has not been used before, and draw  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-b_i=\\alpha_i (x-a_i).\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct and, by construction, none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n   Lemma 1 guarantees that each \\(S_i\\) meets any diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point, so the choice can be continued until \\(k\\) sunny lines have been drawn.\n\n   *Remaining non‑sunny lines (horizontal).*  \n   After the previous step some points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) may still be uncovered.  All uncovered points have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, because any two uncovered points with the same \\(y\\) would lie on the same horizontal line, which we could add now.  Let  \n\n   \\[\n   B=\\{\\,b\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\text{ is still uncovered}\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(b\\in B\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\;y=b .\n   \\]\n\n   Horizontal lines are non‑sunny.  The number of such lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   |B| = n-(2+k)=n-k-2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   because we have already used \\(2+k\\) lines and we need a total of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   - If \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(L_1\\).  \n   - If \\(b=1\\) 