            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal P=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line (i.e. a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains at most one point of the column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nand at most one point of the row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contains two distinct points of \\(C\\) then it is vertical, hence its slope is \\(\\infty\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nIf a line contains two distinct points of \\(R\\) then it is horizontal, hence its slope is \\(0\\), also impossible for a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAll points of \\(C\\) must be covered. By Lemma 1 a sunny line can cover at most one of them, so at least one vertical line is required. Similarly, at least one horizontal line is required to cover all points of \\(R\\). Both a vertical line (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) and a horizontal line (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) are not sunny. Consequently any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least two non‑sunny lines, whence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### Step 1 – two mandatory non‑sunny lines\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1 .\n\\]\nThese two lines cover the whole column \\(C\\) and the whole row \\(R\\).\n\n#### Step 2 – selection of \\(k\\) points with distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:=\\mathcal P\\setminus (C\\cup R)\n      =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n\\) there exists at least one \\(a\\ge2\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q\\) (take \\(a=2\\) while \\(2+b\\le n+1\\), otherwise increase \\(a\\) appropriately). Hence each row \\(y=b\\) (\\(b=2,\\dots ,n\\)) contains at least one point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\).\n\nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\), we can choose \\(k\\) distinct rows among \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\); denote them \\(b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\). From each chosen row pick any point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal Q\\). Thus the points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) satisfy\n\n* \\(a_i\\ge2,\\;b_i\\ge2\\);\n* \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\);\n* the \\(b_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) select a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) with \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\alpha_i\\neq\\infty\\); for concreteness set \\(\\alpha_i=i\\) (any distinct non‑zero numbers different from \\(-1\\) work). Define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-b_i=\\alpha_i\\,(x-a_i).\n\\]\n\nBecause the slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct. Their slopes are neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_i\\).\n\n#### Step 3 – completing the family with horizontal lines\nAfter Steps 1–2 the rows \\(y=b_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)) already contain a covering line (the sunny line \\(S_i\\)). The remaining rows among \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are those whose index \\(b\\) is **not** among \\(\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}\\). For each such \\(b\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\; y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny. The number of added horizontals is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}|=(n-1)-k=n-k-2 .\n\\]\n\n#### Counting the lines\nWe have used  \n\n* 2 lines in Step 1,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines in Step 2,\n* \\(n-k-2\\) horizontal lines in Step 3.\n\nThus the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2+k+(n-k-2)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them (\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)) are sunny.\n\n#### Verification that every required point is covered\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in C\\) and lies on \\(L_v\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in R\\) and lies on \\(L_h\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\((a,b)=(a_i,b_i)\\) for some \\(i\\), then it lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\) is not one of the selected \\(b_i\\); by construction the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) is present, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\nLemma 1 forces at least two non‑sunny lines, giving the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
