            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of the column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nand at most one point of the row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(C\\) it would be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(R\\) it would be horizontal, also contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition\n\nAll points of \\(C\\) must be covered. By Lemma 1 a sunny line can cover at most one of them, so at least one vertical line is required. Similarly, at least one horizontal line is required to cover all points of \\(R\\). Both a vertical line (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) and a horizontal line (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) are not sunny. Consequently any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least two non‑sunny lines, whence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n#### Step 1 – two mandatory non‑sunny lines\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1 .\n\\]\nThese two lines cover the whole column \\(C\\) and the whole row \\(R\\).\n\n#### Step 2 – interior points\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) there exists at least one integer \\(a\\ge2\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q\\): if \\(2+b\\le n+1\\) we may take \\(a=2\\); otherwise increase \\(a\\) until the inequality holds. Hence every row \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) contains a point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\). The row \\(y=n\\) contains no point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\), but the point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(L_v\\) and is already covered.\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\), we can select \\(k\\) distinct rows among \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); denote them \\(b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\). From each selected row pick any point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal Q\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\na_i\\ge2,\\;b_i\\ge2,\\;a_i+b_i\\le n+1,\\qquad b_i\\neq b_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\n#### Step 3 – sunny lines through the selected points\nChoose distinct real numbers \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) with \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_i=i\\)). Define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-b_i=\\alpha_i\\,(x-a_i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(\\alpha_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny. The lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct, and \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_i\\).\n\n#### Step 4 – completing the family with horizontal lines\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB:=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(b\\in B\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\; y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny. The cardinality of \\(B\\) is \\((n-2)-k\\).\n\n#### Counting the lines\nTotal number of lines used:\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2}_{L_v,L_h}\n\\;+\\;\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }S_i}\n\\;+\\;\\underbrace{(n-2)-k}_{\\text{extra horizontals}}\n\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n#### Verification of coverage\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in C\\) and lies on \\(L_v\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in R\\) and lies on \\(L_h\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\in\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}\\), then \\((a,b)=(a_i,b_i)\\) for some \\(i\\) (by construction) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\in B\\), then the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) is present, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n* If \\(b=n\\) (the only remaining possible row with \\(b\\ge2\\)), then necessarily \\(a=1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); such a point is already on \\(L_v\\).\n\nThus every required point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\nLemma 1 yields the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
