id b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nD_d=\\{(a,d)\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(C_c\\) it would be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(D_d\\) it would be horizontal, again contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points on the first column and first row\n\nThe points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) all belong to the column \\(C_1\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so to cover the whole column we need a **vertical** line. The only vertical line that can contain all these points is  \n\n\\[\nL_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) lie on the row \\(D_1\\); they require a **horizontal** line  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\) are not sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Interior rows\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) consider the set  \n\n\\[\nR_b:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a=2\\) satisfies \\(2+b\\le n+1\\) for all \\(b\\le n-1\\), each \\(R_b\\) is non\u2011empty; in particular it contains the point \\((2,b)\\).\n\nAssume that a line \\(L\\) (sunny or not) covers all points of \\(R_b\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) were sunny, then by Lemma\u202f1 it could contain at most one point of the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); but \\(R_b\\subseteq\\{y=b\\}\\) contains at least two distinct points (e.g. \\((2,b)\\) and \\((3,b)\\) whenever \\(3+b\\le n+1\\)). Hence a sunny line cannot cover \\(R_b\\).\n\nTherefore each row \\(R_b\\) must be covered by a **horizontal** line \\(y=b\\). Denote these lines by  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b\\qquad (b=2,3,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(H_b\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting the lines\n\nThe family of lines\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_h\\}\\cup\\{H_b\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{horizontal }y=1)\\;+\\;(n-2)\\;(\\text{horizontal }y=b)\n= n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to one of the following:\n\n* If its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(1\\), it lies on \\(L_v\\).\n* If its \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(1\\), it lies on \\(L_h\\).\n* If its \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\), it lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\nThus the covering condition is satisfied.\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are either vertical or horizontal, i.e. **none of them is sunny**. Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in any admissible configuration must be\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessity of \\(k=0\\)\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that an admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contained a sunny line \\(S\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1, \\(S\\) can cover at most one point of the column \\(x=1\\) and at most one point of the row \\(y=1\\). Since the whole column \\(x=1\\) and the whole row \\(y=1\\) must be covered, we would need at least one vertical line and one horizontal line in addition to \\(S\\). Moreover, as shown in Section\u202f2, each row \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) requires a distinct horizontal line. Hence the total number of required lines would be at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{vertical})+1\\;(\\text{horizontal }y=1)+(n-2)\\;(\\text{rows }y=b)=n,\n\\]\n\nleaving **no room** for any sunny line. This contradiction shows that a sunny line cannot appear in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nThe only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\,}.\n\\]"

