 sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Interior rows\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_b:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a=2\\) satisfies \\(2+b\\le n+1\\) for all \\(b\\le n-1\\), each \\(R_b\\) is non‑empty; in fact it contains at least the two points \\((2,b)\\) and \\((3,b)\\) whenever \\(3+b\\le n+1\\). Hence \\(|R_b|\\ge 2\\).\n\nAssume a line \\(L\\) (sunny or not) covers all points of \\(R_b\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) were sunny, Lemma 1 would imply that \\(L\\) meets the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, contradicting \\(|R_b|\\ge 2\\). Consequently a sunny line cannot cover the whole set \\(R_b\\).\n\nTherefore each row \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) must be covered by a line that is **horizontal**, i.e. by the line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny. There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such rows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A covering with \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines\n\nCollect the lines obtained above:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_h\\}\\cup\\{H_b\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{vertical})+1\\;(\\text{horizontal }y=1)+(n-2)\\;(\\text{horizontal }y=b)=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to one of the following:\n\n* points with \\(x=1\\) lie on \\(L_v\\);\n* points with \\(y=1\\) lie on \\(L_h\\);\n* points with \\(y=b\\) for \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) lie on \\(H_b\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the covering requirement, and it contains **no sunny line**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  No configuration can contain a sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line \\(S\\).\n\n*Column \\(x=1\\).*  \nBy Lemma 1, \\(S\\) can cover at most one point of the column \\(C_1\\). Since all \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must be covered, a vertical line \\(x=1\\) is still required in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Row \\(y=1\\).*  \nSimilarly, \\(S\\) can cover at most one point of the row \\(R_1\\); therefore a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is also required.\n\n*Rows \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\).*  \nEach such row contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). By Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet the row in at most one point, so to cover the whole row we need **at least one additional line** that is horizontal (the only line capable of covering more than one point of the row). Consequently for each \\(b=2,\\dots ,n-1\\) the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) must contain a distinct horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F\\) must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{vertical})+1\\;(\\text{horizontal }y=1)+(n-2)\\;(\\text{horizontal }y=b)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines **in addition to** the sunny line \\(S\\). This would give at least \\(n+1\\) lines, contradicting the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used. Hence no sunny line can appear in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nThe only possible number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
