-------------------------------\n### 2.  The remaining rows\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_b:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\)* then \\(a=2\\) and \\(a=3\\) both satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n(2,b),\\;(3,b)\\in R_b,\\qquad\\text{so }|R_b|\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\)* then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le 2\\); together with \\(a\\ge 2\\) we obtain the single point  \n\n\\[\nR_{n-1}=\\{(2,n-1)\\},\\qquad |R_{n-1}|=1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering the rows \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\)\n\nTake a row \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\). Because \\(|R_b|\\ge 2\\), Lemma 1 implies that a sunny line can meet this row in at most one point, and therefore cannot cover all points of \\(R_b\\). Consequently a **horizontal** line \\(y=b\\) (which is not sunny) is required for each such \\(b\\).\n\nThus we must have the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H:=\\{y=b\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-2\\},\n\\]\n\nconsisting of \\(n-3\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The last row \\(y=n-1\\)\n\nThe row \\(y=n-1\\) contains exactly the point \\((2,n-1)\\). There are two possibilities:\n\n* **(i) Use the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\).**  \n  This line is non‑sunny and together with the lines already listed we obtain a family of  \n\n  \\[\n  1\\;(x=1)+1\\;(y=1)+(n-3)\\;(y=2,\\dots ,n-2)+1\\;(y=n-1)=n\n  \\]\n\n  distinct lines, none of which is sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n* **(ii) Use a sunny line through \\((2,n-1)\\).**  \n  Choose any line through \\((2,n-1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); call it \\(S\\).  \n  The family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}\n  \\]\n\n  again contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, now with precisely one sunny line \\(S\\). All points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered because the only uncovered point of the row \\(y=n-1\\) is \\((2,n-1)\\), which lies on \\(S\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition contains at least two sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line meets any given row in at most one point.  \nRows \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\) contain at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\); therefore none of them can be completely covered by a sunny line, and each such row must be covered by a horizontal (non‑sunny) line. Hence \\(\\mathcal F\\) must contain the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\).\n\nRows \\(y=1\\) and the column \\(x=1\\) force the inclusion of the non‑sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) (Lemma 1).  \n\nThus at this stage \\(\\mathcal F\\) already contains  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(x=1)+1\\;(y=1)+(n-3)\\;(y=2,\\dots ,n-2)=n-1\n\\]\n\ndistinct non‑sunny lines. Only one line remains to reach the total of \\(n\\) lines, but we have assumed the presence of **two** sunny lines, which is impossible. Consequently a configuration cannot have \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
