            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf \\(L\\) is a sunny line then  \n\n* \\(L\\) meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point;\n* \\(L\\) meets each horizontal line \\(y=d\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) met a vertical line \\(x=c\\) in two distinct points, those two points would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing \\(L\\) to be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny. The same argument works for a horizontal line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Forced non‑sunny lines from the first row and column\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset\\mathcal P\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points. By Lemma 1 a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so to cover all points of \\(C_1\\) we must include the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nforces the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\) are not sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The remaining rows\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_b:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\)* then both \\((2,b)\\) and \\((3,b)\\) satisfy the inequality, hence  \n\n\\[\n|R_b|\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\)* then the inequality forces \\(a\\le 2\\); together with \\(a\\ge 2\\) we obtain the single point  \n\n\\[\nR_{n-1}=\\{(2,n-1)\\},\\qquad |R_{n-1}|=1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of the horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) for \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\)\n\nAssume, for a fixed \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\), that the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is **not** used in our family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of lines.  \nAll points of \\(R_b\\) must then be covered by other lines.\n\nBecause \\(|R_b|\\ge 2\\) and Lemma 1 tells us that any sunny line can intersect the row \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, **no single line (sunny or non‑sunny) can contain two distinct points of \\(R_b\\)**. Consequently each point of \\(R_b\\) must lie on a *different* line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nHence at least \\(|R_b|\\ge 2\\) distinct lines are required solely for the row \\(y=b\\).\n\nNow count the minimal number of lines that any admissible family must contain.\n\n* The forced vertical line \\(x=1\\) (1 line).\n* The forced horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (1 line).\n* For every \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\) we need at least two distinct lines, because we have assumed the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is absent. Thus we need at least \\(2\\) lines for each of the \\(n-3\\) rows \\(b=2,\\dots ,n-2\\), i.e. at least \\(2(n-3)\\) lines.\n* The last row \\(R_{n-1}\\) contains a single point; it can be covered by one additional line.\n\nSumming these lower bounds gives  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(x=1)+1\\;(y=1)+2(n-3)+(1)=2n-4 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), we have \\(2n-4>n\\). Therefore any family that omits a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) for some \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) would require **more than \\(n\\) distinct lines**, contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) lines be used.\n\nConsequently **the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) must be present for every \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\)**. All these lines are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The last row \\(y=n-1\\)\n\nThe row \\(R_{n-1}\\) consists of the single point \\((2,n-1)\\). There are two ways to cover it while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n* **(i) Use the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\).**  \n  This line is non‑sunny. Adding it to the already forced lines gives  \n\n  \\[\n  1\\;(x=1)+1\\;(y=1)+(n-3)\\;(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2)+1\\;(y=n-1)=n\n  \\]\n\n  distinct lines, none of which is sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n* **(ii) Use a sunny line through \\((2,n-1)\\).**  \n  Choose any line through \\((2,n-1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); call it \\(S\\).  \n  The family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}\n  \\]\n\n  again contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, now with precisely one sunny line \\(S\\). All points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered, because the only uncovered point of the last row is \\((2,n-1)\\), which lies on \\(S\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfying the covering condition contained at least two sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2\\).  \nFrom Lemma 1 each sunny line meets any given row in at most one point. As shown in Section 3, every row \\(y=b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\) must already be covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); otherwise we would need more than \\(n\\) lines. Hence the only rows that could possibly contain points of a sunny line are the first row \\(y=1\\) (already covered by \\(y=1\\)) and the last row \\(y=n-1\\) (which contains a single point). The row \\(y=1\\) is already fully covered by the non‑sunny line \\(y=1\\), so a sunny line cannot contribute any new point there without creating a duplicate line. Consequently each sunny line can contribute at most the single point \\((2,n-1)\\). Since the point \\((2,n-1)\\) can lie on only one line, at most **one** sunny line can be used. Therefore \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
