            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf \\(L\\) is a sunny line then  \n\n* \\(L\\) meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point;\n* \\(L\\) meets each horizontal line \\(y=d\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(L\\) met a vertical line \\(x=c\\) in two distinct points, those two points would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing \\(L\\) to be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny. The same argument works for a horizontal line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line (i.e. a vertical line \\(x=\\alpha\\) or a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=\\beta\\)) cannot contain two distinct points of a fixed row \\(y=b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Vertical line.*  All its points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(\\alpha\\). Two points of the same row have different \\(x\\)-coordinates (they are \\((a_1,b)\\) and \\((a_2,b)\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\)), so they cannot both lie on the same vertical line.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line.*  Its equation is \\(x+y=\\beta\\). For a point \\((a,b)\\) on the row \\(y=b\\) we have \\(a+b=\\beta\\). If two distinct points \\((a_1,b)\\) and \\((a_2,b)\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) were on the same line, we would obtain \\(a_1+b=\\beta=a_2+b\\), implying \\(a_1=a_2\\), a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nFix a row index \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\). If the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is **not** included among the chosen lines, then each point of  \n\n\\[\nR_b:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by a distinct line, and no such line can be used to cover any other point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that is not already on that line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can intersect the row \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, and by Lemma 2 a non‑sunny line can also intersect the row in at most one point. Hence **every** line can contain at most one point of \\(R_b\\). Consequently the \\(|R_b|\\) points of the row require at least \\(|R_b|\\) distinct lines.\n\nAssume a line \\(L\\) that contains a point \\((a,b)\\in R_b\\) also contains another point \\(P\\in\\mathcal P\\setminus R_b\\).  \nIf \\(P\\) lies on a forced line (\\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\)), then \\(L\\) would coincide with that forced line, contradicting the assumption that the forced lines are already counted separately.  \nIf \\(P\\) lies on a different row \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq b\\), then \\(L\\) would meet two distinct rows, which by Lemma 1 forces \\(L\\) to be sunny. But a sunny line can meet each row in at most one point, so it could not also contain \\((a,b)\\) and another point of a different row simultaneously. Hence such a \\(P\\) cannot exist. Therefore each line used for a point of \\(R_b\\) is dedicated solely to that point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Forced non‑sunny lines from the first row and column\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1