ere are two admissible ways to cover it while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n* **(i) Non‑sunny option.**  \n  Add the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\). The family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\n  \\]\n\n  contains exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  1+(n-1)=n\n  \\]\n\n  distinct lines, none of which is sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n* **(ii) Sunny option.**  \n  Choose any line \\(S\\) through \\((2,n-1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny.  \n  The family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}\n  \\]\n\n  again has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, now with precisely one sunny line \\(S\\). Thus a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfying the covering condition contained at least two sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line meets any fixed row in at most one point.  \n\nRows \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\) already have their horizontal lines \\(y=b\\); a sunny line cannot contribute any additional point to such a row without creating a second line for the same row, which would increase the total beyond \\(n\\) (the counting argument of Section 3).  \n\nThe first row \\(y=1\\) is completely covered by the forced line \\(y=1\\). If a sunny line also passed through a point of this row, that point would then lie on two distinct lines, which is allowed, but the sunny line would still need to cover some other point of \\(\\mathcal P\\). The only remaining uncovered point after all forced horizontal lines is \\((2,n-1)\\). Hence any sunny line other than the one through \\((2,n-1)\\) would have to contain a point of a row \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\), contradicting the previous paragraph. Consequently **at most one** sunny line can be used, namely the one covering \\((2,n-1)\\)