### 3.  Lemma 1  \n\nA sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) and each horizontal line \\(y=d\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  If a sunny line met a vertical line \\(x=c\\) in two distinct points, those points would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny. The same argument works for a horizontal line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Lemma 2  \n\nA non‑sunny line (vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) cannot contain two distinct points of a fixed row \\(y=b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Vertical line.*  All its points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, whereas two distinct points of the row have different \\(x\\)-coordinates.  \n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line.*  Its equation is \\(x+y=\\beta\\). For a point \\((a,b)\\) on the row we have \\(a+b=\\beta\\). Two distinct points \\((a_1,b)\\) and \\((a_2,b)\\) would give \\(a_1+b=\\beta=a_2+b\\), implying \\(a_1=a_2\\), a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lemma 3 (row‑covering bound)\n\nFix \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\). Suppose the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is **not** among the chosen lines. Then:\n\n1. Each point of \\(R_b\\) must be covered by a distinct line.\n2. No line that covers a point of \\(R_b\\) can be used to cover any other point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. By Lemmas 1 and 2 any line can contain at most one point of \\(R_b\\); therefore at least \\(|R_b|\\) distinct lines are required for the \\(|R_b|\\) points.\n\n2. Let \\(L\\) be a line that contains a point \\((a,b)\\in R_b\\) and suppose that \\(L\\) also contains another point \\(Q\\in\\mathcal P\\setminus R_b\\).\n\n   *Case 2.1 – \\(Q\\) lies on a forced line.*  \n   The forced lines are \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\). If \\(Q\\) lies on one of them, then \\(L\\) meets that forced line in a point distinct from the whole forced line; consequently \\(L\\) is neither v