otal number of lines equal to \\(n\\):\n\n* **(i) Non‑sunny choice.** Add the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\).  \n  The set  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_0=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\n  \\]\n\n  consists of \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n* **(ii) Sunny choice.** Choose any sunny line \\(S\\) through \\((2,n-1)\\) (its slope may be any real number except \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n  The set  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_1=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}\n  \\]\n\n  also contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, now with precisely one sunny line.  Thus a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  No configuration can contain two sunny lines\n\nAfter step 6 the rows \\(y=2,\\dots ,n-2\\) already have their dedicated horizontal lines, and the first row is covered by the forced line \\(y=1\\).\n\nA sunny line can intersect a given row in at most one point (Lemma 1).  Consequently a sunny line can contribute points only from rows that do **not** already have a dedicated horizontal line, i.e. from the first row or the last row.\n\nThe first row is completely covered by the forced line \\(y=1\\); if a sunny line also passed through a point of this row, that point would lie on two distinct lines, and the sunny line would still need to cover at least one additional point (otherwise it would be unnecessary).  The only point not already covered by a dedicated horizontal line is \\((2,n-1)\\).  Hence at most one sunny line can be used, namely the one described in step 7.\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion\n\nThe analysis shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]"
