iagonal’s sum is one larger than the row index, i.e. the diagonal \\(D_{c+1}\\) is completely covered by the horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  Therefore, for each diagonal we have two possibilities:\n\n   * either we take the diagonal itself as a line (non‑sunny, slope \\(-1\\));\n   * or we take the horizontal line of the lower row, which then covers that diagonal completely.\n\n   There are exactly \\(n-2\\) diagonals \\(D_4,\\dots ,D_n\\).  Hence, using the \\(n-2\\) remaining lines, we can cover all these diagonals **without any sunny line** (choose all the horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\)).  This yields a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n   If we wish to introduce a sunny line, we must replace one of the horizontal lines by a sunny line.  The only diagonal that is *not* already covered by a horizontal line is \\(D_{n+1}\\), which consists of the single point \\((2,n-1)\\) (all other points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) have \\(x=1\\) and are already on the forced vertical line).  Thus we may keep the horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) (covering diagonals \\(D_4,\\dots ,D_{n-1}\\)), keep the forced lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\), and cover the remaining point \\((2,n-1)\\) with a sunny line of any admissible slope.  This uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains **exactly one** sunny line.  \n\n   Attempting to have two or more sunny lines is impossible: each sunny line can contribute at most one point from each diagonal (Lemma 2), and the only diagonal that still needs a point after the forced lines and the horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) is \\(D_{n+1}\\).  Hence a second sunny line would have no uncovered point to serve, forcing the total number of lines to exceed \\(n\\).  \n\n   Therefore \\(k\\ge 2\\) cannot occur.\n\n8. **Existence constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n   This family has \\(n\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on one of them.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Choose any real number \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and let \\(S\\) be the line through \\((2,n-1)\\) with slope \\(m\\).  Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_1=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}.\n   \\]\n   Again we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; the only sunny line is \\(S\\).  All points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered: the forced lines cover the first row and column, the horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) cover the diagonals \\(D_4,\\dots ,D_{n-1}\\), and the sunny line \\(S\\) covers the remaining point \\((2,n-1)\\).\n\nSince both constructions satisfy the requirements, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Forced non‑sunny lines\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset\\mathcal P\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nIf a line is not vertical, its equation can be written \\(y=mx+q\\) with finite slope \\(m\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives a unique value of \\(y\\).  Hence a non‑vertical line can contain at most one point of \\(C_1\\).  Since we must cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) with at most \\(n\\) lines, the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_v:\\;x=1\n\\]\n\nmust be present.  It is non‑sunny.\n\nSimilarly, the set  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nforces the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is also non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Diagonals\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to exactly one diagonal.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) is precisely a diagonal \\(D_s\\); conversely, any line that is not vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemmas\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA non‑vertical line contains at most one point with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate; a non‑horizontal line contains at most one point with a given \\(y\\)-coordinate.\n\n*Proof.*  If two distinct points \\((x_0,y_1),(x_0,y_2)\\) with the same \\(x_0\\) lay on a line, the line’s equation must be \\(x=x_0\\) (vertical).  The horizontal case is analogous. ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a sunny line \\(y=mx+q\\) (\\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains two points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\) of the same diagonal, so \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=s\\).  Subtracting the two equations \\(b_i=ma_i+q\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nm(a_1-a_2)=b_1-b_2=-(a_1-a_2),\n\\]\n\nhence \\((m+1)(a_1-a_2)=0\\).  Because the points are distinct, \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\), forcing \\(m=-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting the needed lines\n\nWe already have the two forced lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nAfter removing the points on these two lines, the remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); they lie on the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_4,D_5,\\dots ,D_n .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n\\) the diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains at least two points, e.g. \\((2,s-2)\\) and \\((3,s-3)\\).\n\nWe have to use in total exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Hence, besides the forced two, we have \\(n-2\\) lines left to cover all points of the diagonals \\(D_4,\\dots ,D_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. How a line can cover a diagonal\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge2\\)) contains all points of the diagonal \\(D_{c+1}\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least \\(2\\); the remaining point of \\(D_{c+1}\\) (namely \\((1,c)\\)) is already on the forced vertical line \\(x=1\\).  Thus a single horizontal line completely takes care of the diagonal \\(D_{c+1}\\).\n\n*Diagonal line.*  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with a diagonal \\(D_s\\) and therefore covers **all** points of that diagonal.\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nBy Lemma 2 a sunny line can contribute at most one point from each diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines.  The remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines (besides the forced ones) are either horizontal or diagonal lines.\n\nBecause each diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n\\) contains at least two points, covering it without a sunny line requires **one** non‑sunny line (either the horizontal line \\(y=s-1\\) or the diagonal line itself).  Consequently, to cover all \\(n-3\\) diagonals \\(D_4,\\dots ,D_n\\) we need at least \\(n-3\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nWe have exactly \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines available, so  \n\n\\[\nn-2-k\\ge n-3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus **at most one** sunny line can appear in any admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt consists of \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny.  \nThe forced vertical line covers the whole first column, the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covers the whole first row, and for each \\(b\\ge2\\) the line \\(y=b\\) covers the diagonal \\(D_{b+1}\\) (all its points have \\(x\\ge2\\)).  Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nLet \\(S\\) be any sunny line passing through the point \\((2,n-1)\\) (its slope may be any real number except \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nThe forced lines cover the first row and column.  \nFor each \\(b=2,\\dots ,n-2\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) covers diagonal \\(D_{b+1}\\).  \nThe only point not yet covered is \\((2,n-1)\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(S\\).  Thus all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered and precisely one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nIf we attempted to use two sunny lines, Lemma 2 would allow each of them to contribute at most one point from each diagonal.  After the forced lines and the horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) are taken, the only diagonal that still lacks a covering point is \\(D_{n+1}\\), which consists solely of \\((2,n-1)\\) (the other point \\((1,n)\\) is already on \\(x=1\\)).  Hence a second sunny line would have no uncovered point to serve, forcing the total number of lines to exceed \\(n\\).  Therefore \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9. Conclusion\n\nThe analysis shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the statement are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]"
