## Case 1: \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) given by the hypothesis.  \nThe new points that have to be covered are those with \\(a+b=n+2\\); they form the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) lie on the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y=n+2-a\\quad\\text{(equivalently } y = -x + (n+2)\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_h\\) is **not sunny** because it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nAdd \\(\\ell_h\\) to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\). The resulting family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_h\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}=T_n\\cup S_{n+1}\\), and still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the added line is non‑sunny). Hence (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**) holds for this range of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\)\n\nWe need a family with **all** lines sunny.  \nBegin again with \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (which exists by the hypothesis).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) are sunny, and they already cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nReplace one of the non‑sunny directions (if any) by a sunny line that still passes through all points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by that line. This is always possible because any two distinct points determine a unique line, and we may choose a line through those two points whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). After the replacement we have a family \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,n}\\) of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\nNow add the horizontal line \\(\\ell_h\\) defined above; it is non‑sunny, so the total number of sunny lines would drop to \\(n\\). To keep the count at \\(n+1\\) we **replace** \\(\\ell_h\\) by a sunny line that also contains all points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y = mx + c,\n\\]\n\nwhere we may choose any slope \