ints that belong to \\(T_{n+1}\\) but not to \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{0}: x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is **not** sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Keeping the number of sunny lines  \n\nTake any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) supplied by the hypothesis.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{0}\\}.\n\\]\n\n* The added line \\(\\ell_{0}\\) contains every point of \\(S_{n+1}\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) covers all of \\(T_{n+1}=T_n\\cup S_{n+1}\\).\n* \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is not sunny, so \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k})=\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_{n,k})=k\\).\n* The lines remain distinct because \\(\\ell_{0}\\) has a direction different from the three forbidden directions, while any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) that is parallel to \\(\\ell_{0}\\) would have been non\u2011sunny; such a line cannot belong to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) because otherwise two parallel lines would be identical (they would have the same constant term \\(n+2\\)), contradicting the distinctness required in the hypothesis.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n\\) we have produced a family of \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nNow let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct a family of \\(n+1\\) lines that contains **\\(k+1\\)** sunny lines.\n\n#### 6.1.  Turning a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one  \n\nIn the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) there are \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nChoose one of them, call it \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), it contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise it would be redundant).  \nSelect two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell'\\) passing through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) with any slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) is sunny and still contains \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).  \nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\); denote the new family by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'_{n,k+1}= (\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\nNow \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k+1}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\) (the two points \\(P,Q\\) are still covered, and every other point was covered before), and has exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 6.2.  Adding the required line for the new points  \n\nFinally add the non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{0}\\) defined in \u00a74:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}= \\mathcal L'_{n,k+1}\\cup\\{\\ell_{0}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*All points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{0}\\); together with the coverage of \\(T_n\\) by \\(\\mathcal L'_{n,k+1}\\) the whole set \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.*  \nThe number of sunny lines is unchanged by the addition of \\(\\ell_{0}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}) = k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n\\) we have produced a family of \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Completion of the induction  \n\nFrom \u00a7\u00a75 and 6 we have shown that, assuming (**H\\(_n\\)**), for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can obtain families for \\(n+1\\) with\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and\n* exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**) holds: for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) there exists a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the base case \\(n=3\\) is true, the statement is true for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) by mathematical induction.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nBecause the configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Final answer  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
