han \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe point \\((a_0,b_0)\\) belongs to both \\(\\ell_{b_0}\\) and \\(\\sigma\\); therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by \\(\\ell_{b_0}\\) remains covered after the replacement. All other points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered because the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are unchanged.  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\) has slope \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), it is sunny, so the number of sunny lines increases by one. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe construct \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n*Base case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake \\(\\mathcal L_0=\\mathcal H\\) from Lemma 1. It consists of \\(n\\) horizontal (hence non‑sunny) lines and covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines has been built, where \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, at least one horizontal line remains; choose one, say \\(\\ell_{b_0}\\).  \nSelect any point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in\\ell_{b_0}\\cap T_n\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\ell_{b_0}\\) meets the triangle).  \nPick a slope \\(\\alpha\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the already existing sunny lines (there are only finitely many such slopes, so this is possible).  \nDefine the sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) by \\(y-b_0=\\alpha(x-a_0)\\).  \nBy Lemma 2, replacing \\(\\ell_{b_0}\\) with \\(\\sigma\\) yields a new family \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) that still covers \\(T_n\\) and now has exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThus the induction proceeds up to \\(k=n-2\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#