r any diagonal \\(D_s\\) a sunny line can intersect \\(D_s\\) in at most one point; otherwise two distinct points of the same diagonal would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of having no sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_n\\) and that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D_s\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(|D_s|-k\\) points of \\(D_s\\) must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nIf a non‑sunny line is not of slope \\(-1\\), it meets \\(D_s\\) in at most one point, as shown above. Consequently, to cover the \\(|D_s|-k\\) uncovered points of a diagonal we would need at least \\(|D_s|-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines for that diagonal alone.\n\nThe only way to avoid this is to use a non‑sunny line **of slope \\(-1\\)** for the diagonal, because such a line covers the whole diagonal in a single line. Hence **each diagonal must be assigned its own slope‑\\(-1\\) line**.\n\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals, we need at least \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nBut the total number of lines is \\(n\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer, the only admissible value is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), hence non‑sunny, and \\(\\ell_s\\) coincides with the diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nThe union \\(\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}\\ell_s\\) is exactly \\(S_n\\).  \n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n------------