((n,1)\\) is still on the vertical line \\(x=n\\).  \n* If \\(s\\) is not of the form \\(j+1\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\), the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) is still present and covers every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=s\\).  \n* For each \\(j\\) the sunny line \\(S_j\\) contains both \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) that lost the diagonal line \\(x+y=j+1\\). Thus they are still covered.  \n* Any other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the unchanged lines, so it is also covered.\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_k}L .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nSince at most two of the three prohibited directions can be used simultaneously, at least one of the families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) would be absent from the set of non\u2011sunny lines. Suppose, without loss of generality, that no vertical line is used. Then every line in the configuration has slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) (horizontal or diagonal). A horizontal line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (those with a fixed \\(y\\)-coordinate), while a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. With at most \\(n\\) lines we can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\) points, but \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence covering all points is impossible. The same argument works for any choice of the missing direction. Therefore \\(k\\) cannot be \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2,\n\\]\n\nthe construction above yields \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNo configuration exists for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
