            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_s\\) are pairwise disjoint, their union is \\(S_n\\), and  \n\n\\[\n|D_s|=s-1\\qquad (2\\le s\\le n+1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Lines of the three forbidden directions  \n\n* A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\); it coincides with a diagonal \\(D_s\\) and therefore contains **all** points of that diagonal.  \n\n* A horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (or a vertical line \\(x=a\\)) meets each diagonal in at most one point, because two distinct points of the same diagonal determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n* A sunny line (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) also meets each diagonal in at most one point, for the same reason.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(S_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nEach sunny line can contribute at most one point to each diagonal, hence the total number of points that can be supplied by the sunny lines is at most \\(k\\cdot n\\).  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can also contribute at most one point to each diagonal, so together they can supply at most \\((n-k)\\cdot n\\) points.  \nThus the whole family can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n\\) points.\n\nSince  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nthe inequality imposes **no restriction** on \\(k\\); the only obvious constraints are \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nFor \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-k\\) distinct lines, each of slope \\(-1\\); together they cover all points of the diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{\\,n-k+1}\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the remaining diagonals\n\nFor each \\(t\\in\\{n-k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(1,\\,t-1)\\in D_t .\n\\]\n\nConsider the finite set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_t=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-(t-1)}{a-1}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in S_n,\\;(a,b)\\neq P_t\\Bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nthe slopes of all lines that would join \\(P_t\\) to another point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal F_t\\) is finite (its cardinality is \\(|S_n|-1\\)).  \n\nChoose a rational number \\(m_t\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm_t\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\cup\\mathcal F_t .\n\\]\n\nThis is possible because the rationals are infinite.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_t:\\; y=m_t x + b_t,\\qquad b_t=(t-1)-m_t .\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(\\sigma_t\\) passes through \\(P_t\\) and through no other point of \\(S_n\\); its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), so \\(\\sigma_t\\) is sunny.\n\nIf \\(\\sigma_{t_1}=\\sigma_{t_2}\\) for \\(t_1\\neq t_2\\), then that common line would contain both \\(P_{t_1}\\) and \\(P_{t_2}\\), contradicting the fact that each \\(\\sigma_t\\) contains no point of \\(S_n\\) other than its own \\(P_t\\). Hence the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_{n-k+2},\\dots ,\\sigma_{n+1}\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 3.3  Verification\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n-k+1\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_t\\mid n-k+2\\le t\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(\\sigma_t\\) are sunny, i.e. \\(k\\) of them.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on one of the \\(\\ell_s\\) (if it belongs to a kept diagonal) or equals some \\(P_t\\) (if it belongs to an omitted diagonal), and therefore belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot be larger than \\(n\\) nor negative. Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur, and no other values are possible. The answer to the problem is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
