a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Condition (3) shows that no value of \\(k\\) outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur, and the construction above shows that every integer in this set does occur.  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nd_s:\\;x+y=s\n\\]\n\nthe diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Counting the points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor a fixed sum \\(s\\) the points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nhence there are \\(s-1\\) of them. Summing over all admissible sums gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any diagonal \\(d_s\\), the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap d_s\\) consists of at most one point.  \nTherefore \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most one point from each diagonal, i.e.\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThe \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines must be among the diagonals \\(\\{d_s\\}\\); each such line contains all points of one diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points.  \nUsing (2) for the sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\n\\le (n-k)\\cdot n + k\\cdot n = n^2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (1) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Choose the sums to be \u201creplaced\u201d.**  \n   Select distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le s_1<s_2<\\dots <s_k\\le k+2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (Such a choice is possible because \\(k+2\\ge s_k\\).)\n\n2. **Define the sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   (a_i,b_i)=(s_i-1,1)\\in S_n .\n   \\]\n\n   Choose a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha_i\\neq\\frac{b_i-1}{a_i-1}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (The right\u2011hand side of (5) is a single forbidden value; hence such an \\(\\alpha_i\\) exists.)  \n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-1=\\alpha_i\\,(x-1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a_i,b_i)\\); by (5) its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n3. **Form the final family of lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\bigl\\{d_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1,\\; s\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The first set contains \\(n-k\\) diagonals, the second set contains \\(k\\) sunny lines; therefore \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n4. **Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.**  \n\n   *If a point \\((a,b)\\) has sum \\(a+b\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\)*, then the diagonal \\(d_{a+b}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\)