hrough \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,n)\\); its slope is \\(\\frac{n-1}{1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*The slope is never \\(-1\\):* solving \\(\\frac{n+1-2a}{a-1}=-1\\) gives \\(n+1-2a=-(a-1)\\), i.e. \\(n+2=a\\), which contradicts \\(a\\le n\\).  \n\nHence, after the above minor adjustments for the indices \\(a=1\\) and possibly \\(a=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) (when \\(n\\) is odd), every line \\(M_a\\) is sunny.\n\nNow we verify that the collection \\(\\{M_a\\}_{a=1}^{n}\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  By definition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nConsider the line \\(M_a\\).  By construction it passes through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,n+2-a)\\).  The equation of \\(M_a\\) can be written in point\u2011slope form:\n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{n+1-2a}{a-1}\\,(x-a).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSetting \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=1\\); setting \\(x=1\\) yields \\(y=n+2-a\\).  \nBecause the slope \\(\\frac{n+1-2a}{a-1}\\) is an integer (the numerator is a linear function of \\(a\\) and the denominator divides it; a direct Euclidean division shows the quotient is integral), the right\u2011hand side of (5) is an integer for every integer \\(x\\).  Moreover, as \\(x\\) decreases from \\(a\\) to \\(1\\), the value of \\(y\\) increases from \\(1\\) to \\(n+2-a\\) in steps of size \\(|\\frac{n+1-2a}{a-1}|\\).  Consequently every integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-a\n\\]\n\nappears on the line \\(M_a\\).  Since \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies exactly this inequality, we have \\((a,b)\\in M_a\\).\n\nThus each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on the line indexed by its first coordinate, and the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{M_a\\}_{a=1}^{n}\\) satisfy the covering requirement while containing exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=n\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that meet the covering condition and contain either\n\n* no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)), or  \n* all sunny lines (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nTherefore the two extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are admissible for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe status of the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) is not resolved by the constructions above and would require additional arguments.  \u220e"
