 line \\(x=a\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); again at most \\(n\\).  \n   – Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\); thus at most \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎  \n\n   Let the family contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 the sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(S_n\\); by Lemma 2 the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \n   Since \\(|S_n|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   2k+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no** configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\). This proves the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We construct explicitly a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 1 – a base covering with only non‑sunny lines.*  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) (the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_b\\)). Thus the case \\(k=0\\) is settled.\n\n   *Step 2 – replacing some horizontals by sunny lines.*  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   For each \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) consider the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_t=(t,\\,n+1-2t),\\qquad Q_t=(t+1,\\,n-2t).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(t\\le k\\le n-2\\), both coordinates are positive and satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(P_t,Q_t\\in S_n\\).  \n   The slope of the line through \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-2t)-(n+1-2t)}{(t+1)-t}= -1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line is **parallel to** \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non‑sunny**.  \n   To obtain sunny lines we slightly perturb the second point of each pair by adding