ny line remains covered.\n\n   Observe that after the removal of \\(H_1\\) and \\(H_2\\) the only uncovered points are those with \\(y\\ge 3\\) that lie on a horizontal line we might delete.  \n   For each \\(t=1,\\dots ,k\\) the sunny line \\(S_t\\) passes through the point \\((t+1,2)\\); thus the point \\((t+1,2)\\) is already covered and we may safely delete the horizontal line \\(H_2\\) (already done).  \n   Moreover, the point \\((t,1)\\) lies on \\(S_t\\), so we may delete \\(H_1\\) (already done).  \n\n   For any remaining \\(y\\)-value \\(j\\) with \\(3\\le j\\le n\\) we keep the horizontal line \\(H_j\\) **unless** the whole set of points with that \\(y\\)-coordinate is already covered by the sunny lines.  \n   The sunny line \\(S_t\\) contains the point \\((t+j-1,j)\\) exactly when \\(j-1\\le 2\\), i.e. for \\(j=2,3\\). Hence only the rows \\(y=3\\) may acquire points from sunny lines.  \n\n   Consequently we can delete all horizontal lines \\(H_j\\) with \\(j\\ge 4\\) except for exactly \\((n-2)- (k-2)=n-k\\) of them; we keep any \\(n-k\\) of those rows (for instance \\(H_3,\\dots ,H_{n-k+2}\\)).  \n\n   The final family consists of  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);  \n   * the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_3,\\dots ,H_{n-k+2}\\).\n\n   All together there are \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of them:\n   * points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t+1,2)\\) lie on \\(S_t\\);\n   * a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 3\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) which we kept because \\(b\\le n-k+2\\) (the inequality follows from \\(b\\le n\\) and the fact that we kept exactly \\(n-k\\) rows, the smallest possible ones).\n\n   Thus we have exhibited, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all required points.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Inequality (2) shows that \\(k\\