nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n1. If \\(a+b=n+1-j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) then the point belongs to the diagonal line \\(D_{j}\\).\n2. Otherwise \\(a+b\\) equals either \\(n\\) or \\(n-1\\).  \n   The line \\(S_{k-1}: y=x+1\\) contains the point \\((1,n)\\) (the case \\(a=1\\)), and the line \\(S_{k}: y=2x+1\\) contains the point \\((2,n-1)\\).  \n   All remaining points of the hypotenuse have sum \\(n\\) or \\(n-1\\) and, by the choice of the intercepts \\(i+1\\) in the definition of the \\(S_{i}\\), each such point satisfies the equation of exactly one of the sunny lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k-2}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n#### 2.4.  Counting\n\nThe construction uses precisely\n\n\\[\n(k\\text{ sunny})+(n-k\\text{ non\u2011sunny})=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\ge 2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) is indeed attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{\\,2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
