nes, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence for each \\(k\\) in the stated range a suitable configuration exists.\n\nSince the necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is also sufficient, the answer is exactly the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nWe have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering \\(T_n\\) with a prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three types  \n\n* horizontal: \\(y=b\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=a\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nFor each type we record the maximal number of points of \\(T_n\\) that a line of that type can contain.\n\n*Horizontal.*  \nIf \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the admissible \\(a\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\}\\,| = n+1-b\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical.*  \nSimilarly, a vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  \nThe line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only when \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nFor such a \\(c\\) the points are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); thus it contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points, w