g \\(L_j\\).\n\n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nSince the upper bound shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible and the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), the required set of \\(k\\) is precisely the interval stated above.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n* A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.\n* A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, by symmetry.\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\); intersecting \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) gives at most \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.\n\nThus **every non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)**. Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines does not exceed \\((n-k)n\\).\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered, so by (1)\n\n\\[\n(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n>0\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. Non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\; y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and \\(b\\le n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\); hence all points with second coordinate at most \\(n-k\\) are already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. Points that remain uncovered\n\nThe points not yet covered satisfy  \n\n\\[\nn-k+1\\le b\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le a\\le n+1-b .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor any such point we compute  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge (n-k+1)- (n+1-b)=2b-n-1\\ge 2(n-k+1)-n-1=n-2k+1,\n\\]\n\\[\nb-a\\le b-1\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus every uncovered point fulfills  \n\n\\[\nn-2k+1\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe integer values taken by \\(b-a\\) in (6) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nc_j=n-2k+j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\ni.e. there are precisely \\(k\\) possible differences.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3. Sunny lines covering the remaining points\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + c_j\\qquad\\bigl(c_j=n-2k+j\\bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(L_j\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \nThe lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because they have distinct intercepts \\(c_j\\).\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (5), then by (6) there exists a unique \\(j\\) with \\(b-a=c_j\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_j\\) given by (7). Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover all points that were not covered by the horizontal lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4. Completion of the construction\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nThe counting argument (2) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines. The explicit construction above demonstrates that every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nis attainable. Therefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor \\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
