nes already cover every point.\n\n* **Upper bound.**  \n  Obviously we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, because the total number of lines is \\(n\\).  \n  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n  \\[\n  0\\le k\\le n. \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n#### 2.1.  Sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k-2\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_{1},m_{2},\\dots ,m_{k-2}\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\n(e.g. \\(m_{i}=i\\) works for all \\(i\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=m_{i}x+(i+1)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k-2).\n\\]\n\nAdd the two further sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_{k-1}:\\; y=x+1,\\qquad   S_{k}:\\; y=2x+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these \\(k\\) lines are sunny because none of their slopes equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nTheir intercepts \\(i+1\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k-2\\)), \\(1\\) and \\(1\\) are pairwise distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n#### 2.2.  Non‑sunny lines\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and are distinct.\n\n#### 2.3.  Verification of coverage\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on \\(H_{b}\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).*  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n-k+1\\).  \nSet \\(s=a+b\\) (an integer with \\(n-k+1\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nConsider the quantities  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{i}=b-m_{i}a\\quad (1\\le i\\le k-2),\\qquad\n\\Delta_{k-1}=b-a,\\qquad\n\\Delta_{k}=b-2a .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\Delta_{i}\\) is an integer because \\(a,b\\) are integers and the slopes \\(m_{i}\\) are integers (our concrete choice \\(m_{i}=i\\) makes this true).  \nMoreover the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\Delta_{1},\\dots ,\\Delta_{k-2},\\Delta_{k-1},\\Delta_{k}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one element equal to the corresponding intercept:\n* if \\(\\Delta_{i}=i+1\\) for some \\(i\\le k-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_{i}\\);\n* if \\(\\Delta_{k-1}=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(S_{k-1}\\);\n* if \\(\\Delta_{k}=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(S_{k}\\).\n\nBecause the intercepts \\(1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\) are all distinct, exactly one of the equalities above can hold; consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on **one** of the sunny lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs either to a horizontal line \\(H_{j}\\) (when \\(b\\le n-k\\)) or to a sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) (when \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\)).  \n\nAll lines are distinct, their total number is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)=n,\n\\]\n\nand precisely the first \\(k\\) lines listed are sunny.  Hence for the chosen \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable by families of horizontal lines (with or without an extra sunny line);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) an explicit construction provides a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\);\n* trivially \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
