            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); equivalently its slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); also it cannot be negative.  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that (1) is also sufficient, i.e. no further restriction follows from the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) satisfying (1).  Partition the set \\(T_n\\) according to the second coordinate:\n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n} C_b,\\qquad \nC_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\) the column \\(C_b\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\) points, namely  \n\n\\[\nC_b=\\{(1,b),(2,b),\\dots ,(n+1-b,b)\\}.\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nAll points of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-k}\\) lie on these horizontals, because for any \\((a,b)\\in C_b\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) we have \\(y=b\\).\n\nConsequently after introducing the horizontals the only points of \\(T_n\\) that may remain uncovered are those belonging to the columns  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\,n-k+1},\\;C_{\\,n-k+2},\\dots ,C_{\\,n}.\n\\]\n\nFor each such column \\(C_{\\,n-k+i}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)) we have  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\,n-k+i}=\\{(1,n-k+i),\\,(2,n-k+i),\\dots ,(k+1-i,n-k+i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nObserve that the **topmost** point of each of these columns is  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nand that the set \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\\) consists of distinct points (they lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  Moreover, each \\(P_i\\) is not covered by any of the horizontal lines because its second coordinate \\(n+1-i\\) exceeds \\(n-k\\).\n\nThus after the horizontals the uncovered set is exactly  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{P_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines covering the remaining points\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define a line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = m_i x + c_i,\n\\]\n\nwhere we choose the slope \\(m_i\\) to be a distinct integer satisfying  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad m_i\\neq m_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\nA convenient choice is \\(m_i=i\\) (note that \\(i\\ge 1\\) so \\(m_i\\neq 0,-1\\)).  \nThe intercept \\(c_i\\) is then uniquely determined by requiring that \\(L_i\\) pass through the point \\(P_i\\):\n\n\\[\nc_i = y_i - m_i x_i = (n+1-i) - i\\cdot i = n+1-i - i^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + \\bigl(n+1-i-i^{2}\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct.  By construction each \\(L_i\\) contains the point \\(P_i\\); consequently the set \\(U_k\\) in (2) is covered.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that all points are covered\n\n* Points with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-k\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n* Points with second coordinate \\(b>n-k\\) belong to some column \\(C_{\\,n-k+i}\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \n  In that column the only point not already on a horizontal line is \\(P_i\\), and \\(P_i\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the horizontals).  All lines are distinct, and the construction works for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe only restriction on the number of sunny lines is the trivial bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  For every integer \\(k\\) in this interval we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
