st \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Horizontal*: a line \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,b)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); the number of such points is \\(n+1-b\\le n\\).  \n*Vertical*: a line \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.  \n*Slope \\(-1\\)*: a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only when \\(c\\le n+1\\).  For a fixed \\(c\\) there is at most one integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b=c\\); hence at most one point of \\(T_n\\) lies on such a line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of sunny lines (\\(k\\ge 1\\))\n\nAssume that we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, where \\(k\\ge 1\\).  \n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points.  \n\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, and by Lemma 2 each of them covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n\\[\nk + (n-k)\\cdot n = n^{2}-kn + k. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(k\\ge 1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn + k \\le n^{2}-n + 1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(n^{2}-n+1 = \\frac{2n^{2}-2n+2}{2} < \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\nBut by (1) the set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points, which is larger than the bound (3).  Hence it is impossible to cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with a family containing even a single sunny line.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nk=0. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,