2}-kn + k \\le n^{2}-n + 1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(n^{2}-n+1 = \\frac{2n^{2}-2n+2}{2} < \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\nBut by (1) the set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points, which is larger than the bound (3).  Hence it is impossible to cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with a family containing even a single sunny line.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nk=0. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.  For any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) horizontal lines cover the whole set \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe covering condition forces the number of sunny lines to be zero, and a configuration with zero sunny lines indeed exists.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
