amily of \\(n\\) distinct lines in which all \\(n\\) lines are sunny; i.e. the value \\(k=n\\) never occurs.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe prove the following statement.\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). Suppose \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) are \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines. Then at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\) is *non‑sunny* (i.e. it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nWe prove the lemma by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe required set of points is  \n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that three distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) cover \\(T_3\\).  \nA sunny line has slope belonging to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nHence the three slopes are pairwise distinct, because two distinct lines with the same slope would be parallel and could not both contain a point of \\(T_3\\) (otherwise they would coincide, contradicting distinctness).\n\nTwo non‑parallel lines intersect in exactly one point.  \nThus the three lines intersect pairwise in three distinct points.  \nBut the only points that may belong to the configuration are the three points of \\(T_3\\); consequently the three intersection points must be precisely \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\nHowever, the three points of \\(T_3\\) are not collinear, and no three lines with slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) can have their three pairwise intersections equal to these three points (a direct verification shows that at least one of the three lines would have to be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThis contradiction shows that among any three lines covering \\(T_3\\) at least one line is non‑sunny. ∎\n\n---\n\n### Inductive step\n\nAssume the lemma holds for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nWe prove it for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\