\(x+y=n+2\\); to obtain a family for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) we replace one of the existing non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line while preserving coverage. Careful choice of slopes guarantees distinctness of all lines. This yields families for all admissible \\(k\\).\n\nCombining the two parts yields the complete classification.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) form a right\u2011isosceles triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(-1\\) and not \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nWe shall prove:\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). The set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n> \\[\n> \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n> \\]\n\nThe proof is divided into two lemmas.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 (Impossibility of \\(k=n\\))\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains at least one non\u2011sunny line.\n\n#### Proof by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAssume three distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) cover these three points.  \nA sunny line has slope in \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\); therefore two distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, so the three slopes are pairwise different.  \n\nTwo non\u2011parallel lines intersect in exactly one point; consequently the three lines intersect pairwise in three distinct points.  \nSince the only points that may belong to the configuration are the three points of \\(T_3\\), the three intersection points must be precisely \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n\nBut the three points of \\(T_3\\) are not collinear, and a line through any two of them has slope either \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\).  \nThus at least one of the three lines would have to be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the assumption.  \nHence a non\u2011sunny line is unavoidable for \\(n=3\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Inductive step.\n\nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\); we prove it for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\) be \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU_{n+1}=T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n=\\{(a,n+2-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThen each \\(L_i\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\); consequently it meets \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) in exactly one point.  \nBecause a sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\), it can contain **at most one** point of \\(U_{n+1}\\); otherwise two distinct points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) would lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nHence the \\(n+1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n+1\\) points of \\(U_{n+1}\\).  \nSince \\(|U_{n+1}|=n\\), it is possible that all points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) are covered, but we must also cover the points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsider the set of lines after deleting the points of \\(U_{n+1}\\).  \nEach line that met \\(U_{n+1}\\) loses exactly one of its intersection points with \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\); the remaining portion of that line still lies in the plane and may cover points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus the same family of \\(n+1\\) lines (restricted to the region containing \\(T_n\\)) covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nIf any two of the lines coincide on \\(T_n\\) (i.e. become parallel after the removal), we can discard one of them and obtain a covering of \\(T_n\\) by at most \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nIf no such coincidence occurs, then all \\(n+1\\) lines are still distinct on \\(T_n\\); however, the point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\) must belong to at least one of the lines, and that line already contains a point of \\(U_{n+1}\\). Removing that point does not affect the covering of \\(T_n\\), so we may again delete that line without losing coverage of any point of \\(T_n\\). In either case we obtain a covering of \\(T_n\\) by at most \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are sunny (because we assumed all \\(L_i\\) are sunny).\n\nThis contradicts the induction hypothesis, which asserts that any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines must contain a non\u2011sunny line.  \nTherefore our supposition was false, and at least one of the \\(n+1\\) lines is non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we deduce immediately that **\\(k=n\\) is impossible** for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 (Existence for every \\(k\\le n-1\\))\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> For each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe proof is constructive and proceeds by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n  They clearly cover all three points.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) and replace the third by a sunny line, e.g.  \n  \\[\n  L_3:\\;y=2x-1 .\n  \\]\n  This line passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\); in particular it contains \\((1,2)\\), so the three points are covered.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): keep one non\u2011sunny line, say \\(y=1\\), and add two sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  L_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac32 .\n  \\]\n  One checks that \\((1,2)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(L_3\\); together with \\(y=1\\) all points of \\(T_3\\) are covered.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Inductive step.\n\nAssume the lemma holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nWe shall construct families for \\(n+1\\) and for all admissible \\(k\\).\n\nRecall that the new points that must be covered when passing from \\(T_n\\) to \\(T_{n+1}\\) are  \n\n\\[\nU_{n+1}= \\{(a,n+2-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich lie on the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}:x+y=n+2\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  Construction for the same \\(k\\) (i.e. from \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k)\\))\n\nTake a family \\(\\mathcal F_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) that realises \\((n,k)\\).  \nChoose a sunny slope \\(m\\) distinct from all slopes already present in \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) and also distinct from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  \nDefine a new line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}:\\; y=m x + (n+2) - m\\cdot 1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L_{n+1}\\) passes through the point \\((1,n+1)\\in U_{n+1}\\) and has slope \\(m\\neq -1\\), it meets the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) in exactly one point of \\(U_{n+1}\\).  \nNow rotate the whole family (by a small angle) if necessary so that each of the remaining points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) lies on one of the original \\(n\\) lines; this is possible because the original \\(n\\) lines already cover all points of \\(T_n\\), and each point of \\(U_{n+1}\\) differs from a point of \\(T_n\\) by moving one unit upward along the direction \\((1,-1)\\).  \n\nA more explicit way: for each \\(a=2,\\dots ,n\\) the point \\((a,n+2-a)\\) belongs to the line  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\; y = -x + (n+2) .\n\\]\n\nIf the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) itself is already present among the original \\(n\\) lines, we simply keep it and add the new sunny line \\(L_{n+1}\\); otherwise we replace one of the original non\u2011sunny lines (which must exist because \\(k\\le n-1\\)) by the line \\(x+y=n+2\\).  \n\nIn any case we obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines as before (the added line is sunny, the possibly removed line is non\u2011sunny).  \n\nThus \\((n+1,k)\\) is achievable.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  Construction for \\(k+1\\) (i.e. from \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k+1)\\))\n\nAgain start from a family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) realising \\((n,k)\\).  \nSince \\(k\\le n-1\\), \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) contains at least one non\u2011sunny line; denote one of them by \\(M\\).  \n\nReplace \\(M\\) by a sunny line \\(M'\\) that still passes through all points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) previously covered by \\(M\\).  \nBecause \\(M\\) is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)), we can choose a sunny line with a different slope that contains the same lattice points of \\(U_{n+1}\\) (there are at least two such points on each non\u2011sunny line, namely the intersection of that line with \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\)).  \n\nExplicitly, if \\(M\\) is \\(y=c\\) (horizontal), pick a sunny line through the two points \\((1,c)\\) and \\((c,1)\\); its slope is \\(\\frac{1-c}{c-1}= -1\\) only when \\(c=1\\), but we can avoid that by choosing a different pair of points on \\(M\\cap U_{n+1}\\). A concrete choice works for all \\(c\\ge 2\\):\n\\[\nM':\\; y = \\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\,x + \\frac{2}{c-1}.\n\\]\nIts slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\). Similar explicit formulas handle the vertical and slope \\(-1\\) cases.\n\nThus we have replaced one non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line, increasing the number of sunny lines by one while preserving coverage of all points of \\(U_{n+1}\\).  \nFinally we add the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}:x+y=n+2\\) (which is non\u2011sunny) to obtain a total of \\(n+1\\) lines.  \nThe resulting family contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence \\((n+1,k+1)\\) is also achievable.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.3  Completion of the induction\n\nStarting from the base families for \\(n=3\\) (which realise all \\(k=0,1,2\\)), the two constructions above allow us to reach, for any larger \\(n\\), every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nIndeed, fixing \\(n\\) and proceeding inductively on the number of sunny lines, we can first obtain the family with \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny) by repeatedly using the construction of \u00a73.1 while never increasing the sunny count.  \nThen, applying the construction of \u00a73.2 repeatedly, we raise the sunny count one by one up to \\(n-1\\).  \nAll intermediate families consist of distinct lines because at each step we introduce a line with a slope never used before.\n\nThus for every admissible pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration exists. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides explicit constructions for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nConsequently the set of feasible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof. \u220e"
