            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) form a right‑isosceles triangular lattice of side length \\(n\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(-1\\) and not \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nWe shall prove:\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). The set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n> \\[\n> \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n> \\]\n\nThe proof is divided into two lemmas.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 (Impossibility of \\(k=n\\))\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains at least one non‑sunny line.\n\n#### Proof by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAssume three distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) cover these three points.  \nA sunny line has slope in \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\); therefore two distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, so the three slopes are pairwise different.  \n\nTwo non‑parallel lines intersect i