s impossible, establishing the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   - **Step 1 (non‑sunny backbone).**  \n     Use two non‑sunny lines:\n     \\[\n     L_{1}\\colon y=1,\\qquad L_{2}\\colon x=1 .\n     \\]\n     These two lines contain respectively the points \\((a,1)\\) for \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) and \\((1,b)\\) for \\(2\\le b\\le n\\).  \n     All points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) are therefore already covered.\n\n   - **Step 2 (cover the interior with sunny pairs).**  \n     The remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n     List them in order of increasing sum \\(s=a+b\\) (which runs from \\(4\\) to \\(n+1\\)).  \n     For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line\n     \\[\n     S_{s}\\colon y = -(a_{s})\\,x + s,\n     \\]\n     where \\(a_{s}\\) is any rational number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (for instance \\(a_{s}=2\\)).  \n     The line \\(S_{s}\\) passes through exactly the two lattice points\n     \\[\n     (2,\\,s-2)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (s-2,\\,2),\n     \\]\n     both of which satisfy \\(a+b=s\\) and lie in the required triangular region.  \n     Because the slope \\( -a_{s}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), each \\(S_{s}\\) is sunny.\n\n   - **Step 3 (counting lines).**  \n     The number of interior sums \\(s\\) is \\((n+1)-4+1 = n-2\\).  \n     Hence we have constructed exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(S_{4},S_{5},\\dots ,S_{n+1}\\) together with the two non‑sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\), giving a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   - **Step 4 (adjusting the number of sunny lines).**  \n     To obtain a configuration with fewer than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, simply replace some of the sunny lines \\(S_{s}\\) by a diagonal non‑sunny line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) that passes through the same two points.  \n     Since a diagonal line is allowed (it is non‑sunny), each replacement reduces the number of sunny