ntributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   However, the set \\(T_n\\) contains exactly  \n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3)\n   \\]  \n   points, which is impossible. Hence the assumption is false, and at least two lines must be sunny.  \n\nThe argument establishes the necessary lower bound \\(k\\ge 2\\). The complementary upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) is trivial (there are only \\(n\\) lines). Determining whether every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) can actually be realized remains open.  \n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the number of integer solutions of \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\).  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions of \\(a+b=s\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of *sunny* lines among them, i.e. lines that are **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{e}_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{-}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nWe prove that \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA line that is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Case 1:* \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so its equation is \\(y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\cap\\ell\\) then \\(b=c\\). The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\). Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\) must be an integer, there is at most one admissible value of \\(a\\); thus at most one point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*Case 2:* \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. \\(x=c\\). The same reasoning with the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) exchanged gives the claim.\n\n*Case 3:* \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); its equation can be written \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\cap\\ell\\) then \\(a+b=c\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\), the only possible integer solutions are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\). The additional inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\). For each admissible \\(c\\) there is exactly one pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most one point. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is sunny, then the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines is at most \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\). If there are at most one sunny line, the total contribution is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{possible point on the sunny line})\\;+\\;(n-1)\\times 1\\;=\\;n.\n\\]  \n\u220e\n\n\n\n#### Contradiction  \n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that \\(k\\le 1\\); i.e. at most one line among the \\(n\\) covering lines is sunny.  \nThen, by Lemma\u202f2, the \\(n\\) lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHowever, from (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3.\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n\\) lines cannot possibly cover all points of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \n\nTherefore the assumption \\(k\\le 1\\) is false, and we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge 2}.\n\\]\n\nSince obviously \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines), the admissible values of \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n2\\le k\\le n.\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations attaining every integer \\(k\\) in this interval remains an open sub\u2011problem. \u220e  "
