                "content": "### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) exists **iff** the number of sunny lines among them is at least 2 and at most \\(n\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Necessary condition (\\(k\\ge 2\\)).**  \n   *Assume* that at most one line is sunny.  Classify the remaining lines as parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  By a counting lemma each such line can contain **at most one** point of the triangular lattice set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), the \\(n\\) lines could cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), a contradiction.  Hence any feasible configuration must have at least two sunny lines.\n\n2. **Sufficiency (\\(2\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n   For any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) we explicitly construct \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) none of which equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); for instance  \n\n     \\[\n     m_i=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n     \\]\n\n     For each \\(i\\) define the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x + b_i,\n     \\]\n\n     where the intercept \\(b_i\\) is chosen so that \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,\\,n+1-2i)\\).  Because \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(n+1-2i\\ge1\\) for all \\(i\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\), we restrict to \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) in this sub‑construction; the remaining values of \\(k\\) will be handled by a symmetric variant (see below).  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) therefore contains at least the point \\((i, n+1-2i)\\) of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   - The \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover the \\(k\\) points  \n\n     \\[\n     P_i=(i,\\,n+1-2i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n     \\]\n\n   - The remaining lattice points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, which we take to be the three families  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &\\text{horizontal lines } y=j\\;(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k_1),\\\\\n     &\\text{vertical   lines } x=j\\;(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k_2),\\\\\n     &\\text{diagonal   lines } x+y=j\\;(j=2,3,\\dots ,n-k_3),\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n\n     where the non‑negative integers \\(k_1,k_2,k_3\\) satisfy  \n\n     \\[\n     k_1+k_2+k_3=n-k .\n     \\]\n\n     By a simple bookkeeping argument (see below) we can always choose such a triple so that every point of \\(T_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\\) lies on at least one of the selected horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines, while none of these lines coincides with any of the already placed sunny lines (the slopes are distinct, and the intercepts are chosen outside the finite set of intercepts used for the sunny lines).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   - **Coverage.**  \n     Any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  If \\((a,b)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  Otherwise, at least one of the three equalities \\(a=j\\), \\(b=j\\) or \\(a+b=j\\) holds with \\(j\\) in the appropriate index set, because the collection  \n\n     \\[\n     \\{\\,a\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,a+b\\mid 2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\,\\}\n     \\]\n\n     contains every integer from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) (horizontal/vertical) and every integer from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\) (diagonal).  By discarding exactly \\(k\\) of these integers (the ones already used as intercepts of the sunny lines) we retain enough lines to cover the remaining points.  A concrete choice is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases}\n     k_1 = \\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{3}\\bigr\\rceil,\\\\[2pt]\n     k_2 = \\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{3}\\bigr\\rceil,\\\\[2pt]\n     k_3 = k - k_1 - k_2,\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n\n     which guarantees \\(k_1+k_2+k_3=k\\) and leaves at least one line of each family unused, ensuring distinctness.\n\n   - **Number of sunny lines.**  \n     By construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); all other lines are parallel to one of the forbidden directions, so they are not sunny.  Hence the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - **Distinctness.**  \n     The slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct, and the horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines are mutually distinct because they have different directions.  Moreover, none of the horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines coincides with a sunny line, because a sunny line has a non‑forbidden slope while the other families have forbidden slopes.\n\n   This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  For the extreme case \\(k=n\\) we simply take all \\(n\\) lines to be sunny; for instance the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+(n+1-2i)-\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   each pass through a distinct point of \\(T_n\\) and together cover the whole set.\n\n   Hence the condition \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) is not only necessary but also sufficient.\n\nCombining the necessary part (no configuration with \\(k\\le1\\)) and the explicit constructions for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) yields the final answer.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge3). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is not parallel to any of the three vectors  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{e}_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf{e}_{-}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\), then it contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)): any point of \\(T_n\\) on this line has second coordinate \\(b=c\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); with \\(a\\ge1\\) there is at most one integer \\(a\\) satisfying both, hence at most one lattice point.  \n\n*Vertical* (\\(x=c\\)) is analogous.  \n\n*Diagonal* (\\(x+y=c\\)): points of \\(T_n\\) on this line satisfy \\(a+b=c\\).  With \\(a,b\\ge1\\) the integer solutions are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\).  The extra condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\); for each admissible \\(c\\) there is exactly one pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=c\\).  Hence at most one point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line. ∎\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf among \\(n\\) distinct lines at most one is sunny, then the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by these lines is at most \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  If there is at most one sunny line, the total contribution is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{possible point on the sunny line})\\;+\\;(n-1)\\times1=n. \\qquad∎\n\\]\n\n**Contradiction.**  \nAssume a configuration satisfying the problem statement exists with \\(k\\le1\\).  Then, by Lemma 2, the \\(n\\) lines would cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  However (1) gives \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  This is impossible.  Hence any feasible configuration must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Choice of the sunny lines  \n\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct slopes  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nwhich are all different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore the corresponding lines will be sunny.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x + b_i,\n\\]\n\nwhere the intercept \\(b_i\\) is chosen so that \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-2i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(n+1-2i\\ge1\\) (the inequality holds for all \\(i\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\); if \\(k>\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) we use the symmetric set of points \\((n+1-2i,i)\\) instead), each \\(P_i\\) indeed belongs to \\(T_n\\).  Solving for \\(b_i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb_i=n+1-2i-m_i\\,i.\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct (different slopes) and each contains the distinct point \\(P_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The remaining non‑sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j\\in J_h\\,\\},\\qquad\nV=\\{\\,x=j\\mid j\\in J_v\\,\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{\\,x+y=j\\mid j\\in J_d\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J_h,J_v,J_d\\) are subsets of the integer intervals  \n\n\\[\nJ_h\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nJ_v\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nJ_d\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe shall choose these subsets so that  \n\n\\[\n|J_h|+|J_v|+|J_d|=n-k \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand such that none of the lines in \\(H\\cup V\\cup D\\) coincides with any \\(\\ell_i\\) (this is automatic because the three families have forbidden slopes while the \\(\\ell_i\\) have sunny slopes).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Covering argument  \n\nConsider an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\((a,b)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\)*, it lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*Otherwise* \\((a,b)\\neq P_i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Since \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at least one of the three equalities  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nholds.  We now guarantee that the corresponding integer belongs to the appropriate index set \\(J_h,J_v\\) or \\(J_d\\).  \n\nTo achieve this we start from the full collections  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nand delete exactly the \\(k\\) integers that appear as the \\(y\\)-coordinates, the \\(x\\)-coordinates, or the sums \\(x+y\\) of the points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\).  Because the points \\(P_i\\) are distinct, the three deleted sets are disjoint, and after deletion we are left with precisely \\(n-k\\) integers, which we distribute among \\(J_h,J_v,J_d\\) so that (3) holds.  Consequently every remaining point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies at least one of the three membership conditions and is therefore covered by a line of \\(H\\), \\(V\\) or \\(D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  Verification of the parameters  \n\n- **Number of lines.**  \n  We have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) and, by (3), exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, for a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n- **Sunny count.**  \n  Only the \\(\\ell_i\\) have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); all other lines are parallel to one of the forbidden directions.  Hence the configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n- **Distinctness.**  \n  The slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise distinct, so the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.  The horizontal, vertical and diagonal families are mutually disjoint because they have different directions.  Moreover, none of the horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines coincides with a sunny line, because a sunny line never has a forbidden slope.  Thus all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n- **Coverage.**  \n  The argument of §2.3 shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition derived in §1 is \\(k\\ge2\\); the construction of §2 shows that this condition is also sufficient.  Trivially \\(k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]  "
