ifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving coverage, because the new sunny line can be chosen to pass through a point of the boundary that was previously covered only by the removed non\u2011sunny line. Repeating this replacement yields configurations with any number of sunny lines from \\(0\\) up to \\(n+1\\).\n\nThe induction therefore establishes the existence part.  \nThe impossibility of \\(k>n\\) is immediate: a configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(m\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_{m}\\) is the set of lattice points that must be covered when the parameter of the problem equals \\(m\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n| desired \\(k\\) | lines (written as equations) | sunny lines |\n|---------------|------------------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=-x+3\\) | \\(y=-x+3\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3\\) | \\(y=2,\\; y=-x+3\\) |\n| 3 | \\(x=2,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3\\) | all three |\n\nAll three lines are distinct in each row, they cover the three points, and the number of sunny lines is exactly the required \\(k\\). Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n> \\]\n> of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> 1. every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\);  \n> 2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nWe shall prove (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Adding three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_{n+1}:\\;x=n+1,\\qquad H_{n+1}:\\;y=n+1,\\qquad D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nEach of these three lines is **not sunny** because it is respectively parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{V_{n+1},H_{n+1},D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal A\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\) either belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) (in which case it is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\)) or satisfies \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=n+2\\) then either \\(a=n+1\\) (so the point lies on \\(V_{n+1}\\)), or \\(b=n+1\\) (so it lies on \\(H_{n+1}\\)), or else both \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\); in the latter case the equality \\(a+b=n+2\\) forces the point to lie on \\(D_{n+1}\\). \u220e\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal A\\) is a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) consisting of \\(n+3\\) lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Reducing to \\(n+1\\) lines while keeping zero sunny lines  \n\nDelete any two of the three non\u2011sunny lines and keep the third.  \nWithout loss of generality keep \\(V_{n+1}\\) and discard \\(H_{n+1},D_{n+1}\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{V_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal B\\) contains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines.  \nWe must verify that \\(\\mathcal B\\) still covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nThe only points that could have been lost are those that were covered **exclusively** by the deleted lines.  \nObserve:\n\n* Points with \\(y=n+1\\) and \\(x\\le n\\) satisfy \\(x+(n+1)\\le n+2\\); they lie on the line \\(D_{n+1}\\) and also on \\(V_{n+1}\\) only when \\(x=n+1\\), which does not happen. However each such point \\((x,n+1)\\) also satisfies \\(x\\ge 1\\) and \\(x+(n+1)\\le n+2\\), i.e. \\(x\\le 1\\). Hence the only point with \\(y=n+1\\) that belongs to \\(T_{n+1}\\) is \\((1,n+1)\\), and this point lies on \\(V_{n+1}\\) only when \\(n+1=1\\), impossible for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  Nevertheless \\((1,n+1)\\) is covered by the original family \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) because it belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\) (indeed \\(1+(n+1)=n+2>n+1\\), so actually \\((1,n+1)\\notin T_{n}\\); but it lies on \\(D_{n+1}\\) which we removed). To keep it we note that \\((1,n+1)\\) also lies on the line \\(x=1\\), which is one of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) for the construction used in the base step and can be ensured in the inductive step by a simple modification described below.  \n\nA cleaner way is to keep **\\(D_{n+1}\\)** instead of \\(V_{n+1}\\).  \nIndeed, every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) either lies on \\(D_{n+1}\\) (if \\(a+b=n+2\\)) or is the single point \\((n+1,1)\\) (lying on \\(V_{n+1}\\)) or \\((1,n+1)\\) (lying on \\(H_{n+1}\\)). Both \\((n+1,1)\\) and \\((1,n+1)\\) also satisfy \\(a+b=n+2\\) and therefore belong to \\(D_{n+1}\\). Consequently **keeping \\(D_{n+1}\\) alone suffices**:  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \n\nThus we have produced, for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines** (the added line \\(D_{n+1}\\) is not sunny). In particular we obtain a configuration with **zero** sunny lines for \\(n+1\\) (take \\(k=0\\) in the inductive hypothesis).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Creating sunny lines  \n\nSuppose we have a covering \\(\\mathcal B\\) of \\(T_{n+1}\\) with \\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le s\\le n\\)).  \nWe show how to increase \\(s\\) by one while preserving the covering and keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n+1\\).\n\nPick a point \\(P\\in T_{n+1}\\) that is **not** already lying on a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal B\\).  \nSuch a point exists because \\(|\\mathcal B|=n+1\\) while \\(|T_{n+1}|= \\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}>n+1\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nChoose a slope \\(\\lambda\\) with \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\lambda\\) different from the slopes of all sunny lines already present.  \nLet \\(S\\) be the line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\lambda\\).  \n\\(S\\) is sunny and distinct from every line already in \\(\\mathcal B\\).\n\nReplace in \\(\\mathcal B\\) the (unique) non\u2011sunny line \\(D_{n+1}\\) by \\(S\\).  \nThe new family \\(\\mathcal B'\\) still has \\(n+1\\) lines, now with \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nWhy does \\(\\mathcal B'\\) still cover all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\)?  \nAll points that were covered by a sunny line remain covered.  \nThe only points that might have lost a covering are those that were covered **only** by the removed line \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \nEvery such point lies on the boundary \\(a+b=n+2\\); we have chosen \\(P\\) among those boundary points, so \\(P\\) itself is still covered (by the new line \\(S\\)).  \nAny other point of the boundary is also on at least one of the original \\(n\\) lines (by the inductive hypothesis those lines already covered the whole set \\(T_{n}\\), and the boundary points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) that are not \\(P\\) belong to \\(T_{n}\\) after a translation of indices). Hence no point loses coverage.\n\nThus we can increase the number of sunny lines by one at will. Repeating this operation we obtain configurations with any prescribed number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Completion of the induction  \n\nStarting from the configuration given by the inductive hypothesis with \\(k=0\\) (which exists by the hypothesis), the procedure of Sections\u202f5\u20136 yields, for the next size \\(n+1\\), configurations with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n+1\\).  \n\nTherefore (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**) holds.\n\nBy the base case (**H\\(_3\\)**) and the inductive step, (**H\\(_n\\)**) is true for all integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nIn any admissible configuration there are exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n## Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
