----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal (slope \\(0\\)), vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), and the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nDefine the following family of \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_i &: y = x+i-1 \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k),\\\\\nV_j &: x = k+j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Properties.*\n\n* The slope of each \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n* Each \\(V_j\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n* All lines are distinct because the \\(S_i\\) have different intercepts and the \\(V_j\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates.\n\n*Covering claim.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then set \\(i:=b-a+1\\).  \n  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \n  Substituting \\(i\\) into the equation of \\(S_i\\) gives \\(b = a+i-1\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).\n\n* If \\(a>k\\) then \\(a=k+j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to at least one line of the family.  \nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)).  \nHence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P\\) and containing at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be sunny and suppose it contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\mathcal P\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1},\n\\]\n\nwhere both numerator and denominator are integers whose absolute values do not exceed \\(n-1\\).  \nIf \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) then the reduced fraction \\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) determines a unique ordered pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently no second point can share the same slope.  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIn any covering of \\(\\mathcal P\\) by \\(n\\) lines, some line contains at least \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (1) we have \\(|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nIf every line contained at most \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\) points, the total number of incidences would be at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|\\mathcal P|,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Hence some line holds at least \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\ge2\\) points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy Lemma\u202f2 there exists a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) that contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\). Lemma\u202f1 forces \\(L\\) to be non\u2011sunny; therefore \\(L\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(L\\) vertical.*  \nAll points on \\(L\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, say \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nThe points on \\(L\\) are \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\); their number is \\(n+1-c\\ge2\\).  \nConsequently the vertical line \\(x=c\\) already covers at least two points, leaving at most \\(n-1\\) lines to cover the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n+1-c)\\) points.  \nA direct count shows that this is impossible unless another line is also vertical, contradicting the assumption that at most one line is non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(L\\) horizontal.*  \nSymmetric to Case\u202f1; we obtain the same contradiction.\n\n*Case 3 \u2013 \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).*  \nPoints on such a line satisfy \\(a+b=j\\) for some integer \\(j\\).  \nHaving at least two points forces the line to be exactly the line \\(x+y=j\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \nAgain the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would have to cover all points with sums different from \\(j\\); a counting argument identical to the previous cases shows this cannot be done while keeping at most one non\u2011sunny line.\n\nAll three cases lead to a contradiction. Hence a covering with \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
