rizontal line only if they have the same \\(b\\)-coordinate; but for a fixed \\(b\\) there is at most one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) with that \\(b\\) and a given sum \\(a+b\\), so our pairing never uses two points with equal \\(b\\).  \nThe same argument excludes a vertical line.  \nIf the slope were \\(-1\\) then the two points would satisfy \\(a_{1}+b_{2}=a_{2}+b_{1}\\), i.e. they would lie on the same diagonal \\(x+y=c\\); but all points on a given diagonal are already covered by the non‑sunny lines \\(L_{c}\\), so our pairing never selects two points from the same diagonal.  \n\nHence every line obtained in this way is sunny.  \nBecause each pair is distinct, the resulting \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct.\n\n#### 2.4  Completion of the covering\n\nThe \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(L_{c}\\) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed above give exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covering all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe inequality (1) is necessary, and the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) can indeed be realised.  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\Big|\\;\n0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Big\\rfloor\\right\\}} .\n\\]"
