\nFor a positive integer \\(m\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe following four families each consist of three distinct lines, cover \\(T_{3}\\), and contain exactly the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nk & \\text{family of lines} & \\text{sunny lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2 & \\text{none}\\\\\n1 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=-x+3 & y=-x+3\\\\\n2 & x=1,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3 & y=2,\\; y=-x+3\\\\\n3 & x=2,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3 & \\text{all three}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the statement is true for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds.\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n> \\]\n> of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nWe shall prove (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Adding three non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_{n+1}:x=n+1,\\qquad H_{n+1}:y=n+1,\\qquad D_{n+1}:x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nEach of these three lines is non‑sunny.  \nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{V_{n+1},H_{n+1},D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal A\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) it is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_{n+1}\\). ∎\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal A\\) is a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) consisting of \\(n+3\\) lines and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Reducing to \\(n+1\\) lines – covering lemma  \n\nKeep only the diagonal line \\(D_{n+1}\\) and discard the two axis‑parallel lines. Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 5.1.* \\(\\mathcal B\\) covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPoints of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) (inductive hypothesis).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_{n+1}\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal B\\). ∎\n\nConsequently we have a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, **none** of which is sunny (the added line \\(D_{n+1}\\) is non‑sunny). This yields the case \\(k=0\\) for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal C\\) be any covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) consisting of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines and containing \\(s\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le s\\le n\\). We describe a procedure that produces a new covering \\(\\mathcal C'\\) with \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Existence of a point not on a sunny line.**  \n   Each sunny line can contain at most \\(n+1\\) points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the whole set has size \\(\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}>n+1\\)). Hence the union of all sunny lines contains at most \\(s(n+1)\\le n(n+1)\\) points, which is strictly fewer than \\(|T_{n+1}|\\). Therefore a point  \n\n   \\[\n   P=(p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\n   \\]\n\n   exists that does not lie on any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\n2. **The non‑sunny line through \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(\\mathcal C\\) covers \\(T_{n+1}\\), the point \\(P\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\). Since it is not on a sunny line, it must belong to a non‑sunny line; denote this line by \\(L\\). By construction \\(L\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n3. **Choosing a sunny replacement.**  \n   Select a slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\lambda\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal C .\n   \\]\n\n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{(x,y)\\mid y-q=\\lambda (x-p)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is sunny and passes through \\(P\\).\n\n4. **Form the new family**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C':=\\bigl(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S\\}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n\n   *Lemma 6.1.* \\(\\mathcal C'\\) still covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - Points lying on a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) remain covered, because those lines are unchanged.  \n   - Points lying on a non‑sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) different from \\(L\\) are also unchanged.  \n   - Consider a point \\(Q\\) that was covered **only** by \\(L\\). The set \\(L\\cap T_{n+1}\\) is a finite arithmetic progression of lattice points lying on a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). One endpoint of this progression is the boundary point \\(P\\) (the only point of the progression that is not on any sunny line). Choose the slope \\(\\lambda\\) so that the line \\(S\\) passes through the two extreme points of the progression; this is possible because any two distinct lattice points determine a unique line, and the slope of that line cannot be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (otherwise the two points would already lie on a non‑sunny line, contradicting the choice of the extreme points). Consequently **all** points of the progression, and in particular every point that was covered only by \\(L\\), lie on \\(S\\). Hence every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) remains covered. ∎\n\n   The construction replaces a non‑sunny line by a sunny one, thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Obtaining every \\(k\\)  \n\nStarting from the covering \\(\\mathcal B\\) of Lemma 5.1 (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines), apply the replacement procedure of Section 6 repeatedly. After the \\(k\\)-th application we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Because each step replaces a distinct non‑sunny line, we can perform at most \\(n+1\\) steps, giving coverings for all \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor the original problem the number of lines must be exactly \\(n\\); the construction above yields \\(n+1\\) lines. To obtain a configuration with **\\(n\\) lines**, simply discard one of the non‑sunny lines that is *not* needed for the covering. In the family produced after the \\(k\\)-th replacement, the only non‑sunny line present is the original diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (if it has not been replaced) or a line that became non‑sunny after a previous replacement. Removing that line does not destroy the covering because every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that was covered solely by that line is also on the newly introduced sunny line (by Lemma 6.1). Hence we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step together prove that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
