l, or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n3. **Choosing a sunny replacement.**  \n   Select a slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\lambda\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal C .\n   \\]\n\n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{(x,y)\\mid y-q=\\lambda (x-p)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is sunny and passes through \\(P\\).\n\n4. **Form the new family**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C':=\\bigl(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S\\}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n\n   *Lemma 6.1.* \\(\\mathcal C'\\) still covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - Points lying on a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) remain covered, because those lines are unchanged.  \n   - Points lying on a non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) different from \\(L\\) are also unchanged.  \n   - Consider a point \\(Q\\) that was covered **only** by \\(L\\). The set \\(L\\cap T_{n+1}\\) is a finite arithmetic progression of lattice points lying on a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). One endpoint of this progression is the boundary point \\(P\\) (the only point of the progression that is not on any sunny line). Choose the slope \\(\\lambda\\) so that the line \\(S\\) passes through the two extreme points of the progression; this is possible because any two distinct lattice points determine a unique line, and the slope of that line cannot be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (otherwise the two points would already lie on a non\u2011sunny line, contradicting the choice of the extreme points). Consequently **all** points of the progression, and in particular every point that was covered only by \\(L\\), lie on \\(S\\). Hence every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) remains covered. \u220e\n\n   The construction replaces a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one, thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Obtaining every \\(k\\)  \n\nStarting from the covering \\(\\mathcal B\\) of Lemma\u202f5.1 (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines), apply the replacement procedure of Section\u202f6 repeatedly. After the \\(k\\)-th application we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Because each step replaces a distinct non\u2011sunny line, we can perform at most \\(n+1\\) steps, giving coverings for all \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor the original problem the number of lines must be exactly \\(n\\); the construction above yields \\(n+1\\) lines. To obtain a configuration with **\\(n\\) lines**, simply discard one of the non\u2011sunny lines that is *not* needed for the covering. In the family produced after the \\(k\\)-th replacement, the only non\u2011sunny line present is the original diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (if it has not been replaced) or a line that became non\u2011sunny after a previous replacement. Removing that line does not destroy the covering because every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that was covered solely by that line is also on the newly introduced sunny line (by Lemma\u202f6.1). Hence we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step together prove that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"

