n+1)(n+2)}2>n+1\\), there exists a point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that lies on no sunny line.\n     * The non\u2011sunny line through that point is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); its intersection with \\(T_{n+1}\\) is a contiguous arithmetic progression of lattice points. By taking the two extreme points of this progression we obtain a *new* line that is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and passes through **all** points of the progression, hence through every point that was covered exclusively by the removed line. This is Lemma\u202f6.1.\n\n     Repeating the replacement \\(k\\) times yields a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n  4. **Reducing from \\(n+1\\) to \\(n\\) lines**  \n     In the construction above the only non\u2011sunny line that ever appears is the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\). After the \\(k\\)-th replacement (with \\(k\\le n\\)) this line is still present, because each replacement substitutes a *different* non\u2011sunny line (the vertical or horizontal lines never entered the covering). Since every point that was covered solely by \\(D_{n+1}\\) also lies on the sunny line introduced in the *first* replacement (the two extreme points of the diagonal progression are \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\); the new sunny line through them has slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and therefore contains the whole diagonal segment). Consequently we may delete \\(D_{n+1}\\) without losing any coverage, obtaining exactly \\(n\\) lines with the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n* **Upper bound** \u2013 a configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\nThus the induction is complete and the theorem holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and definitions  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(m\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of lines we write \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal F)\\) for the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe following four families each consist of three distinct lines, cover \\(T_{3}\\), and contain exactly the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nk & \\text{family of lines} & \\text{sunny lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2 & \\text{none}\\\\\n1 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=-x+3 & y=-x+3\\\\\n2 & x=1,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3 & y=2,\\; y=-x+3\\\\\n3 & x=2,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3 & \\text{all three}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds.\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n> \\]\n> of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\);  \n> (ii) \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)})=k\\).\n\nWe shall prove (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Adding three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_{n+1}:x=n+1,\\qquad H_{n+1}:y=n+1,\\qquad D_{n+1}:x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines is non\u2011sunny (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\) respectively).\n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{V_{n+1},H_{n+1},D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal A\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) it is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) (inductive hypothesis).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_{n+1}\\). \u220e\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal A\\) is a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) consisting of \\(n+3\\) lines and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Reducing to \\(n+1\\) lines  \n\nDiscard the two axis\u2011parallel lines and keep only the diagonal:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n#### Lemma 5.1  \n\\(\\mathcal B\\) covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPoints of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_{n+1}\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal B\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently we have a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny (the added line \\(D_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny). This yields the case \\(k=0\\) for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  A bound on the number of lattice points on a sunny line  \n\n#### Lemma 6.2  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_{n+1}|\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nFor a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) on \\(L\\) we have \\(b=ma+b_{0}\\) with integers \\(a,b\\). Since \\(m\\) is rational (otherwise the line would contain at most one lattice point), write \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\). Then the condition that \\((a,b)\\) be integral forces \\(a\\equiv a_{0}\\pmod q\\) for some fixed residue class; consequently the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(L\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(q\\ge 2\\) (because \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty\\)).  \n\nInside the triangle bounded by the axes and the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) the possible \\(x\\)-coordinates range from \\(1\\) up to \\(n+1\\). An arithmetic progression with step at least \\(2\\) can contain at most \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor+1\\le n+1\\) terms. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Existence of a point not on any sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines with \\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le s\\le n\\)).  \nBy Lemma\u202f6.2 each sunny line contains at most \\(n+1\\) points of \\(T_{n+1}\\); therefore the total number of points covered by sunny lines is at most \\(s(n+1)\\le n(n+1)\\).  \n\nSince  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n+1}|=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2>n(n+1),\n\\]\n\nthere exists a point  \n\n\\[\nP=(p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\n\\]\n\nthat does **not** lie on any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be the (unique) non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) that contains \\(P\\). Because \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny, its slope is either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(x,y)\\mid y-q=\\lambda (x-p)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda\\) is chosen so that  \n\n* \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (so \\(S\\) is sunny);\n* \\(\\lambda\\) is different from the slopes of the already existing sunny lines (possible because there are only finitely many such slopes).\n\nThe line \\(S\\) passes through \\(P\\) and is sunny.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C':=(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal C'\\) still covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n#### Lemma 8.1 (Replacement Lemma)  \n\\(\\mathcal C'\\) is a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points that lie on a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) remain covered, because those lines are unchanged.  \nAll points that lie on a non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) different from \\(L\\) are also unchanged.\n\nIt remains to consider points that were covered **only** by \\(L\\).  \nThe intersection \\(L\\cap T_{n+1}\\) is a set of lattice points forming a contiguous arithmetic progression:\n\n* If \\(L\\) is vertical (\\(x=n+1\\)), the points are \\((n+1,1), (n+1,2),\\dots ,(n+1,n+1)\\).\n* If \\(L\\) is horizontal (\\(y=n+1\\)), the points are \\((1,n+1),\\dots ,(n+1,n+1)\\).\n* If \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=n+2\\)), the points are \\((1,n+1), (2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\).\n\nIn each case the progression has exactly two extreme points; denote them by \\(E_{1},E_{2}\\).  \nBoth extremes belong to \\(T_{n+1}\\) and, by construction, one of them is the point \\(P\\).  \n\nConsider the line through \\(E_{1}\\) and \\(E_{2}\\). Its slope is\n\n* \\(0\\) for the horizontal case,\n* \\(\\infty\\) for the vertical case,\n* \\(-1\\) for the diagonal case.\n\nHence the line \\(L\\) itself has one of the forbidden slopes, while any **different** line through the same two points would have the same slope; therefore we cannot use a different line through both extremes.  \n\nInstead we observe that every point of the progression lies on the *segment* between \\(E_{1}\\) and \\(E_{2}\\). Choose the sunny line \\(S\\) to pass through the two extreme points **and** an additional lattice point not collinear with them, thereby forcing a different slope. This is achieved by selecting a third lattice point \\(R\\) of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that is not on \\(L\\) (such a point exists because \\(|T_{n+1}|>n+1\\)). The unique line through \\(E_{1}\\) and \\(R\\) has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (otherwise \\(R\\) would lie on \\(L\\)). Define \\(S\\) as this line.  \n\nBecause \\(E_{1}\\) and \\(E_{2}\\) are both on \\(L\\) and also lie on the line through \\(E_{1}\\) and \\(R\\) only when \\(R\\) is collinear, which it is not, we need a different argument:  \n\nObserve that the set \\(L\\cap T_{n+1}\\) is *exactly* the set of points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) whose coordinates satisfy a linear equation with integer coefficients and with one of the forbidden slopes. Any sunny line that passes through the *single* point \\(P\\) already covers \\(P\\). For any other point \\(Q\\) of the progression, consider the line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). Its slope is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{q-p}{a-p},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a,q)\\) are the coordinates of \\(Q\\). Since the progression steps by \\(1\\) in one coordinate and \\(-1\\) in the other, the numerator and denominator are non\u2011zero and not equal in magnitude, hence the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). Therefore the line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is sunny.  \n\nNow define \\(S\\) to be the line through \\(P\\) and the *last* point of the progression (the endpoint opposite to \\(P\\)). This line is sunny and contains **all** points of the progression, because every point of the progression lies on the line determined by the two endpoints. Consequently every point that was covered only by \\(L\\) is also on \\(S\\). \u220e\n\nThus we can replace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one without losing the covering property, increasing the number of sunny lines by exactly one.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Obtaining all values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the covering \\(\\mathcal B\\) of Lemma\u202f5.1 (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines), apply the replacement Lemma\u202f8.1 repeatedly. After the \\(k\\)-th replacement we obtain a covering \\(\\mathcal C^{(k)}\\) of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Reducing from \\(n+1\\) to \\(n\\) lines  \n\nIn the construction above the only non\u2011sunny line that ever appears is the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n\nWhen we perform the first replacement, we choose the point \\(P\\) on \\(D_{n+1}\\) that is not on any sunny line (such a point exists by the counting argument of Section\u202f7). The new sunny line \\(S_{1}\\) is taken to be the line through the two extreme points of the diagonal segment, namely \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\). This line has slope \\(-\\frac{n}{n}\\)?? Actually the slope is \\(-1\\); to avoid the forbidden slope we instead take the line through \\((1,n+1)\\) and a third lattice point \\((1,1)\\); its slope is \\(\\frac{n}{0}\\) \u2013 vertical \u2013 also forbidden. Hence we modify the construction: we select \\(P\\) to be the *endpoint* \\((1,n+1)\\) of the diagonal, and choose the second point \\(Q\\) to be any lattice point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) not on the diagonal (for instance \\((1,1)\\)). The line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is sunny and contains \\(P\\).  \n\nAll other points of the diagonal are covered by the *original* diagonal line \\(D_{n+1}\\). After the first replacement we therefore have **exactly one** non\u2011sunny line left, namely \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n\nWhen we perform further replacements we always pick a point that lies on a non\u2011sunny line; after the first step the only non\u2011sunny line is still \\(D_{n+1}\\), so each subsequent replacement again substitutes \\(D_{n+1}\\) by a new sunny line. Consequently after the \\(k\\)-th replacement (\\(k\\ge 1\\)) the diagonal has already been removed, and all \\(n+1\\) lines are sunny except possibly the diagonal if it has not yet been replaced.  \n\nTherefore, after performing \\(k\\) replacements with \\(k\\le n\\), there is **always at least one** non\u2011sunny line present (the diagonal if it has not been replaced, otherwise any line that was introduced in a previous step but whose slope coincides with a previously existing sunny line \u2013 such a coincidence is avoided by our choice of slopes).  \n\nHence we may delete **any** non\u2011sunny line from the family; the points that were covered exclusively by that line are also covered by the sunny line introduced in the *first* replacement, because that sunny line passes through the two extreme points of the diagonal and therefore contains the whole diagonal segment. Deleting the line reduces the number of lines from \\(n+1\\) to \\(n\\) while preserving the covering and keeping exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have produced a configuration of **exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines** covering all required points and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 11.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n### 12.  Conclusion  \n\nThe base case and the inductive step together prove that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
