\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of lines we write \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal F)\\) for the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe following four families each consist of three distinct lines, cover \\(T_{3}\\), and contain exactly the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nk & \\text{family of lines} & \\text{sunny lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2 & \\text{none}\\\\\n1 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=-x+3 & y=-x+3\\\\\n2 & x=1,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3 & y=2,\\; y=-x+3\\\\\n3 & x=2,\\; y=2,\\; y=-x+3 & \\text{all three}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds.\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n> \\]\n> of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\);  \n> (ii) \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)})=k\\).\n\nWe shall prove (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Adding three non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_{n+1}:x=n+1,\\qquad H_{n+1}:y=n+1,\\qquad D_{n+1}:x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines is non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\) respectively).\n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\cup\\{V_{n+1},H_{n+1},D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal A\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) it is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) (inductive hypothesis).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\)