            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set that has to be covered  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the lines’’ is exactly the requirement that the family of lines covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nC_{i}:=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}=T_{n}\\cap\\{x=i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(C_{1},C_{2},\\dots ,C_{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=C_{1}\\cup C_{2}\\cup\\cdots\\cup C_{n}.\n\\]\n\nThus the covering problem reduces to covering each column \\(C_{i}\\) by at least one line.\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_{i}\\) contains exactly the column \\(C_{i}\\); therefore \\(\\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}\\) covers \\(T_{n}\\).  \nAll these lines are vertical, hence none of them is sunny.  \nConsequently a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A covering with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor \\(i=1\\) put  \n\n\\[\nS_{1}:\\;y=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is horizontal and therefore **not** sunny; we will later replace it by a sunny line.  \nFor each \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\); indeed substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=1\\) and substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=n+1-i\\).\n\n*Claim 1.*  Every point of the column \\(C_{i}\\) lies on \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((i,b)\\in C_{i}\\). Because the three points \\((1,1),\\,(i,n+1-i),\\,(i,b)\\) are collinear (they share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(i\\) and the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\) has equation (3.1)), the point \\((i,b)\\) satisfies (3.1). ∎\n\n*Claim 2.*  For each \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope \\(\\displaystyle m_{i}= \\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(m_{i}=0\\) would imply \\(n+1-i=0\\), i.e. \\(i=n+1\\), impossible because \\(i\\le n\\).  \n\\(m_{i}=\\infty\\) would require \\(i-1=0\\), i.e. \\(i=1\\), excluded.  \n\\(m_{i}=-1\\) would give \\(n+1-i=-(i-1)\\), i.e. \\(n+2=2i\\). Since \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(i\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n\\), the equality can hold only when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(i=\\frac{n+2}{2}\\). In that case \\(m_{i}=-1\\) **does** occur; to avoid it we modify the construction for that single index as follows.\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, let \\(j=\\frac{n+2}{2}\\). Replace \\(S_{j}\\) by the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((j,n+2-j)\\); its slope is \\(\\frac{n+2-j-1}{j-1}= \\frac{n+1-j}{j-1}\\neq -1\\) because the numerator and denominator differ by \\(1\\). The new line still contains the whole column \\(C_{j}\\) (the same argument as above) and is sunny. ∎\n\nThus each \\(S_{i}\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) is sunny, and by Claim 1 the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n}:=\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every column, hence all of \\(T_{n}\\). Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Interpolating between the two extremes  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe use sunny lines for the first \\(k\\) columns and vertical lines for the remaining columns:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\;:=\\;\\{S_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{V_{i}\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\;}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nFor \\(i\\le k\\) the line \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny, hence its slope is finite and different from \\(\\infty\\); therefore it cannot coincide with any vertical line \\(V_{j}\\).  \nTwo different sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\) and \\(S_{j}\\) have different slopes because the slopes \\(\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}\\) are strictly decreasing in \\(i\\). Hence all lines in (4.1) are distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). It belongs to the unique column \\(C_{a}\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(C_{a}\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) (Claim 1).  \n* If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then \\(C_{a}\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny, so  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\bigr)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (the problem statement asks for “\\(n\\) distinct lines”). Consequently it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe converse direction follows from the trivial observation that at most \\(n\\) sunny lines can appear. Hence the complete list of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
