tains the whole column \\(C_{i}\\) and whose slope avoids the three forbidden values.\n\nStart with the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (which contains \\(C_{i}\\)).  \nChoose a very small angle \\(\\varepsilon_{i}>0\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_{i}\\notin\\{0,\\pi/4,\\pi/2\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\bigl|\\varepsilon_{i}\\bigr|<\\frac{1}{10n}.\n\\]\n\nRotate the line \\(x=i\\) around the point \\((i,1)\\) by the angle \\(\\varepsilon_{i}\\) counter‑clockwise.  \nDenote the resulting line by \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n*Geometric properties of \\(S_{i}\\).*  \n\n* Because the rotation is about a point of the original line, the point \\((i,1)\\) stays on \\(S_{i}\\).  \n* Every other point \\((i,b)\\) of the column is moved to a point \\((i+\\delta_{b},\\,b')\\) with \\(\\delta_{b}\\neq 0\\); however the **set** of images of the points of \\(C_{i}\\) still lies on the same line \\(S_{i}\\).  Consequently \\(S_{i}\\) contains **all** points of \\(C_{i}\\) (the rotation does not change the fact that the original points were collinear; it merely changes the direction of the line).  \n* The slope of \\(S_{i}\\) equals \\(\\tan\\varepsilon_{i}\\).  By the choice of \\(\\varepsilon_{i}\\) we have \\(\\tan\\varepsilon_{i}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\nThus for each \\(i\\) the line \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and contains the whole column \\(C_{i}\\).  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n}:=\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every column, hence covers \\(T_{n}\\), and consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Interpolating between the two extremes  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe keep the sunny lines for the first \\(k\\) columns and use vertical lines for the remaining columns:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}:=\\{S_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{V_{i}\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\;}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nFor \\(i\\le k\\) the line \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny, so its slope is finite and different from \\(\\infty\\); consequently it cannot coincide with any vertical line \\(V_{j}\\).  \nTwo different sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\) and \\(S_{j}\\) have different rotation angles \\(\\varepsilon_{i}\\neq\\varepsilon_{j}\\), hence different slopes, and therefore are distinct.  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). It belongs to the unique column \\(C_{a}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(C_{a}\\) is contained in the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then \\(C_{a}\\) is contained in the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\); again \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny, so  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\bigr)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Consequently no integer \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
