ts,\n   we can choose the parameter \\(\\alpha\\) so that **all** those integer points belong to the set\n   \\(\\{\\alpha i+\\beta_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}\\).  \n   This is achieved by taking \\(\\alpha\\) to be a sufficiently large positive irrational number.\n   Indeed, for each fixed \\(i\\) the numbers \\(\\alpha i+\\beta_{j}=j+( \\alpha i-\\alpha j)\\) are of the form\n   \\(j+\\gamma\\) where \\(\\gamma=\\alpha(i-j)\\) is irrational; consequently each of them lies in a distinct interval\n   \\((j-1,j)\\).  Hence the integer points of the column \\(C_{i}\\) (which are exactly the integers\n   \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-i\\)) are all hit by the family \\(\\{S_{j}\\}\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the sunny lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{n}\\).\n\n   Thus the family \\(\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\\) is a covering of \\(T_{n}\\) consisting of \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Interpolation.**  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we keep the first \\(k\\) sunny lines and replace the remaining\n   \\(n-k\\) lines by the vertical lines that were used in the non\u2011sunny construction.  Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}:=\\{S_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\}\\cup\\{x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Distinctness.*  The sunny lines have slope \\(\\alpha\\neq\\infty\\), so none of them coincides with a vertical line.\n   Their intercepts \\(\\beta_{j}\\) are distinct, hence the sunny lines are pairwise distinct.\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Coverage.*  Take any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  It belongs to the unique column \\(C_{a}\\).\n\n   * If \\(a\\le k\\) then the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\) (by the property proved in step\u202f2);\n     hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n   * If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(k)}\\) and contains the whole column\n     \\(C_{a}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) is