            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation\n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points lying in the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(1,n+1),(n+1,1)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nFor a configuration \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines we denote by  \n\n\\[\nk(\\mathcal L)=\\#\\{\\,L_i\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to determine all integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) covering \\(T_n\\) (every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)) and satisfying \\(k(\\mathcal L)=k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound: \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n> **Lemma 2.1.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). If \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\), then at least one of the lines is non‑sunny. Consequently \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nSuppose three distinct lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) cover these three points and all are sunny.  \nA sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\).  \n\nThe three points are not collinear: the segment \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\) is vertical, \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\) is horizontal, and \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence any line through two of the points has one of the forbidden directions, contradicting the assumption that all three lines are sunny.  \nThus at least one line is non‑sunny and \\(k\\le 2=3-1\\).\n\n#### Inductive step.\n\nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nConsider a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\).\n\nThe point \\((1,n+1)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n+1}\\) and is the unique point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) with maximal \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \nLet \\(L\\) be the (unique) line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains \\((1,n+1)\\).\n\nRemove \\(L\\) from the family and delete the point \\((1,n+1)\\) from the set of required points.  \nThe remaining points are exactly \\(T_n\\) because every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) already lies in \\(T_{n+1}\\) and none of them equals \\((1,n+1)\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\) cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nIf \\(L\\) were sunny, then the covering of \\(T_n\\) would be realized by the \\(n\\) remaining lines, all distinct.  \nBy the induction hypothesis, among those \\(n\\) lines there must be a non‑sunny one, which would already be a non‑sunny line in the original family \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nHence the assumption that *all* lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny leads to a contradiction.\n\nTherefore the line \\(L\\) containing \\((1,n+1)\\) cannot be sunny; it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nConsequently any covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by \\(n+1\\) distinct lines contains at least one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le (n+1)-1\\).\n\nBy induction the lemma holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n\nThus **\\(k=n\\) is impossible** for any admissible configuration, and we have the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction showing that every \\(k\\le n-1\\) is attainable\n\nWe now prove the converse: for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction is again inductive.\n\n#### 3.1  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the three non‑sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n  They clearly cover the three points.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the two lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) (non‑sunny) and replace the third by a sunny line, e.g. the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), which has slope \\(-1\\) – oops, that is non‑sunny. Choose instead the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(0\\) – also non‑sunny).  \n  Better: use the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) (slope \\(1\\)), which is sunny. Together with \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) all three points are covered and exactly one line (the slope‑\\(1\\) line) is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): keep \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) and take two sunny lines, e.g.  \n  \\[\n  L_2:\\; y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,3)),\\qquad\n  L_3:\\; y=\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac12\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(2,1.5)).\n  \\]\n  Both have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and together with \\(x=1\\) they cover all three points.\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe construct configurations for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe distinguish two sub‑cases.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.  \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\nStart from a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) for the same \\(k\\).  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{new}: x = n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe new line is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) either belongs to \\(T_n\\) (hence already covered) or has the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\). Such a point satisfies \\(a\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\le n+1\\); if \\(a=n+1\\) it lies on \\(L_{new}\\), otherwise \\(b=n+1\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\), which is already present in \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\) because the construction for \\(T_n\\) always contains the line \\(y=n\\) when needed; however we can guarantee coverage by observing that the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) and \\(a\\neq n+1\\) lies on the line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\), both of which are in \\(T_n\\) and therefore belong to some line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,k}\\). Consequently the added vertical line together with the original \\(n\\) lines cover all of \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \n\nThe number of sunny lines is unchanged, i.e. still \\(k\\). Hence we have produced a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.  \\(k=n\\).**  \n\nWe need a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nBegin with a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n,n-1}\\) (which exists by the induction hypothesis) that has \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line, say \\(L_{ns}\\).  \n\nReplace the non‑sunny line \\(L_{ns}\\) by a sunny line that still passes through all the points that were previously covered exclusively by \\(L_{ns}\\).  \nA convenient choice is the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{new}: y = (n+1)x - n .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(n+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  \nBecause \\(L_{ns}\\) was either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), the set of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L_{ns}\\) consists of a whole side of the triangle \\(T_n\\). The new line \\(L_{new}\\) contains the two vertices of that side, namely \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\