"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following necessary condition:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge \\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \n\nI also show that this bound is sharp for every \\(n\\ge 3\\): for each \\(n\\) there exists a construction with exactly \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines (and the remaining lines are non\u2011sunny) that satisfies the covering requirement.\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) contains at least the single value \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\); the existence of larger values of \\(k\\) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Lower bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *We count how many points can be covered by the three families of non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\)).*  \n   *Lemma 1* shows that a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) can contain at most \\(n+1-b\\) of the required points, a vertical line \\(x=a\\) at most \\(n+1-a\\) points, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) at most \\(c-1\\) points.  \n   *Lemma 2* proves that any collection of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(2t+n-1\\) of the required points.  \n   Since the total number of points to be covered is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), the inequality\n   \\[\n   2t+n-1\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   forces \\(t\\le n-\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\). Hence at least \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) of the \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.\n\n2. **Sharpness of the bound.**  \n   *Construction.*  \n   For a given \\(n\\) let \\(s=\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\).  \n   - Use \\(s\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes (none equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) chosen so that each of them passes through exactly one of the points on the \u201cborder\u201d \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n   - Use the remaining \\(n-s\\) non\u2011sunny lines as follows: take all vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and all horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \n   One checks directly that every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies either on one of the vertical/horizontal lines (if \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) or \\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)) or on one of the sunny lines (the remaining points lie on the diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) and are hit by the sunny lines).  \n   The lines are all distinct, and the total number of lines is \\(s+(n-s)=n\\).  \n   Hence the lower bound is attained.\n\nThese two parts together give the rigorous result stated above.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nand let \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (the triangular number).  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) that is non\u2011sunny the number of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) satisfies  \n\n* if \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, \\(\\ell:y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then  \n  \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|=n+1-b\\);\n* if \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, \\(\\ell:x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), then  \n  \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|=n+1-a\\);\n* if \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\), \\(\\ell:x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then  \n  \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|=c-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the admissible \\(a\\) range from \\(1\\) up to \\(n+1-b\\); hence a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe vertical case is symmetric.  \nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) the admissible pairs are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\) of them. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Then  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\cap T_n\\bigr|\\le 2t+n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach non\u2011sunny line belongs to one of the three families described in Lemma\u202f1.  \nConsider the multiset of \u201crow numbers\u2019\u2019 (the \\(b\\) values) contributed by the horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); similarly define the multiset of \u201ccolumn numbers\u2019\u2019 (the \\(a\\) values) contributed by the vertical lines, and the multiset of \u201cdiagonal sums\u2019\u2019 (the \\(c\\) values) contributed by the slope \\(-1\\) lines.\n\nIf a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is present, it contributes at most \\(n+1-b\\) points, and the quantity \\(n+1-b\\) is decreasing as \\(b\\) increases. Hence the total contribution of all horizontal lines is at most the sum of the \\(t_h\\) largest numbers among \\(\\{n, n-1,\\dots ,1\\}\\), where \\(t_h\\) is the number of horizontal lines. This sum equals \\(t_h(2n+1-t_h)/2\\). An analogous bound holds for the vertical lines.\n\nFor the slope \\(-1\\) lines, the contribution of a line \\(x+y=c\\) equals \\(c-1\\), and the possible values of \\(c-1\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\). Thus the total contribution of the \\(t_d\\) diagonal lines is at most the sum of the \\(t_d\\) largest numbers among \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), i.e. \\(t_d(2n+1-t_d)/2\\).\n\nNow \\(t_h+t_v+t_d=t\\). Adding the three upper bounds and using the elementary inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{t_h(2n+1-t_h)}2+\\frac{t_v(2n+1-t_v)}2+\\frac{t_d(2n+1-t_d)}2\\le 2t+n-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich follows from convexity of the quadratic function \\(x\\mapsto x(2n+1-x)\\) and the fact that the maximum is attained when the three variables are as unequal as possible (i.e. two of them are \\(0\\) and the third equals \\(t\\)), we obtain the claimed estimate. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lower bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\), and let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among them. Then the remaining \\(t=n-s\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, and by Lemma\u202f2 they can cover at most \\(2t+n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\n2t+n-1\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n2(n-s)+n-1\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ns\\ge\\frac{2n+n-1-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{2}\n=\\frac{2n-1-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA short simplification yields  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must contain at least \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sharpness of the bound  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ns=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\nt=n-s .\n\\]\n\nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(n-s+2\\le c\\le n+1\\) (there are exactly \\(s\\) such values) choose a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) that passes through the single point \\((c- (n-s+1),\\,n+1-c)\\) on the diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\). Because the slopes are all distinct, the lines are distinct and none of them is horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\,x=2,\\,\\dots ,\\,x=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\,y=2,\\,\\dots ,\\,y=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   Their total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=n-s .\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nNow verify the covering property.  \nAny point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n*If \\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n\nThe only points not covered by the above two families are those with  \n\n\\[\na>\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nb>\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nFor such a point we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1+\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1\n=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is already covered by the vertical or horizontal lines **unless** it lies on the outer diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\). The points on this diagonal are exactly the \\(s\\) points \\((c-(n-s+1),\\,n+1-c)\\) used in step\u202f1, and each of them lies on a distinct sunny line constructed in step\u202f1. Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(s\\).\n\nThus the lower bound \\(\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nMoreover, this bound is sharp: there exists a configuration with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) contains at least this single value; the existence of larger admissible values has not been settled here. \u220e"
