missible pairs are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\) of them. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Then  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\cap T_n\\Bigr|\\le 2t+n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) where \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) are respectively the horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\). Set  \n\n\\[\nh=|\\mathcal H|,\\qquad v=|\\mathcal V|,\\qquad d=|\\mathcal D|,\\qquad h+v+d=t .\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 the capacities of the possible horizontal lines are the numbers\n\\[\nn,\\,n-1,\\,\\dots ,\\,1 .\n\\]\nIf we choose any \\(h\\) horizontal lines, the total number of points they can cover is at most the sum of the \\(h\\) largest of these numbers, i.e.\n\\[\nS_h=\\sum_{i=0}^{h-1}(n-i)=h(2n+1-h)/2 .\n\\]\n\nThe same bound holds for the vertical lines, giving a contribution at most \\(S_v\\) with \\(v\\) in place of \\(h\\).\n\nFor the slope \\(-1\\) lines the possible capacities are\n\\[\n1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\nChoosing any \\(d\\) of them yields at most\n\\[\nS_d=\\sum_{i=0}^{d-1}(i+1)=d(2n+1-d)/2 .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\cap T_n\\Bigr|\n\\le S_h+S_v+S_d\n= \\frac{h(2n+1-h)+v(2n+1-v)+d(2n+1-d)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the quadratic function \\(f(x)=x(2n+1-x)\\) on \\([0,n]\\); it is concave, attaining its maximum at the endpoints. Therefore for non\u2011negative integers \\(h,v,d\\) with fixed sum \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nf(h)+f(v)+f(d)\\le f(t)+f(0)+f(0)=t(2n+1-t).\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(2\\) we obtain\n\\[\nS_h+S_v+S_d\\le \\frac{t(2n+1-t)}{2}=2t+n-1-\\frac{(t-1)(t-2)}{2}\\le 2t+n-1,\n\\]\nbecause \\((t-1)(t-2)/2\\ge 0\\). This yields the desired inequality. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lower bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among them. The remaining \\(t=n-s\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, so by Lemma\u202f2 they can cover at most \\(2t+n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered,\n\\[\n2t+n-1\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) gives\n\\[\n2(n-s)+n-1\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns\\ge\\frac{2n+n-1-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{2}\n=\\frac{2n-1-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification yields\n\\[\ns\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must contain at least  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sharpness of the bound  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ns=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\nt=n-s .\n\\]\n\n**Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake all vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and all horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\). Their total number is\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil=n-s=t .\n\\]\nAll are non\u2011sunny.\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any of the above vertical/horizontal lines are exactly the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\); there are \\(s\\) of them. For each such point \\((a,b)\\) choose a line through it whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); pick the slopes distinct for different points, which is always possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes. These \\(s\\) lines are sunny and distinct from one another and from the previously chosen vertical/horizontal lines.\n\n**Covering property.**  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); if \\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) it lies on the horizontal lin