an \\(n\\) lines. Therefore a configuration that contains a horizontal line cannot contain any sunny line; it yields \\(k=0\\).  \n   - Suppose the configuration contains **no** horizontal line. Then every line is either vertical, of slope \\(-1\\), or sunny. All three types meet each row in at most one point. Hence, to cover a row that contains \\(L\\) points we need at least \\(L\\) distinct lines. Row \\(R_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, so at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required just for this row. Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines in total, all of them must be used on \\(R_1\\), leaving no line to cover any point of the other rows. This is impossible since the other rows are non\u2011empty. Therefore a configuration with no horizontal line cannot exist unless **every** line is sunny; in that case the previous construction shows it is possible, giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n   Hence the only feasible values are \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny) and \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny). No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) can satisfy the covering condition.\n\nCombining the constructions with the impossibility proof yields the final answer \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) we denote its slope by \\(\\operatorname{slope}(\\ell)\\) (allowing the value \\(\\infty\\) for a vertical line).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Two explicit families of lines\n\n#### 2.1  All lines non\u2011sunny (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\; y=b\\qquad (b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\) are  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_b\\) contains exactly the row \\(R_b\\).  \nThe union \\(\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}L_b\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  \nAll \\(L_b\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none is sunny; the configuration uses \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0<\\frac{i}{i+1}<1,\\qquad \\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq -1,\n\\]\n\nso every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, hence the lines are distinct.\n\n*Containment of the points.*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n0<\\frac{b}{a}\\le\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\le\\frac{n}{n+1}<1 .\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)) form a strictly increasing sequence ranging from \\(\\frac12\\) to \\(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\). Consequently there exists a unique integer \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}\\le\\frac{b}{a}<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (1) by \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a\\le b<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}a .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) and \\(a\\) are integers, the left inequality forces \\(b=\\frac{i}{i+1}a\\); otherwise the right inequality would be violated because the gap between the two fractions equals \\(\\dfrac{a}{(i+1)(i+2)}<1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i+1}a,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\). Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\). The configuration uses exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of intermediate values \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains at least one sunny line and at least one non\u2011sunny line. We prove a contradiction.\n\n#### 3.1  Structure of the set \\(T_n\\)\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the **row**\n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}R_b,\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nIn particular \\(R_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, and every row is non\u2011empty.\n\n#### 3.2  Behaviour of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the following three types:\n\n* **Horizontal**: equation \\(y=c\\). It coincides with exactly one row \\(R_c\\) and contains all points of that row.\n* **Vertical**: equation \\(x=c\\). For a fixed row \\(R_b\\) the condition \\(x=c\\) determines at most one integer \\(a\\); hence a vertical line meets each row in **at most one** point.\n* **Slope \\(-1\\)**: equation \\(x+y=c\\). Again, for a fixed \\(b\\) the equation \\(a=n+1-b-c\\) yields at most one integer \\(a\\); thus a line of slope \\(-1\\) also meets each row in at most one point.\n\nConsequently, except for the horizontal case, a non\u2011sunny line can contribute **at most one** point to any given row.\n\n#### 3.3  Case analysis\n\n**Case 1 \u2013 \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a horizontal line.**  \nLet \\(y=c\\) be such a line; it covers the whole row \\(R_c\\). Every other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is not horizontal, so it meets any row \\(R_b\\) (\\(b\\neq c\\)) in at most one point. Row \\(R_b\\) contains \\(n+1-b\\ge 2\\) points for all \\(b\\le n-1\\). Hence to cover all points of \\(R_b\\) we would need at least \\(n+1-b\\) distinct non\u2011horizontal lines. Summing over all \\(b\\neq c\\) gives a total number of required lines exceeding \\(n\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\). Therefore a configuration that contains a horizontal line cannot contain any sunny line; it forces \\(k=0\\).\n\n**Case 2 \u2013 \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains no horizontal line.**  \nThen every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is either vertical, of slope \\(-1\\), or sunny. As observed above, each of these three types meets any row in **at most one** point. Consequently, to cover a row that contains \\(L\\) points we need at least \\(L\\) distinct lines. Row \\(R_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, so at least \\(n\\) distinct lines are required just for \\(R_1\\). Because \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), all lines must be used on \\(R_1\\), leaving no line to cover any point of the other rows \\(R_2,\\dots ,R_n\\), which are non\u2011empty. This is impossible unless the lines used on \\(R_1\\) are also sunny and, as shown in Section\u202f2.2, a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines can indeed cover all rows simultaneously. Hence the only viable possibility in this case is that **all** \\(n\\) lines are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\).\n\nThe two cases together show that a configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe constructions of Sections\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2 demonstrate that the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are attainable.  \nSection\u202f3 proves that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n } .\n\\]"
