stance between the two bounds in (5) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i+1}{i+2}a-\\frac{i}{i+1}a\n   =\\frac{a}{(i+1)(i+2)}<1\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\((i+1)(i+2)\\ge 2\\).  \nSince \\(b\\) is an integer, the only integer that can satisfy (5) is the lower bound; therefore  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i+1}a .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\). This yields a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line and at least one non‑sunny line. We derive a contradiction.\n\n#### 3.1  Behaviour of non‑sunny lines  \n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the following three types:\n\n* **Horizontal**: \\(y=c\\). It coincides with the whole row \\(R_c\\).\n* **Vertical**: \\(x=c\\). For a fixed row \\(R_b\\) the condition \\(x=c\\) yields a point \\((c,b)\\) only when \\(c\\le n+1-b\\); hence a vertical line meets each row in **at most one** point.\n* **Slope \\(-1\\)**: \\(x+y=c\\). Similarly, for a fixed row \\(R_b\\) there is at most one integer \\(a\\) satisfying \\(a+b=c\\); thus such a line also meets each row in at most one point.\n\nConsequently **any non‑horizontal line contributes at most one point to each row**.\n\n#### 3.2  Every line meets the first row  \n\nRow \\(R_1\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nBecause \\(|R_1|=n\\) and we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each point of \\(R_1\\) must lie on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\); otherwise some point of \\(R_1\\) would be uncovered.  \nThus every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) intersects \\(R_1\\), and distinct lines intersect \\(R_1\\) in distinct points. Hence the set of intersection points with \\(R_1\\) is precisely \\(\\{(1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.3  How many lines can miss the second row?  \n\nRow \\(R_2\\) c