  \nBecause \\(|R_1|=n\\) and we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each point of \\(R_1\\) must lie on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\); otherwise some point of \\(R_1\\) would be uncovered.  \nThus every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) intersects \\(R_1\\), and distinct lines intersect \\(R_1\\) in distinct points. Hence the set of intersection points with \\(R_1\\) is precisely \\(\\{(1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.3  How many lines can miss the second row?  \n\nRow \\(R_2\\) contains the points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them).\n\n*If a line is horizontal*, the only horizontal line intersecting \\(R_1\\) is \\(y=1\\); it does **not** meet \\(R_2\\).\n\n*If a line is vertical*, say \\(x=c\\), it meets \\(R_2\\) exactly when \\(c\\le n-1\\). Hence the only vertical line intersecting \\(R_1\\) but missing \\(R_2\\) is \\(x=n\\).\n\n*If a line has slope \\(-1\\)* and passes through \\((a,1)\\) (with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\)), its equation is \\(x+y=a+1\\). Its intersection with \\(R_2\\) would be \\((a-1,2)\\); this point belongs to \\(R_2\\) provided \\(a\\ge2\\). Therefore the only slope \\(-1\\) line intersecting \\(R_1\\) but missing \\(R_2\\) is the one through \\((1,1)\\), i.e. \\(x+y=2\\).\n\nThus **at most three** lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can fail to meet \\(R_2\\). Consequently at least \\(n-3\\) lines intersect \\(R_2\\).\n\n#### 3.4  Contradiction  \n\nSince each line contributes at most one point to a given row, the number of points of \\(R_2\\) that can be covered is at most the number of lines intersecting \\(R_2\\). Hence we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-3\\;\\ge\\;|R_2|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThis inequality simplifies to \\(-3\\ge -1\\), which is false for every \\(n\\ge3\\). The contradiction shows that the assumption “\\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a non‑sunny line” is impossible.\n\nTherefore any configuration that covers \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n\\) lines must be of one of the two types already exhibited:\n\n* all lines are non‑sunny (the horizontal family, giving \\(k=0\\));\n* all lines are sunny (the family \\(\\ell_i\\), giving \\(k=n\\)).\n\nNo configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) exists.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final Answer  \n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
