1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}R_b,\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Two explicit families of lines  \n\n#### 2.1  All lines non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the set of points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\) is exactly \\(R_b\\); thus \\(L_b\\) contains \\(R_b\\).  \nThe union \\(\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}L_b\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  \nAll \\(L_b\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none is sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(0<\\dfrac{i}{i+1}<1\\) and \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\neq -1\\), every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny; the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Then \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n0<\\frac{b}{a}\\le\\frac{n}{n+1}<1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)) form a strictly increasing sequence from \\(\\frac12\\) to \\(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\).  \nConsequently there exists a unique integer \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}\\le\\frac{b}{a}<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (4) by \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a\\le b<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}a .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the two bounds in (5) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i+1}{i+2}a-\\frac{i}{i+1}a\n   =\\frac{a}{(i+1)(i+2)}<1\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\((i+1)(i+2)\\ge2\\).  \nSince \\(b\\) is an integer, the only integer that can satisfy (5) is the lower bound; therefore  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i+1}a .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\). This yields a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line and at least one non‑sunny line. We shall reach a contradiction.\n\n#### 3.1  Horizontal lines\n\nIf a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(c\\ge2\\), then it does **not** meet the first row \\(R_1\\).  \nAll remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑horizontal; each such line can meet \\(R_1\\) in at most one point (a non‑horizontal line has a unique intersection with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)).  \nHence at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(R_1\\) could be covered, but \\(|R_1|=n\\).  \nTherefore any horizontal line present must be \\(y=1\\). Consequently there is **at most one** horizontal line in \\(\\mathcal L\\), and if it exists it is \\(y=1\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Behaviour of the other two types of non‑sunny lines  \n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\): it meets row \\(R_b\\) iff \\(c\\le n+1-b\\); in particular it meets every row except possibly the last one it can reach.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line* \\(x+y=c\\): it meets row \\(R_b\\) iff \\(1\\le c-b\\le n+1-b\\); again it meets every row except possibly one.\n\nThus any non‑horizontal, non‑sunny line contributes **at most one** point to each row.\n\n#### 3.3  Row 1\n\nRow \\(R_1\\) consists of the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).\n\n* If the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is present, it already covers all of \\(R_1\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines may intersect \\(R_1\\) arbitrarily, but this does not affect the covering of \\(R_1\\).\n\n* If the horizontal line is absent, each of the \\(n\\) non‑horizontal lines meets \\(R_1\\) in exactly one point, so the \\(n\\) points of \\(R_1\\) are still covered.\n\nHence **every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets \\(R_1\\)** (the horizontal line meets it in all its points, the others meet it in a single point).\n\n#### 3.4  Row 2\n\nRow \\(R_2\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\).\n\nThe horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (if it exists) does **not** meet \\(R_2\\).  \nAmong the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines:\n\n* A vertical line misses \\(R_2\\) exactly when it is \\(x=n\\) (because \\(x=n\\) gives the point \\((n,2)\\), which is not in \\(R_2\\)).\n* A slope \\(-1\\) line misses \\(R_2\\) exactly when it is \\(x+y=2\\), i.e. the line through \\((1,1)\\); this line meets \\(R_2\\) at \\((0,2)\\), which is not a point of the grid.\n\nAll other non‑horizontal, non‑sunny lines intersect \\(R_2\\) in exactly one point.  \nTherefore **at most two** of the \\(n\\) lines can fail to intersect \\(R_2\\). Consequently at least \\(n-2\\) lines intersect \\(R_2\\).\n\nSince each such line contributes at most one point to \\(R_2\\), the total number of points of \\(R_2\\) that can be covered is at most \\(n-2\\).  \nBut \\(|R_2|=n-1\\). Hence we would need \\(n-2\\ge n-1\\), which is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThis contradiction shows that a covering family cannot contain any non‑sunny line **unless** all lines are horizontal (the construction of Section 2.1). Consequently a mixed family with both sunny and non‑sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final Answer  \n\nThe two constructions exhibit that \\(k=0\\) (all lines horizontal) and \\(k=n\\) (the family \\(\\ell_i\\)) are attainable.  \nThe argument above proves that any intermediate value \\(0<k<n\\) leads to a contradiction.  \n\nHence the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n } .\n\\]"
