al lines  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the set of points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\) is exactly \\(R_b\\); thus \\(L_b\\) contains \\(R_b\\).  \nThe union \\(\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}L_b\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  \nAll \\(L_b\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none is sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(0<\\dfrac{i}{i+1}<1\\) and \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\neq -1\\), every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny; the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Then \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n0<\\frac{b}{a}\\le\\frac{n}{n+1}<1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)) form a strictly increasing sequence from \\(\\frac12\\) to \\(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\).  \nConsequently there exists a unique integer \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}\\le\\frac{b}{a}<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (4) by \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a\\le b<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}a . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the two bounds in (5) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i+1}{i+2}a-\\frac{i}{i+1}a\n   =\\frac{a}{(i+1)(i+2)}<1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) is an integer, the only integer satisfying (5) is the lower bound; therefore  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{i+1}a .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\). This yields a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line and at least one non‑sunny line. We shall reach a contradiction.\n\n#### 3.1  Horizontal lines\n\nIf a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(c\\ge2\\), then it does **not** meet the first row \\(R_1\\).  \nAll remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑horizontal; each such line can intersect the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point, so at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(R_1\\) could be covered, contradicting \\(|R_1|=n\\).  \nHence any horizontal line present must be \\(y=1\\), and there can be **at most one** horizontal line in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Non‑horizontal, non‑sunny lines must intersect every row\n\nA non‑sunny, non‑horizontal line has slope either \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\).  \nIt meets row \\(R_b\\) at \\((c,b)\\) provided \\(c\\le n+1-b\\); in particular it meets \\(R_1\\) for every integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line* \\(x+y=d\\).  \nIt meets row \\(R_b\\) at \\((d-b,b)\\) provided \\(1\\le d-b\\le n+1-b\\); for \\(b=1\\) this requires \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).\n\nIf a vertical line had \\(c>n\\) or a slope \\(-1\\) line had \\(d\\notin[2,n+1]\\), then it would miss **all** rows \\(R_b\\) (its intersection with each row would lie outside the allowed column range). Such a line would contribute no point of \\(T_n\\) and therefore cannot belong to a covering family. Consequently every non‑horizontal, non‑sunny line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n1\\le c\\le n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2\\le d\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nand in particular **each of them meets the first row \\(R_1\\)**.\n\n#### 3.3  Row 2\n\nRow \\(R_2\\) consists of the points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\); thus \\(|R_2|=n-1\\).\n\nThe horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (if present) does not intersect \\(R_2\\).\n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(R_2\\) exactly when \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\); it misses \\(R_2\\) only for the single value \\(c=n\\).\n\nA slope \\(-1\\) line \\(x+y=d\\) meets \\(R_2\\) at \\((d-2,2)\\). This point lies in the allowed column range \\(1\\le d-2\\le n-1\\) precisely when \\(3\\le d\\le n+1\\). Hence the two exceptional values \\(d=2\\) (the