            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}R_b,\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Two explicit families  \n\n#### 2.1  All lines non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) the set of points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\) is exactly \\(R_b\\); thus \\(L_b\\) contains \\(R_b\\).  \nThe union \\(\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}L_b\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  \nAll \\(L_b\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none is sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x-i+1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\ni:=a-b+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\n(the inequalities \\(a-b+1\\ge1\\) and \\(a-b+1\\le n\\) follow from \\(a\\ge b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nMoreover \\(b = a-i+1\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\). This yields a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No configuration with \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line and at least one non‑sunny line. We shall reach a contradiction.\n\n#### 3.1  Horizontal lines\n\nIf a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(c\\ge2\\), then it does **not** meet the first row \\(R_1\\).  \nAll remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are non‑horizontal; each such line can intersect the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point, so at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(R_1\\) could be covered, contradicting \\(|R_1|=n\\).  \nHence any horizontal line present must be \\(y=1\\), and there can be **at most one** horizontal line in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Non‑sunny, non‑horizontal lines\n\nA non‑sunny, non‑horizontal line has slope either \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\).  \nTo intersect the first row \\(R_1=\\{(1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\\) we need \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line* \\(x+y=d\\).  \nIts intersection with \\(R_1\\) is \\((d-1,1)\\); to lie inside \\(R_1\\) we need \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).\n\nIf a vertical line had \\(c>n\\) or a slope \\(-1\\) line had \\(d\\notin[2,n+1]\\), then it would miss **every** row \\(R_b\\) (its intersection with each row would lie outside the allowed column range). Such a line would contribute no point of \\(T_n\\) and therefore cannot belong to a covering family. Consequently every non‑horizontal, non‑sunny line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the above inequalities and **meets the first row \\(R_1\\)**.\n\n#### 3.3  Row 2\n\nRow \\(R_2\\) consists of the points \\((1,2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\); thus \\(|R_2|=n-1\\).\n\nThe horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (if present) does not intersect \\(R_2\\).\n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(R_2\\) exactly when \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\); it misses \\(R_2\\) only for the single value \\(c=n\\).\n\nA slope \\(-1\\) line \\(x+y=d\\) meets \\(R_2\\) at \\((d-2,2)\\). This point lies in the allowed column range \\(1\\le d-2\\le n-1\\) precisely when \\(3\\le d\\le n+1\\). Hence the two exceptional values \\(d=2\\) (the line \\(x+y=2\\)) and \\(d=n+2\\) (the line \\(x+y=n+2\\)) miss \\(R_2\\).\n\nTherefore **at most three** of the \\(n\\) lines can fail to intersect \\(R_2\\):\n\\[\nx=n,\\qquad x+y=2,\\qquad x+y=n+2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery line that does intersect \\(R_2\\) contributes **exactly one** point of \\(R_2\\): a non‑horizontal line meets a horizontal row in a single point, and the horizontal line contributes none. Hence the number of points of \\(R_2\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn-\\bigl(\\text{number of lines missing }R_2\\bigr)\\le n-3 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|R_2|=n-1\\), (4) would require \\(n-3\\ge n-1\\), i.e. \\(-3\\ge -1\\), which is false for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThus a covering family cannot contain a non‑sunny line **unless** the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is absent and *all* lines are sunny.\n\n#### 3.4  Consequences\n\n*If a horizontal line is present.*  \nThe argument above shows that any non‑horizontal line would miss \\(R_2\\) (it would be one of the three exceptional lines), contradicting the necessity to cover all points of \\(R_2\\). Hence the only possible covering family containing a horizontal line is the one consisting solely of the \\(n\\) horizontal lines of Section 2.1, which yields \\(k=0\\).\n\n*If no horizontal line is present.*  \nThen, as shown, the presence of any non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction; therefore all \\(n\\) lines must be sunny. The construction of Section 2.2 provides 