a covering of the same set with \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n* Using Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f2 provides the existence for the extreme value \\(k=n-2\\).  \n* Lemma\u202f3 together with the induction hypothesis yields the existence for\n  every intermediate \\(k\\).\n\nThus the admissible values are exactly the integers \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\n(and from \\(-1\\)).  Otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\nWe shall work with families \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines such that  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq T_n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) let \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\) be the\nnumber of sunny lines in it.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfies (1), then }\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is\nnon\u2011sunny.  Then at least \\(n-1\\) lines have slopes belonging to the set\n\\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  Two distinct sunny lines intersect\nin a unique point; consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines determine at most\n\\(\\binom{n-1}{2}\\) intersection points.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on\na sunny line must be either an intersection point of two sunny lines or a\npoint where a sunny line meets a non\u2011sunny line.\n\nIf there is **no** non\u2011sunny line, each sunny line can contain at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise two of those points would have the same\n\\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be horizontal or vertical,\ncontradiction).  Hence the total number of covered points is at most\n\\((n-1)n<|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), impossible.\n\nIf there is exactly **one** non\u2011sunny line, say a horizontal line\n\\(y=c\\), then all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(b=c\\) are covered by that line.\nAll remaining points have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, so each of them must\nlie on a different sunny line.  Thus we would need at least\n\\(|T_n|-|\\,\\{(a,c)\\in T_n\\}\\,|\\ge n\\) sunny lines, contradicting the\nassumption that only \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk=\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese two lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) that have either coordinate\nequal to \\(1\\).  The remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); they form a smaller triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\) shifted by the\nvector \\((1,1)\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = \\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), and the\nslopes are all distinct.  A direct substitution shows that the line\n\\(L_t\\) passes through the lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\) and\n\\(a,b\\ge2\\).  Hence the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).  The family contains exactly two\nnon\u2011sunny lines, therefore \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nThus the upper bound (2) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{Let }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfy (1) for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=s<m-1.\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Then there exists a family }\\mathcal L'\\text{ for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=s+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(s<m-1\\), at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny.\nPick a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).\nAll points of \\(T_m\\) that lie on \\(L\\) have a common coordinate\n(either \\(x\\), \\(y\\) or \\(x+y\\)).  Remove \\(L\\) from the family; the points\nthat were covered only by \\(L\\) now become uncovered.\n\nChoose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) and different\nfrom the slopes of the remaining sunny lines.  Because the set of\nuncovered points is a subset of a single horizontal (or vertical, or\nanti\u2011diagonal) line, they are collinear; hence there exists a line\n\\(L'\\) of slope \\(\\alpha\\) passing through **all** of them.  Adding \\(L'\\)\nrestores condition (1).  Since \\(\\alpha\\) is a sunny slope, the number of\nsunny lines has increased by one. \u220e\n\n\n\nLemma\u202f3 shows that, starting from a configuration with the minimal\npossible number of sunny lines, we can raise the count step by step up to\nthe maximal value \\(n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove the statement:\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n> \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n> (1) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  The following families work:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\}\\) (all three lines are non\u2011sunny);\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\) (the last line is sunny).\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the claim true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Let \\(k\\) be an integer with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\le n-2\\):*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there is a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\(T_n\\)\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Append to \\(\\mathcal L\\) the horizontal\nline \\(y=n+1\\); this new line is non\u2011sunny and covers the additional points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) (which are precisely the points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus\nT_n\\)).  The resulting family has \\(n+1\\) lines and still exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines, establishing the claim for \\(n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\):*  \nWe need a configuration for \\(T_{n+1}\\) with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines,\ni.e. with exactly one non\u2011sunny line.  Start from the configuration\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) given by Lemma\u202f2; it has \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nApply Lemma\u202f3 to replace one of its two non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line\nof a new slope.  The number of sunny lines becomes \\(n-1\\), and the\nfamily still covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).  Hence the claim holds for the maximal\nvalue \\(k=n-1\\) as well.\n\nThus the claim is true for \\(n+1\\).  By induction it holds for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1 (which gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\))\nwith the construction above (which shows that every integer in this\nrange is attainable) we obtain the complete description of the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
