            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nFor a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of distinct lines define  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=\\#\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\supseteq T_n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – at least two non‑sunny lines are unavoidable  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2\\quad\\text{for any }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfying (1)}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, to the contrary, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is\nnon‑sunny; then at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Case 1: no non‑sunny line.*  \nTwo distinct sunny lines intersect in a single point.  Hence the\n\\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(\\binom{n}{2}+n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n(the intersection points plus at most one extra point on each line).\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{2}+n = \\frac{n(n-1)}2+n = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 < |T_n|\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (the triangular number).  Thus (1) cannot\nhold.\n\n*Case 2: exactly one non‑sunny line, say \\(L\\).*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) share a common coordinate\n(\\(x\\), \\(y\\) or \\(x+y\\)).  The remaining points have pairwise distinct\nvalues of that coordinate, so each of them must lie on a different sunny\nline.  Consequently we need at least \\(|T_n|-|L\\cap T_n|\\ge n\\) sunny\nlines, contradicting the assumption that only \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nBoth cases lead to a contradiction; therefore at least two lines must be\nnon‑sunny, i.e. \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate equals\n\\(1\\).  The remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); they form the translated set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a-1,b-1)\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\;a,b\\ge2\\}=T_{\\,n-2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = \\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), and the\nslopes are pairwise distinct.  Substituting \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a+b=t+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge2\\) shows that \\(L_t\\) passes through all points\n\\((a,b)\\) of that diagonal.  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).  It contains exactly two non‑sunny lines,\nso \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma 3 – increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{Let }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfy (1) for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=s<m-1.\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Then there exists }\\mathcal L'\\text{ for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=s+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(s<m-1\\), at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non‑sunny.\nChoose one such line \\(L\\); without loss of generality suppose \\(L\\) is\nhorizontal, i.e. \\(L:\\;y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\).\n\nRemove \\(L\\) from the family.  The points of \\(T_m\\) that were covered\nonly by \\(L\\) are precisely those with \\(y=c\\) and \\(x\\ge1\\) satisfying\n\\(x+c\\le m+1\\); they lie on a single horizontal segment.\n\nPick a slope \\(\\alpha\\) that is sunny and different from the slopes of\nall remaining sunny lines (there are infinitely many such slopes).  There\nexists a unique line \\(L'\\) of slope \\(\\alpha\\) passing through the\nleftmost uncovered point; because all uncovered points share the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, the line \\(L'\\) actually passes through **all** of them.\nAdding \\(L'\\) restores condition (1).  Since \\(L'\\) is sunny, the number\nof sunny lines has increased by one: \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=s+1\\).\n∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove the following claim.\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n> \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n> (1) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  The families  \n\n\\[\n\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\}\\quad(k=0),\\qquad\n\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\quad(k=1)\n\\]\n\nfulfil (1).  By Lemma 1 we cannot have \\(k\\ge2\\); thus the claim holds for\n\\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the claim true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Let \\(k\\) be an integer with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\le n-2\\):*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there is a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\(T_n\\)\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Add the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\); it\ncovers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\) (those with \\(a+b=n+2\\))\nand is non‑sunny.  The new family has \\(n+1\\) lines and still exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines, giving a configuration for \\(n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\):*  \nFor \\(n+1\\) the maximal allowed number of sunny lines is \\((n+1)-2=n-1\\).\nLemma 2 provides a configuration for \\(T_{n+1}\\) with \\(n-1\\) sunny\nlines (two non‑sunny lines).  Applying Lemma 3 to replace one of those\nnon‑sunny lines by a sunny line of a new slope yields a configuration\nwith exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Hence the maximal value is also\nrealizable.\n\nThus for \\(n+1\\) we have constructed configurations for every\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2\\).  The induction is complete.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\le n-2\\).  Lemma 2 gives a construction attaining the extreme value\n\\(k=n-2\\).  Lemma 3 together with the induction argument produces\nconfigurations for every intermediate value down to \\(k=0\\).  Therefore\nthe set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
