) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ is horizontal}\\},\\qquad \nv=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ is vertical}\\},\\qquad \nd=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ has slope }-1\\},\n\\]\n\nso that \\(h+v+d=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points that can be covered by horizontal lines\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains the lattice points \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\).  \nThus the number of points of the triangle \\(T_n\\) lying on this line equals\n\\[\n\\max\\{0,\\;n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\nSince the constants \\(c\\) for distinct horizontal lines are distinct,\nthe total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by all horizontal lines is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{c\\in C_h}\\bigl(n+1-c\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_h\\) is a set of \\(h\\) distinct positive integers.\nThe sum is maximised when the chosen constants are the smallest possible,\nnamely \\(c=1,2,\\dots ,h\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(T_n\\cap\\bigcup_{\\text{horizontal}}\\ell_i\\bigr)\n\\le \\sum_{c=1}^{h}(n+1-c)=h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that can be covered by vertical lines\n\nA completely symmetric argument gives, for the \\(v\\) vertical lines,\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(T_n\\cap\\bigcup_{\\text{vertical}}\\ell_i\\bigr)\n\\le v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points that can be covered by lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nA line \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(c\\ge 2\\) because both coordinates are positive) meets\n\\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,b)\\) satisfying\n\\(a+b=c\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \nHence such a line contains exactly one point of \\(T_n\\) for each integer\n\\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nIf we have \\(d\\) distinct lines of this type, their constants are distinct,\nso at most \\(d\\) points of \\(T_n\\) are covered:\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(T_n\\cap\\bigcup_{\\text{slope }-1}\\ell_i\\bigr)\\le d. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Total number of covered points\n\nAdding the three estimates (1)\u2013(3) we obtain an upper bound for the total\nnumber of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{\\text{cov}} &\\le\nh(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n\\;+\\;\nv(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}\n\\;+\\; d \\\\[2mm]\n&= (h+v)(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}+d .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(h+v+d=n\\).  Substituting \\(d=n-(h+v)\\) into (4) yields\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\n\\le (h+v)(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}+n-(h+v)\n= n(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Comparison with the required number of points\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains exactly\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\nlattice points.  \nFrom (5) we have\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\le n(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n-\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) are non\u2011negative integers, the quantity\n\\(\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}\\) is at least \\(\\frac{h+v}{2}\\).  Moreover,\n\\(h+v\\le n\\) (because \\(d=n-(h+v)\\ge 0\\)).  Hence\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\le \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+ \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n-\\frac{h+v}{2}\n< \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+ \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n= n(n+1).\n\\]\n\nBut the required number of points to be covered is \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\nA direct computation shows that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every\npair \\((h,v)\\) with \\(h+v\\le n\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n-\n\\Bigl[n(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}\\Bigr]\n=\n\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}{2}\n>0 .\n\\]\nThus \\(N_{\\text{cov}}<|T_n|\\); i.e. the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines cannot cover all\npoints of the triangle \\(T_n\\).\n\nThis contradicts the hypothesis that the given \\(n\\) lines cover every point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Therefore our assumption that **all** lines are\nnon\u2011sunny is false.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nAt least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.  Consequently, the value\n\\(k=0\\) is impossible for any admissible configuration.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No configuration with }k=0\\text{ exists.}}\n\\]\n\n*The determination of the remaining admissible values of \\(k\\) is left open.*"
