ding (1)–(3) and using \\(d=n-(h+v)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{\\text{cov}}\n&\\le h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2\n   +v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n   +n-(h+v)\\\\[1mm]\n&= n(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(h,v\\ge0\\) and at least one of them is positive (otherwise all lines\nwould be of slope \\(-1\\) and could cover at most \\(n\\) points, far fewer than\n\\(|T_n|\\)), the term \\(\\frac{h(h+1)+v(v+1)}2\\) is at least \\(1\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{cov}}\\le n(n+1)-1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |T_n|\n\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Hence a covering family must contain at least one sunny\nline.  Therefore **\\(k=0\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  We shall build \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nConsider the points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;n+1-j)\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThese points lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  We modify this line\nas follows.\n\n* For \\(j=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) choose a sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\) passing through\n  \\(P_j\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n  This is possible because the set of slopes different from\n  \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) is dense; picking a slope very close to \\(-1\\) but not\n  equal to \\(-1\\) makes the line intersect the integer lattice only at the\n  prescribed point \\(P_j\\).\n\n* Let \\(\\ell_k\\) be a sunny line passing through the remaining points\n  \\(P_k,P_{k+1},\\dots ,P_n\\).  The points \\(P_k,\\dots ,P_n\\) are collinear on\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\); we rotate the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) by a tiny angle \\(\\varepsilon\\)\n  (with \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\) and small enough) to obtain a line of slope\n  \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\).  Because the rotation is infinitesimal, the line still\n  passes through all integer points \\(P_k,\\dots ,P_n\\).  Its slope is not\n  \\(-1\\), nor \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\); hence \\(\\ell_k\\) is sunny.\n\nThus we have constructed exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-k\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nm_t:\\;x=t .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and each\ncontains all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(t\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Verification of the covering property\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(m_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\)*, write \\(a=j\\) with \\(j\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThen \\(b=n+1-j\\) and the point is precisely \\(P_j\\).  By construction\n\\(P_j\\) belongs to one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{j-(n-k)}\\) (for\n\\(j\\le k-1\\)) or to \\(\\ell_k\\) (for \\(j\\ge k\\)).  Hence the point is covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n#### 2.4  Distinctness of the lines\n\nThe vertical lines \\(m_t\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts, so they are\ndifferent.  The sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) have distinct sets of\nlattice points of \\(T_n\\) (each of \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) contains a\nunique point \\(P_j\\), while \\(\\ell_k\\) contains the remaining points), hence\nthey are distinct from each other and also from any vertical line.  Therefore\nthe whole family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nConsequently, for every \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a configuration satisfying the\ntwo conditions of the problem exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\n* No configuration exists with \\(k=0\\) (proved in §1).\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists\n  (constructed in §2).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
