ne \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three forbidden directions are exactly the directions of the three sides of the convex hull \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S)\\), which is the triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) intersected \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S)\\) in three distinct lattice points, those three points would be collinear and the line through them would be parallel to one side of the triangle (a standard fact about lattice triangles).  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any side, such a triple cannot exist.  Hence at most two lattice points of \\(S\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  \nLet  \n\n* \\(k\\) = number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(t=n-k\\) = number of non\u2011sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line of the three allowed directions can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\):  \n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the set \\(S\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points;\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous;\n* a line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(S\\) in the points with \\(a+b=c\\), whose number is \\(c-1\\le n\\).\n\nConsequently the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(n\\,t\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(S\\) must lie on at least one line, we have\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le 2k+n\\,t = 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}+2k-nk .\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) and simplifying gives the necessary inequality\n\n\\[\n2k\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \nk\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}4\\Big\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissi