\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  \nLet  \n\n* \\(k\\) = number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(t=n-k\\) = number of non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S\\); thus the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\):\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(S\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), at most \\(n\\) of them;\n* a vertical line is analogous;\n* a line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(S\\) in the points with \\(a+b=c\\), whose number is \\(c-1\\le n\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(n\\,t\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(S\\) must lie on at least one line, we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le 2k+n\\,t = 2k+n(n-k).\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) and simplifying gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+n^{2}-nk\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n2k\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}4\\Big\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}4\\big\\rfloor\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Order the points of \\(S\\) as  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,n),\\;P_{2}=(2,n-1),\\;P_{3}=(3,n-2),\\dots ,P_{n}=(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) the points \\(P_{2i-1}\\) and \\(P_{2i}\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and different sums, so the line through them is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. it is sunny.  \n   Take the first \\(2k\\) points of the list and pair them consecutively:\n   \\[\n   (P_{1},P_{2}),\\;(P_{3},P_{4}),\\dots ,(P_{2k-1},P_{2k}).\n   \\]\n   Draw the \\(k\\) sunny lines determined by these pairs.  \n   Each such line contains exactly the two points of its pair, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Covering the remaining points.**  \n   The points of \\(S\\) not used in step 1 are \\(|S|-2k\\) in number.  \n   Partition them according to their \\(y\\)-coordinate: for each \\(b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(L_{b}\\) be the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   All points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate lie on the same \\(L_{b}\\); there are at most \\(n\\) points on any such line, so each \\(L_{b}\\) is a non‑sunny line that can be used.\n\n   Because of (2) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|-2k\\le n\\bigl(n-k\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   which guarantees that the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-k}\\) are sufficient to cover all the remaining points.  \n   (If fewer than \\(n-k\\) of the \\(L_{b}\\) are needed, we may add any extra non‑sunny lines that avoid all points of \\(S\\) in order to reach exactly \\(n\\) lines; this does not affect the covering property.)\n\n3. **Counting the lines.**  \n   We have used exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. at most \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   By discarding any unused non‑sunny lines (or, if necessary, adding harmless extra ones) we obtain a family of **exactly** \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe inequality (2) is necessary, and the construction above shows it is sufficient.  \nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n(n+1)}{4}\\Big\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **sum