            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. its slope belongs to\n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  All other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) required lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny and\n\\(n-k\\) are non‑sunny.  To maximise the number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered\nby the non‑sunny lines we may choose them to be horizontal, because a\nhorizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains \\(\\#\\{a\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n\\}=n+1-b\\) points,\nwhich is not smaller than the number obtained with a vertical line\nor a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(m:=n-k\\) lines are distinct and cover  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_m&=\\sum_{b=1}^{m}(n+1-b)\\\\\n   &=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence the number of points of \\(T_n\\) still uncovered is  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{n,k}&=|T_n|-C_{n-k}\\\\\n       &=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n         -\\Bigl[(n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}\\Bigr]  \\\\\n       &=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line in reduced form \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\((p,q)\\notin\\{(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\}\\).\nIf the line contains two lattice points \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) then\n\\(x_2-x_1\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\) and \\(y_2-y_1\\) a multiple of \\(p\\).\nConsequently any third lattice point on the same line would be at distance\n\\(|q|\\ge 1\\) in the \\(x\\)-direction from the second one.\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le n\\); therefore a\nthird lattice point would force either \\(x>n\\) or \\(y>n\\) or\n\\(x+y>n+1\\), contradicting the definition of \\(T_n\\).\n∎\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) of the points counted in\n\\(R_{n,k}\\).  From (5) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le 2k .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k>0\\) this simplifies to \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{k+1}{2}\\le 2\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3 .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(k=0\\) the inequality is trivially satisfied.  Hence **no configuration\nwith \\(k\\ge 4\\) sunny lines exists**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are all non‑sunny.\n  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the point lies on\n  the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point is \\((1,n)\\).  Add any sunny line through this\n  point, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell:\\; y=n-\\frac{x-1}{2}\\qquad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12).\n  \\]\n\n  Exactly one line is sunny and all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,n-1),(1,n),(2,n-1)\\).  \n  Cover \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) with the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\; y=x+n-2\\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\n  \\]\n\n  and cover \\((1,n)\\) with a second sunny line, e.g.  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_2:\\; y=n-\\frac{x-1}{2}\\qquad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12).\n  \\]\n\n  Thus we have exactly two sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\).  \n  The six points not yet covered are  \n\n  \\[\n  (1,n-2),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(1,n),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(3,n-2).\n  \\]\n\n  Pair them as  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: (1,n-2)\\text{ and }(2,n-1),\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: (1,n-1)\\text{ and }(3,n-2),\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: (1,n)\\text{   and }(2,n-2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  The corresponding lines are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y = x + n-3 \\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + n-\\tfrac12 \\qquad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + n+2 \\qquad(\\text{slope }-2),\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  none of which has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence all three are sunny.\n  Together with the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition derived in § 1 forces \\(k\\le 3\\); the explicit\nconstructions of § 2 show that each \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) is attainable for every\ninteger \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader.