mathcal L\\text{ satisfy (1) for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=s<m-1.\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Then there exists }\\mathcal L'\\text{ for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=s+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(s<m-1\\), at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non‑sunny.\nChoose one such line \\(L\\); without loss of generality suppose \\(L\\) is\nhorizontal, i.e. \\(L:\\;y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\).\n\nRemove \\(L\\) from the family.  The points of \\(T_m\\) that were covered\nonly by \\(L\\) are precisely those with \\(y=c\\) and \\(x\\ge1\\) satisfying\n\\(x+c\\le m+1\\); they lie on a single horizontal segment.\n\nPick a slope \\(\\alpha\\) that is sunny and different from the slopes of\nall remaining sunny lines (there are infinitely many such slopes).  There\nexists a unique line \\(L'\\) of slope \\(\\alpha\\) passing through the\nleftmost uncovered point; because all uncovered points share the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, the line \\(L'\\) actually passes through **all** of them.\nAdding \\(L'\\) restores condition (1).  Since \\(L'\\) is sunny, the number\nof sunny lines has increased by one: \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=s+1\\).\n∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove the following claim.\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n> \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n> (1) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  The families  \n\n\\[\n\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\}\\quad(k=0),\\qquad\n\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\quad(k=1)\n\\]\n\nfulfil (1).  By Lemma 1 we cannot have \\(k\\ge2\\); thus the claim holds for\n\\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the claim true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Let \\(k\\) be an integer with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\le n-2\\):*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there is a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\(T_n\\)\nwith exa