"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the flaw in the original argument and now present a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds in three stages.\n\n1. **Upper bound (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   Using a counting argument based on the intersections of sunny lines we show that any covering of the required point set by \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain **at least two non\u2011sunny lines**; consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Existence of the maximal value (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   An explicit construction uses the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) together with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes; this yields a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Realising all intermediate values (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Starting from any covering with \\(s<n-2\\) sunny lines, we replace one of the non\u2011sunny lines by a newly chosen sunny line that passes through all points that became uncovered. This increases the sunny count by one while preserving the covering property. Repeated application produces configurations with every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\).\n\nInduction on \\(n\\) shows that the same set of admissible \\(k\\) works for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nFor a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of distinct lines set  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=\\#\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\supseteq T_n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2\\quad\\text{for any }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfying (1)}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny; then at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nLet the sunny lines be \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\).  \nTwo distinct sunny lines have distinct slopes, hence they intersect in exactly one point.  \nAll intersection points among the sunny lines are distinct (otherwise three lines would meet at a common point, forcing two of them to be parallel, contradicting distinct slopes).  \nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I=\\{S_i\\cap S_j\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality \\(\\binom{n-1}{2}\\).\n\nEach sunny line also contains points of \\(T_n\\) that are not intersection points; denote by \\(p_i\\) a point of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(S_i\\) but on no other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (such a point exists because a line covering only intersection points would be completely determined by those points, contradicting distinctness of the lines).  \nThe points \\(p_1,\\dots ,p_{n-1}\\) are all different and none belongs to \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal I|+(n-1)=\\binom{n-1}{2}+ (n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2+(n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf there is also a non\u2011sunny line, it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) meets each diagonal \\(a+b=t\\) in at most one point, and there are \\(n\\) diagonals).  \nHence the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}2+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (4) would require that every diagonal \\(a+b=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\)) contain **exactly** one point of the non\u2011sunny line and all remaining points be intersection points of sunny lines.  \nBut the non\u2011sunny line can intersect a given diagonal in at most one point, so it would have to contain a point on **every** diagonal, i.e. \\(n\\) distinct points.  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) cannot meet all \\(n\\) diagonals (the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) misses the diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) when \\(c>n\\), etc.).  \nThus the bound (4) is **strictly** smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nTherefore the assumption that at most one line is non\u2011sunny leads to a contradiction.  \nHence any family satisfying (1) must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate equals \\(1\\).  \nThe remaining points are those with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); after translating by \\((-1,-1)\\) they form the set \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), and the slopes are pairwise distinct, so each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\), then substituting \\(a,b\\) into the equation of \\(L_t\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).  It contains exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, so  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{Let }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfy (1) for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=s<m-1.\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Then there exists }\\mathcal L'\\text{ for }T_m\\text{ with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=s+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(s<m-1\\), Lemma\u202f1 guarantees that at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nChoose one such line, say \\(L\\); without loss of generality let it be horizontal,\n\\(L:\\;y=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\).\n\nRemove \\(L\\) from the family.  The points of \\(T_m\\) that were covered **only**\nby \\(L\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(x,c)\\mid x\\ge1,\\;x+c\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(U\\) lie on the same horizontal line, hence they share the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate.\n\nSelect a slope \\(\\alpha\\) that is sunny and different from the slopes of all\nremaining sunny lines (infinitely many such slopes exist).  \nThere is a unique line \\(L'\\) of slope \\(\\alpha\\) passing through the leftmost\npoint of \\(U\\); because every point of \\(U\\) has the same \\(y\\)-coordinate,\nthe line \\(L'\\) actually passes through **all** points of \\(U\\).  \nAdding \\(L'\\) restores condition (1).  Since \\(L'\\) is sunny, the number of\nsunny lines has increased by one:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=s+1.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove the following statement.\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n> \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n> (1) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe families  \n\n\\[\n\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\}\\quad(k=0),\\qquad\n\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\quad(k=1)\n\\]\n\nboth satisfy (1).  By Lemma\u202f1 we cannot have \\(k\\ge2\\).  Hence the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the claim true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Let \\(k\\) be an integer with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\le n-2\\):*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\(T_n\\)\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Add the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\); it is\nnon\u2011sunny and covers all newly introduced points \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\\).\nThe enlarged family has \\(n+1\\) lines and still exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,\nso a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same \\(k\\) exists.\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\):*  \nFor \\(n+1\\) the maximal admissible number of sunny lines is \\((n+1)-2=n-1\\).\nLemma\u202f2 provides a configuration for \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny\nlines (two non\u2011sunny lines).  Applying Lemma\u202f3 to replace one of those\nnon\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line of a new slope yields a configuration with\nexactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Thus the maximal value is also attainable.\n\nHence for \\(n+1\\) we have constructed configurations for every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2\\).  The induction is complete.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\le n-2\\).  Lemma\u202f2 gives a construction attaining the extreme value\n\\(k=n-2\\).  Lemma\u202f3 together with the induction argument produces\nconfigurations for every intermediate value down to \\(k=0\\).  Therefore the\nset of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\] \u220e"

