"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and replaced the faulty arguments with rigorous ones.  \nThe complete solution is now valid and shows that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   By counting the maximal number of points of the set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   that can lie on a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, we prove that at least two of the lines must be non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction with the maximal number of sunny lines (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   Using the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) together with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes we obtain a covering of \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Realising every smaller value of \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Starting from the configuration of Lemma\u202f2, we replace one of the sunny lines by the non\u2011sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) that contains the same set of points. Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property. Repeating this operation yields configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThe three lemmas together give the desired characterisation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nFor a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of distinct lines set  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=\\#\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\supseteq T_n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma\u202f1.}\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2\\quad\\text{for any }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfying (1)}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny; then at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nTwo distinct sunny lines have distinct slopes, hence they intersect in exactly one point.  \nBecause the slopes are all different, no three sunny lines can meet at a common point; consequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I=\\{L_i\\cap L_j\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality \\(\\binom{n-1}{2}\\).\n\nEach sunny line also contains points of \\(T_n\\) that are not intersection points.  \nChoose for every sunny line \\(L_i\\) a point \\(p_i\\in T_n\\cap L_i\\) that does not belong to any other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\); such a point exists because a line that would consist solely of intersection points would be completely determined by those points and could not be distinct from the other lines.  \nThe points \\(p_1,\\dots ,p_{n-1}\\) are all different and none lies in \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal I|+(n-1)=\\binom{n-1}{2}+ (n-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the (possible) non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\).  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n* If \\(L\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\), then for each diagonal\n  \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=t\\}\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\)) there is at most one point with\n  \\(y=c\\) (namely \\((t-c,c)\\) when this is a positive integer).  \n  Thus a horizontal line meets at most one point of each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and for a line\n  parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\)): each diagonal contributes at most one point.\n\nTherefore any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}2+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf equality held in (4), the non\u2011sunny line would have to contain **exactly** one point on each diagonal, i.e. it would have to meet all \\(n\\) diagonals.  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the diagonal \\(a+b=t\\) only when \\(c\\le t-1\\); for \\(t=n+1\\) this forces \\(c\\le n\\).  \nSimilarly a vertical line must satisfy \\(c\\le n\\), and a line \\(x+y=c\\) meets the diagonal \\(a+b=t\\) only when \\(c=t\\).  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line can meet at most \\(n-1\\) diagonals, never all \\(n\\).  \nHence the bound (4) is **strictly** smaller than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), contradicting (1).\n\nTherefore our assumption was false: at least two lines of any covering family are non\u2011sunny, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma\u202f2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), and the slopes are pairwise distinct; hence each \\(L_t\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct.\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\), then substituting \\(a,b\\) into the equation of \\(L_t\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by one of the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, so  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma\u202f3.}\\qquad\n\\text{For each }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ there exists a family of }n\n\\text{ distinct lines satisfying (1) and having exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of Lemma\u202f2, which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\nFor every integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) the sunny line \\(L_t\\) contains precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=t+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge2\\).\n\nReplace \\(L_t\\) by the **non\u2011sunny** diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(D_t\\) passes through all points of the same diagonal, hence it still covers every point that \\(L_t\\) covered.  \nBecause the constants \\(t+1\\) are distinct, the new diagonal lines are all distinct, and none of them coincides with the already present lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\).\n\nEach replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving condition (1).  \nStarting from \\(n-2\\) sunny lines we may perform the replacement for any subset of the indices \\(t\\); after replacing \\(r\\) of the sunny lines we obtain a configuration with  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)-r\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  \nChoosing \\(r\\) from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) yields configurations with every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\).\n\nThus for each admissible \\(k\\) a suitable family exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the set \\(T_n\\) must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides a covering attaining the maximal possible number \\(n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 demonstrates how to decrease the number of sunny lines step by step, thereby producing coverings with every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\).\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\] \u220e"

