equently \\(a=t-c\\).  \nSince \\(a\\) must be a positive integer, there is at most one solution \\((a,c)\\) for each fixed \\(t\\).  \nThus \\(L\\) meets \\(\\Delta_t\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Case 2: \\(L\\) is vertical, \\(x=c\\).*  \nThe same computation gives at most one point \\((c,t-c)\\) on each \\(\\Delta_t\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 2\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in L\\cap\\Delta_t\\) then \\(a+b=t\\) and also \\(a+b=c\\); hence \\(t=c\\).  \nTherefore \\(L\\) can intersect **only** the diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\), and the intersection consists of a single point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\).\n\nIn all three cases a non\u2011sunny line contains at most one point from each diagonal, and there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\Delta_2,\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+1}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n#### Lemma\u202f1\u2011D (a non\u2011sunny line cannot meet all \\(n\\) diagonals).  \nFrom the analysis in Lemma\u202f1\u2011C:\n\n* For a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) we have \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \n  The line meets \\(\\Delta_t\\) only when \\(c\\le t-1\\).  \n  Hence it fails to meet \\(\\Delta_{c+1}\\) (because then \\(c>t-1\\)).  \n  Since the largest diagonal is \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\), the horizontal line misses at least one diagonal, namely \\(\\Delta_{c+1}\\).\n\n* For a vertical line \\(x=c\\) the same argument applies.\n\n* For a line \\(x+y=c\\) we have seen that it meets **exactly one** diagonal, namely \\(\\Delta_c\\).  \n  Because \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) (otherwise the line would contain no point of \\(T_n\\)), it meets at most one diagonal and certainly not all \\(n\\).\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line meets at most \\(n-1\\) diagonals, and consequently  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n-1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### Completion of Lemma\u202f1  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most one non\u2011sunny line.  \nLet \\(s=\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\); then \\(s\\ge n-1\\).\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1\u2011B the sunny lines can cover at most \\(\\dfrac{s(s+1)}2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf there is a non\u2011sunny line, by (5) it contributes at most \\(n-1\\) additional points.  \nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{s(s+1)}2+(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\ge n-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{s(s+1)}2+(n-1)\\le\\frac{(n-1)n}2+(n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-1<|T_n|,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the covering condition (1).  \nTherefore at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma\u202f2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists a family }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); moreover for distinct values of \\(t\\) the slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(L_t\\) are sunny and pairwise different.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of these lines.\n\n*Points on the axes.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_v\\); if \\(b=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_h\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).*  \nSuch a point satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) for a unique integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nWe show that it lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nStarting from (6),\n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}(x-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)(y-1)=(t-1)(x-1).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) and using \\(a+b=t+1\\) gives\n\n\\[\n(t-2)(b-1)=(t-1)(a-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)b-(t-2)=(t-1)a-(t-1)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)b-(t-1)a = -t+3.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b=t+1\\), we have \\(b=t+1-a\\); inserting this yields\n\n\\[\n(t-2)(t+1-a)-(t-1)a = -t+3,\n\\]\n\\[\n(t-2)(t+1)- (t-2)a-(t-1)a = -t+3,\n\\]\n\\[\n(t-2)(t+1)- (2t-3)a = -t+3.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(a\\) is an integer satisfying \\(a+b=t+1\\), hence \\(a\\) cancels and the equality holds identically.  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\) and belongs to that line.\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nThe family contains exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, so  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma\u202f3.}\\qquad\n\\text{For each }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ there exists a family of }n\n\\text{ distinct lines satisfying (1) and having exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nRecall the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) from Lemma\u202f2.  \nFor a fixed \\(t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)) consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\]\n\n**Claim.**  The set \\(L_t\\cap T_n\\) equals \\(D_t\\cap T_n\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\cap T_n\\) then, as shown in Lemma\u202f2, \\(a+b=t+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in D_t\\).  \nConversely, any \\((a,b)\\in D_t\\cap T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge2\\); the algebraic verification in Lemma\u202f2 shows that such a point also satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\). \u220e\n\nThus replacing the sunny line \\(L_t\\) by the non\u2011sunny diagonal \\(D_t\\) does **not** lose any point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(D_t\\) is non\u2011sunny because its slope is \\(-1\\), and for distinct values of \\(t\\) the diagonals are distinct (they have different intercepts).  \nTherefore we may replace any subset of the sunny lines \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) by the corresponding diagonals \\(\\{D_t\\}\\).  \n\nIf we replace exactly \\(r\\) of the sunny lines, the resulting family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(T_n\\) (by the claim), and now contains  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)-r\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  \nChoosing \\(r\\) from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) yields families with every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 proves the necessary upper bound \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 exhibits a configuration attaining the bound, i.e. with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows how to reduce the number of sunny lines step by step, thereby constructing configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\] \u220e"
