ints.  \nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{s(s+1)}2+(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\ge n-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{s(s+1)}2+(n-1)\\le\\frac{(n-1)n}2+(n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-1<|T_n|,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the covering condition (1).  \nTherefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2.\n\\] ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists a family }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Construction.*  \nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); moreover for distinct values of \\(t\\) the slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(L_t\\) are sunny and pairwise different.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of these lines.\n\n*Points on the axes.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_v\\); if \\(b=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_h\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).*  \nSuch a point satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) for a unique integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nWe show that it lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nStarting from (6),\n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}(x-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)(y-1)=(t-1)(x-1).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) and using \\(a+b=t+1\\) gives\n\n\\[\n(t-2)(b-1)=(t-1)(a-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)b-(t-2)=(t-1)a-(t-1)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)b-(t-1)a = -t+3.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b=t+1\\), we have \\(b=t+1-a\\); inserting this yields\n\n\\[\n(t-2)(t+1-a)-(t-1)a = -t+3,\n\\]\n\\[\n(t-2)(t+1)- (t-2)a-(t-1)a 