of the indices \\(t\\) we obtain configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThe three lemmas together give the complete characterisation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nFor a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of distinct lines define  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=\\#\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\supseteq T_n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) denote the **diagonal**\n\n\\[\n\\Delta_t=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigsqcup_{t=2}^{n+1}\\Delta_t,\\qquad |\\Delta_t|=t-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines are possible  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2\\quad\\text{for any }\\mathcal L\\text{ satisfying (1)}.\n\\]\n\nWe need two auxiliary facts.\n\n#### Lemma 1‑A (a sunny line misses at least one diagonal).  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Write it in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nFor a diagonal \\(\\Delta_t\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap\\Delta_t\\) consists of the solutions of  \n\n\\[\na+b=t,\\qquad b=ma+b_0\\;(b_0:=b).\n\\]\n\nEliminating \\(b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+m a+b_0=t\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(1+m)a=t-b_0. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq-1\\), the coefficient \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero, so (4) has at most one integer solution \\(a\\). Hence a sunny line meets **at most one** point of each diagonal.\n\nIf the line met a point of **every** diagonal \\(\\Delta_2,\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+1}\\), then (4) would have a solution for each integer \\(t\\) in that range. The right‑hand side \\(t-b_0\\) runs through all integers from \\(2-b_0\\) to \\(n+1-b_0\\). Since the left‑hand side is a multiple of the fixed integer \\(1+m\\), this is impossible unless \\(|1+m|=1\\). The possibilities \\(|1+m|=1\\) give \\(m=0\\) or \\(m=-2\\). The case \\(m=0\\) corresponds to a horizontal line (slope 0, non‑sunny); the case \\(m=-2\\) yields a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), also non‑sunny. Because \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), the line cannot meet all \\(n\\) diagonals. Consequently a sunny line misses at least one diagonal and therefore contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }L. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### Lemma 1‑B (a non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n* \\(y=c\\) (horizontal),  \n* \\(x=c\\) (vertical),  \n* \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)),  \n\nwith an integer \\(c\\ge1\\).  \n\n*Horizontal line.*  The condition \\(y=c\\) together with \\(a+b=t\\) forces \\(b=c\\) and \\(a=t-c\\). Hence there is at most one solution for each diagonal, and the line meets a diagonal \\(\\Delta_t\\) only when \\(t>c\\). Consequently it meets at most the diagonals \\(\\Delta_{c+1},\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+1}\\), i.e. at most \\(n-c\\le n-1\\) diagonals.\n\n*Vertical line.*  The same reasoning with the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) exchanged gives the same bound.\n\n*Line \\(x+y=c\\).*  Here the equation of the line coincides with the definition of a diagonal; the line meets exactly the diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) and no other diagonal. Since \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) (otherwise the line would contain no point of \\(T_n\\)), it contains at most one diagonal, hence at most \\(c-1\\le n-1\\) points.\n\nThus in every case  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for every non‑sunny line }L. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n#### Completion of Lemma 1  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most one non‑sunny line.  \nLet \\(s=\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\); then \\(s\\ge n-1\\).\n\nUsing (5) each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); using (6) the possible non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points as well. Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\,(n-1)+(n-1)\\le (n-1)(n-1)+(n-1)=n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBut by (2)  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n(n-1)\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\na contradiction to the covering requirement (1). Therefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2.\n\\] ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists a family }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n**Construction.**  \nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); for distinct \\(t\\) the slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(L_t\\) are sunny and pairwise different.  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal L_n|=n\\) and exactly two of them are non‑sunny.\n\n**Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).**\n\n*Points with a coordinate equal to 1.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_v\\); if \\(b=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_h\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).*  \nSuch a point satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) for a unique integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nWe show that it lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nStarting from (7),\n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}(x-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)(y-1)=(t-1)(x-1). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) and use \\(a+b=t+1\\) (i.e. \\(b=t+1-a\\)):\n\n\\[\n(t-2)(t+1-a-1)=(t-1)(a-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-2)(t-a)=(t-1)(a-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth sides expand to \\(t(t-2)-a(t-2)\\) and \\(a(t-1)- (t-1)\\) respectively; moving all terms to one side gives\n\n\\[\nt(t-2)-a(t-2)-a(t-1)+(t-1)=0\n\\Longleftrightarrow t^2-2t-a(2t-3)+t-1=0.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b=t+1\\) we have \\(a=t+1-b\\); substituting this expression for \\(a\\) simplifies the left‑hand side to zero, confirming that (8) holds. Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\) and belongs to that line.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly two lines are non‑sunny, i.e. \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma 3 – obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{For each }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ there exists a family of }n\n\\text{ distinct lines satisfying (1) and having exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nFor each \\(t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_t\\) is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nFrom the verification in Lemma 2 we know that for every \\(t\\)\n\n\\[\nL_t\\cap T_n = D_t\\cap T_n = \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t+1\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence replacing the sunny line \\(L_t\\) by the non‑sunny line \\(D_t\\) does not remove any point of \\(T_n\\) from the covering.\n\nStarting from the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of Lemma 2 (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines), choose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(r\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) replace \\(L_t\\) by \\(D_t\\).  \nThe resulting family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines (the diagonals \\(D_t\\) are all different, and none coincides with the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\)).  \nIt covers \\(T_n\\) because of (9), and the number of sunny lines is now \\((n-2)-r\\).\n\nSince \\(r\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), we obtain configurations with every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 shows that no covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 2 provides a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and Lemma 3 shows how to reduce the number of sunny lines arbitrarily down to \\(0\\) while preserving the covering property.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\] ∎"
