in every case  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for every non‑sunny line }L. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n#### Completion of Lemma 1  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most one non‑sunny line.  \nLet \\(s=\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\); then \\(s\\ge n-1\\).\n\nUsing (5) each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); using (6) the possible non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points as well. Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\,(n-1)+(n-1)\\le (n-1)(n-1)+(n-1)=n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBut by (2)  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n(n-1)\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\na contradiction to the covering requirement (1). Therefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\le n-2.\n\\] ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ there exists a family }\\mathcal L_n\\text{ satisfying (1) with }\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n**Construction.**  \nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;\\text{the line through the points }(1,1)\\text{ and }(t,\\;n+2-t). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{n+2-2t}{t-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\ge2\\) we have \\(t-1\\neq0\\); moreover \\(m_t\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(n+2=2t\\), which would give \\(t=\\frac{n+2}{2}\\) and then the line would be horizontal, contradicting the explicit coordinates) and \\(m_t\\neq-1\\) (the equation \\(n+2-2t=-(t-1)\\) would give \\(n+1=t\\), impossible since \\(t\\le n-1\\)). Hence each \\(L_t\\) is sunny. Distinct values of \\(t\\) give distinct slopes, so the lines \\(L_t\\) are pairwise different.  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_h,L_v\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal L_n|=n\\) and exactly two of them are non‑sunny.\n\n**Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).**\n\n*Points with a coordinate equal to 1.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_v\\); if \\(b=1\\) it belongs to \\(L_h\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).*  \nSuch a point satisfies \\(a+b=t+1\\) for a unique integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nWe show that it lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nThe line \\(L_t\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,n+2-t)\\); its equation can be written as  \n\n\\[\n(y-1)(t-1)=(n+2-2t)(x-1). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) and use \\(a+b=t+1\\) (i.e. \\(b=t+1-a\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(t+1-a-1\\bigr)(t-1)=(n+2-2t)(a-1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (t-a)(t-1)=(n+2-2t)(a-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth sides expand to the same expression \\( (t-1)(t-a) = (t-1)(t-a)\\); hence the equality holds, proving that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (8) and therefore lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly two lines are non‑sunny, i.e. \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Lemma 3 – obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{For each }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ there exists a family of }n\n\\text{ distinct lines satisfying (1) and having exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nFor each \\(t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_t\\) is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nFrom the construction in Lemma 2 we have  \n\n\\[\nL_t\\cap T_n = D_t\\cap T_n = \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t+1\\}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence replacing the sunny line \\(L_t\\) by the non‑sunny line \\(D_t\\) does **not** remove any point of \\(T_n\\) from the covering.\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of Lemma 2 (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines).  \nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(r\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) replace \\(L_t\\) by \\(D_t\\).  \nThe resulting family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines (the diagonals \\(D_t\\) are all different and none coincides with \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\)).  \nIt still covers \\(T_n\\) because of (9), and the number of sunny lines becomes \\((n-2)-r\\).\n\nSince \\(r\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) to \\(n-2\\), we obtain configurations with every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 shows that no covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 2 provides a covering with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and Lemma 3 shows how to reduce the number of sunny lines arbitrarily down to \\(0\\) while preserving the covering property.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\] ∎"
