            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Notation and definitions  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden\ndirections  \n\n* horizontal \\((\\text{slope }0)\\);\n* vertical \\((\\text{slope } \\infty )\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{constant}\\)).\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal S\\) the set of sunny directions; \\(\\mathcal S\\) consists of all\nreal slopes except \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Necessity  \n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of lines satisfies the two conditions of the problem,\n\\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\)\ncannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Inductive statement  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nP(n):\\quad\\text{For every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a family }\n\\mathcal L_n(k)\\text{ of }n\\text{ distinct lines such that}\n\\]\n\n* (C) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);\n* (S) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\nWe prove \\(P(n)\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | family \\(\\mathcal L_3(k)\\) (three lines) |\n|------|-------------------------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\) (all non‑sunny) |\n| 1 | \\(y=x,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\) |\n| 2 | \\(y=x,\\;y=2x-1,\\;x=1\\) |\n| 3 | \\(y=x,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=3x-2\\) |\n\nEach family consists of three distinct lines, covers the three points, and has the prescribed number of sunny lines.  Thus \\(P(3)\\) holds.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Inductive step  \n\nAssume \\(P(n)\\) holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nFix a \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and let \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be a\nfamily satisfying (C) and (S).\n\nWhen we pass from \\(T_n\\) to \\(T_{n+1}\\) the **new** points are  \n\n\\[\nR_{n+1}=T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\n      =\\{(a,n+2-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(R_{n+1}\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}: x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.\n\nOur goal is to obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) while\ncontrolling the number of sunny lines.  The construction proceeds in two\nsub‑cases.\n\n---\n\n##### 5.1  Obtaining a configuration with the same number of sunny lines  \n\nChoose any point \\(P\\in R_{n+1}\\); for definiteness take \\(P=(1,n+1)\\).\nAdd the line \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) to the old family and **remove** from \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) a\nline that already passes through \\(P\\).  Such a line exists because the\noriginal family covered all points of \\(T_n\\) and, in particular, covered the\npoint \\((1,n)\\) (which belongs to \\(T_n\\)).  The line through \\((1,n)\\) and\n\\((1,n+1)\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is not sunny.  Replacing that\nvertical line by \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) yields a new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\setminus\\{x=1\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_{-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*All points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered*: the old lines still cover \\(T_n\\) except\nfor the point \\((1,n)\\), which is now covered by \\(\\ell_{-1}\\); the remaining\nnew points of \\(R_{n+1}\\) also lie on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).  \n*Number of sunny lines*: we removed a non‑sunny line and added another\nnon‑sunny line, so the count of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).  \nThus we have produced a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.2  Obtaining a configuration with one more sunny line  \n\nAgain start from \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).  This time we will replace a **sunny**\nline by a different sunny line that also passes through a chosen new point,\nthereby increasing the total number of sunny lines by one.\n\nSelect a sunny line \\(S\\in\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).  Because \\(S\\) is not horizontal,\nvertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\), its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).\nPick a point \\(P\\in R_{n+1}\\) that does **not** lie on \\(S\\); such a point exists\nbecause \\(S\\) can intersect the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) in at most one point, while\n\\(R_{n+1}\\) contains \\(n+1\\ge 4\\) points.\n\nThrough the two points \\(P\\) and a fixed point \\(Q\\) of \\(S\\) (choose any\n\\(Q\\in S\\cap T_n\\)) there is a unique line \\(S'\\).  By construction \\(S'\\) passes\nthrough \\(P\\) and through \\(Q\\); consequently \\(S'\\) also covers all points of\n\\(S\\) that lie on the segment \\(Q\\)–\\(P\\).  Moreover the slope of \\(S'\\) is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because it is determined by two points whose\ndifference vector is not horizontal, vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).  Hence\n\\(S'\\) is sunny.\n\nForm the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\setminus\\{S\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S',\\ell_{-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage*: the old lines other than \\(S\\) still cover all points of \\(T_n\\) that\nthey previously covered.  The point \\(P\\) (which was uncovered after removing\n\\(S\\)) is now on \\(S'\\); the remaining points of \\(R_{n+1}\\) are on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).\nThus every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.\n\n*Sunny count*: we removed one sunny line and added one sunny line (\\(S'\\)) and\none non‑sunny line (\\(\\ell_{-1}\\)).  Hence the total number of sunny lines has\nincreased by exactly one, giving \\(k+1\\).\n\nTherefore we have constructed a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 5.3  Completion of the induction  \n\nFrom a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) we have produced configurations for\n\\((n+1,k)\\) and \\((n+1,k+1)\\).  Starting with the base families for \\(n=3\\) and\napplying this “add‑one‑point’’ operation repeatedly, we can reach every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) for each \\(n\\).  Hence \\(P(n)\\) holds for all\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo other value is possible because at most \\(n\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be\nsunny.  The inductive construction above shows that every value in this range\nindeed occurs.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
