ution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Two non‑sunny lines covering almost all of \\(T_n\\)\n\nChoose the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_2:\\;y=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are **non‑sunny** because they are parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis and the \\(x\\)-axis respectively.\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line\n\\(x=n-1\\) after a translation of the line by an integer amount; more precisely,\nthe line  \n\n\\[\nL_{1,a}:\\;x=a\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(a\\).  \nSimilarly, if \\(b\\le n-2\\) the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_{2,b}:\\;y=b\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).\n\nThus, using the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2\\,\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(which consists of \\(2(n-2)\\) distinct non‑sunny lines) we already cover every\npoint of \\(T_n\\) except possibly those with both coordinates equal to\n\\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The uncovered corner\n\nThe only points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) are\n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_3=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_4=(2,n-1),\n\\]\n\nand, when \\(n\\) is even, also the point \\((\\tfrac{n}{2},\\tfrac{n}{2})\\).\nAll these points lie in the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\{n-1,n\\},\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains at most two points:\n\\[\nS=\\begin{cases}\n\\{(n-1,1),(1,n-1)\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{(n-1,1),(1,n-1),(\\tfrac n2,\\tfrac n2)\\}, & n