            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximal number of points on a line\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA line that is **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nConsider the three differences \\(d_i=a_i-b_i\\). By the pigeonhole principle two of them are equal, say \\(d_1=d_2\\). Then the segment joining \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) is horizontal, contradicting the fact that \\(L\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis. The same argument with the sums \\(s_i=a_i+b_i\\) shows that if two sums coincide the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); if two abscissas coincide the line would be vertical. Hence three points cannot lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the points \\((1,j),(2,j),\\dots ,(n+1-j,j)\\), i.e. exactly \\(n+1-j\\le n\\) points. The same bound holds for vertical lines \\(x=i\\) and for lines of slope \\(-1\\) (equations \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.  \nLet the non‑sunny lines be \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-k}\\). By Lemma 2 each \\(L_i\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points, so together they cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line covers at most two points, hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered, we obtain\n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+2k\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (2):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^2-kn+2k &\\ge \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\\\\\n\\frac{n^2-n}{2} &\\ge k(n-2)\\\\\nk &\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sufficiency of the bound\n\nWe now prove that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n#### Construction\n\n1. **Horizontal lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. **Points still uncovered.**  \n   The number of points not yet covered is\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   U &= |\\mathcal P_n|-(n-k)n\\\\\n     &=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n(n-k)\\\\\n     &=kn-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   By inequality (4) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   kn-\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\le 2k ,\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\(U\\le 2k\\).\n\n3. **Sunny lines for the remaining points.**  \n   Pair the \\(U\\) uncovered points arbitrarily into at most \\(k\\) unordered pairs; if \\(U\\) is odd, one point remains alone and is regarded as a “pair’’ of size one.\n\n   * For a pair consisting of two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) we draw the unique line through them.  \n     Because the two points lie in different rows (their \\(b\\)-coordinates differ) and different columns (their \\(a\\)-coordinates differ), the line cannot be horizontal, vertical, nor have slope \\(-1\\); consequently it is sunny.\n\n   * For a singleton point we draw any sunny line through it (for instance a line of slope \\(2\\) passing through the point).\n\n   In this way we use at most \\(k\\) sunny lines, and every previously uncovered point lies on one of them.\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\;+\\;(\\text{number of sunny lines}) = (n-k)+k = n ,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny by construction.\n\nThus a covering with the required properties exists for every integer \\(k\\) that satisfies (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (4) with the explicit construction, we obtain that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the non‑negative integers obeying  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\; } .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) one can realise the covering by the method described in Section 3, and no covering with a larger number of sunny lines is possible. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **s