ir union already covers all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate\n   does not exceed \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\); the remaining points\n   (if any) have \\(b>\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) and therefore\n   satisfy \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n   Those leftover points are covered by the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=k .\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(k=0\\) we simply omit this sub\u2011step.)  \n   All lines used so far are non\u2011sunny, and their total number is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil + k\\;\\le\\;n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u2011b (adding the required sunny lines).*  \n   If the total obtained in Step\u2011a is smaller than \\(n\\), we add\n   \\(n-\\bigl(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil+k\\bigr)\\) distinct sunny lines.\n   For instance, choose the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\alpha_i x + \\beta_i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil-k)\n   \\]\n\n   with slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) taken from the set\n   \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and with\n   distinct intercepts \\(\\beta_i\\).  \n   These lines are automatically sunny and do not affect the covering\n   of the points already achieved.\n\n   The construction yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and uses precisely\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\);\n   the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n   \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) is also sufficient.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\nConsequently a *non\u2011sunny* line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nA line of the form (3) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\), the points of \\(S_n\\) on it are\n\\((a,c)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); their number is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).\nThe case \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \nIf the line is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2\\), the points are\n\\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them.\n\u220e\n\n\n\nLet the given family of \\(n\\) distinct lines contain \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines\nand \\(k=n-m\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(mn\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Since every point of \\(S_n\\) must lie on at least one of the lines,\n(2) gives  \n\n\\[\nmn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nm\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (Construction)  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain\nall points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(h\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,h).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor each \\(i\\le h\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains the points\n\\((a,i)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\); 