lds  \n\n\\[\nmn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nm\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction)  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together\ncontain all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the \\(h\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny.  \nNext take the \\(k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=k .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (7)–(8) are distinct and non‑sunny.\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b\\le h\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from\n(7).  \nIf \\(b>h\\) then, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=n-h\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), we have \\(a\\le k\\); hence\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (8).  \nTherefore the \\(h+k\\) lines (7)–(8) already contain **every** point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n**Step 2 – adding sunny lines.**  \nIf \\(h+k=n\\) we are done: the family consists of the \\(h+k\\) non‑sunny lines,\nso the number of sunny lines is \\(k\\).\n\nIf \\(h+k<n\\) we still need \\(n-(h+k)\\) further lines.\nChoose any \\(n-(h+k)\\) distinct lines whose slopes are different from\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); for example\n\n\\[\ny=2x+1,\\;y=3x+2,\\dots ,y=(n-(h+k)+1)x+(n-(h+k)).\n\\]\n\nThese added lines are sunny, are distinct from each other and from the lines\nalready used, and they do not affect the covering already achieved.\nConsequently we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma 2 every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (6) can be realized, and (6) is\nnecessary.  Hence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
