y point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nmn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nm\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (1).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A covering by only horizontal and vertical lines\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\nv=n-h .\n\\]\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=h\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=v\\,\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non‑sunny and there are exactly \\(h+v=n\\) of them.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_0\\) contains the point.  \nIf \\(b>h\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a\\le v\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_0\\) contains the point. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Removing non‑sunny lines while preserving the covering\n\nWe show that we may delete up to \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and still retain a covering of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 3.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\subseteq\\mathcal L_0\\) be a subfamily containing at least \\(h\\) horizontal lines (i.e. all horizontals of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) or a superset thereof). Then \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument of Lemma 2 uses only the fact that **every** integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le h\\) appears as the ordinate of a horizontal line in the family.  \nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains all those \\(h\\) horizontals, the same reasoning shows that any point with \\(b\\le h\\) is covered.  \nFor a point with \\(b>h\\) we still have \\(a\\le v\\); the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\) are all present in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and we have not removed any of them (the removal process will never delete a vertical line because we keep all \\(v\\) verticals).  \nThus the point is covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\). ∎\n\nBecause \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=n\\) and \\(h=\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nn-h=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence we may delete any subset of at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) provided we keep all \\(h\\) horizontal lines; the remaining family still covers \\(S_n\\) by Lemma 3.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Inserting sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \nDelete exactly \\(k\\) vertical lines from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\); the resulting family \\(\\mathcal L\\) has  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|=n-k\n\\]\nand still contains all \\(h\\) horizontals, so by Lemma 3 it covers \\(S_n\\).\n\nFor each deleted vertical line we now add a **sunny** line that avoids all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSuch a line exists because the set \\(S_n\\) is finite; choose a slope \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and a real number \\(\\beta\\) such that the line \\(y=\\alpha x+\\beta\\) does not pass through any point of \\(S_n\\) (this is possible since the set of forbidden \\(\\beta\\) values is finite).  \nAll added lines are sunny, distinct from each other and from the remaining non‑sunny lines.\n\nThe final family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and still covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any configuration satisfying the problem’s conditions must have  \n  \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (inequality (5)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (construction of Sections 3–5).\n\nTherefore th