k\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.  \n   (iii) No \\(\\ell_i\\) coincides with any retained vertical line, because a vertical line has undefined slope.\n\n5. **Why the new family covers all points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n   *Points with \\(y\\le h\\).*  \n   They lie on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) which is present in (2).\n\n   *Points with \\(y>h\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b>h\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\).\n\n   *If \\(a>k\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is kept in (3) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\le k\\)*, we use the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = a + (h+1-a)=h+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((a,h+1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n   Moreover, because \\(\\ell_a\\) has slope \\(1\\), for any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\)\n\n   \\[\n   (a+t,\\;h+1+t)\\in\\ell_a . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Choose \\(t=b-(h+1)\\) (which is non‑negative because \\(b>h\\)).  \n   Since \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   t=b-(h+1)\\le (n+1-a)-(h+1)=n-h-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and \\(h=\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\), one checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   n-h-a\\ge 0 ,\n   \\]\n\n   so the chosen \\(t\\) is admissible.  \n   Consequently \\((a+t,\\;h+1+t)=(a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n\n   Thus every point with \\(y>h\\) and \\(a\\le k\\) is covered by the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n   Combining the two cases, every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n6. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The final family consists of  \n\n   * \\(h\\) horizontal lines,\n   * \\(v-k\\) retained vertical lines,\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\),\n\n   altogether \\(h+(v-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \n   The construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   such a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.  \n   Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The set of required points  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=c\\) contributes \\(c-1\\) lattice points, therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Non‑sunny lines and a necessary bound for \\(k\\)\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus a *non‑sunny* line has one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c ,\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb R .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA line of the form (2) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\), the admissible points are \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.  \nThe case \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \nIf the line is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2\\), the points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); their number is \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\nLet a configuration satisfying the problem consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n\\(m\\) of which are non‑sunny and \\(k=n-m\\) are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 the non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(S_n\\); using (1),\n\n\\[\nmn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow\nm\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (3).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A covering by only horizontal and vertical lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\nv=n-h .\n\\]\n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non‑sunny and \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=h+v=n\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \n\\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le h\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present in (4)) contains the point.  \nIf \\(b>h\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\); hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (also in (4)) contains the point. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Replacing the first \\(k\\) vertical lines by sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe keep  \n\n* all \\(h\\) horizontal lines of (4);\n* the last \\(v-k\\) vertical lines, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nx=k+1,\\;x=k+2,\\dots ,x=v .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) we introduce the line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_i:\\; y = x + (h+1-i)\\; } \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(\\ell_i\\).*  \n\n* Its slope is \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny (it is not horizontal, not vertical, and not of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n* The intercept \\(h+1-i\\) is different for different \\(i\\); consequently the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n* No \\(\\ell_i\\) coincides with any retained vertical line because a vertical line has undefined slope.\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x=k+1,\\dots ,x=v\\}}_{\\text{remaining vertical}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n*Case 1 – points with \\(y\\le h\\).*  \nSuch a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Case 2 – points with \\(y>h\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b>h\\). From the defining inequality we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Subcase 2a – \\(a>k\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is retained in (5) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Subcase 2b – \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nWe use the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nFrom (6) we have for any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\)\n\n\\[\n(a+t,\\;h+1+t)\\in\\ell_a .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(t=b-(h+1)\\) (which is non‑negative because \\(b>h\\)).  \nUsing (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt=b-(h+1)\\le (n+1-a)-(h+1)=n-h-a .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and \\(h=\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\), the right‑hand side \\(n-h-a\\) is at least \\(0\\); therefore the chosen \\(t\\) is admissible.  \nPlugging this \\(t\\) into (9) yields  \n\n\\[\n(a+t,\\;h+1+t)=(a,b)\\in\\ell_a .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) fall into one of the above cases, so \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) indeed covers the whole set.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Completion of the proof\n\n*Necessity.*  \nFrom Lemma 1 we derived the bound (3): any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n* sufficiency.*  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) constructed in (7) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(S_n\\) (Section 5).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]"
