            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The set of required points  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=c\\) contributes \\(c-1\\) lattice points, therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Non‑sunny lines and a necessary bound for \\(k\\)\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus a *non‑sunny* line has one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c ,\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb R .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA line of the form (2) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\), the admissible points are \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) of them.  \nThe case \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \nIf the line is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2\\), the points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); their number is \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\nLet a configuration satisfying the problem consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n\\(m\\) of which are non‑sunny and \\(k=n-m\\) are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 the non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\(mn\\) points of \\(S_n\\); using (1),\n\n\\[\nmn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow\nm\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (3).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A covering by only horizontal and vertical lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\nv=n-h .\n\\]\n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non‑sunny and \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=h+v=n\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \n\\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le h\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present in (4)) contains the point.  \nIf \\(b>h\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\); hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (also in (4)) contains the point. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Replacing the first \\(k\\) vertical lines by sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe keep  \n\n* all \\(h\\) horizontal lines of (4);\n* the last \\(v-k\\) vertical lines, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nx=k+1,\\;x=k+2,\\dots ,x=v .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) we introduce the line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_i:\\; y = x + (h+1-i)\\; } \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nThe intercepts \\(h+1-i\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct and none of them coincides with a vertical line.\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n\\cup\n\\underbrace{\\{x=k+1,\\dots ,x=v\\}}_{\\text{remaining vertical}}\n\\cup\n\\underbrace{\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\)\n\n*Case 1 – points with \\(y\\le h\\).*  \nSuch a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n*Case 2 – points with \\(y>h\\).*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(b>h\\). From the defining inequality we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Subcase 2a – \\(a>k\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is retained in (5) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Subcase 2b – \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nConsider the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (6) gives the point \\((a,\\,h+1)\\) on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell_a\\) has slope \\(1\\), for any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\)\n\n\\[\n(a+t,\\;h+1+t)\\in\\ell_a .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nt:=b-(h+1)\\ (\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\nThen \\((a+t,\\;h+1+t)=(a+b-(h+1),\\,b)\\).  \nBy (8) we have \\(a\\le k\\le v\\) and therefore  \n\n\\[\na+t = a + b-(h+1) \\le v .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the vertical line \\(x=a+t\\) is one of the retained vertical lines (5) and contains the point \\((a+t,\\,b)=(a,b)\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) is covered (first by \\(\\ell_a\\) to reach the column \\(x=a+t\\), then by the vertical line of that column).\n\nIn every situation the point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); hence \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Counting the lines  \n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of  \n\n* \\(h\\) horizontal lines,\n* \\(v-k\\) vertical lines,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n\nso the total number of lines is \\(h+(v-k)+k = h+v = n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom (3) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for **every** integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements, with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTh