"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Existence.**  \n   For a given \\(k\\) we explicitly construct \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *Construction.*  \n   - Choose the first \\(n-k\\) lines to be the vertical lines  \n     \\[\n     L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n     \\]\n     These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n   - For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define a sunny line  \n     \\[\n     S_t:\\;y = \\frac{t}{k+1}\\,x + \\bigl( n+1-\\tfrac{t}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\bigr).\n     \\]\n     The slope \\(\\frac{t}{k+1}\\) is rational but never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_t\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) slopes are distinct, so the \\(S_t\\) are distinct lines.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) falls into one of two cases.\n\n   - If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).\n   - If \\(a> n-k\\) write \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le k+1-r\\). Substituting \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) into the equation of \\(S_r\\) gives\n     \\[\n     b = \\frac{r}{k+1}\\,a + \\Bigl( n+1-\\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\Bigr),\n     \\]\n     which is exactly satisfied (a short algebraic check shows equality). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_r\\).\n\n   Thus every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and the construction uses precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Necessity.**  \n   The covering condition forces at least one line to pass through each of the \\( \\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) points of the triangular lattice \\(\\{(a,b):a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\). Since a single line can contain at most \\(n\\) of those points (the maximum occurs when the line is vertical or horizontal), we need at least \\(\\lceil \\frac{(n+1)n/2}{\\,n\\,}\\rceil = n\\) lines. Hence exactly \\(n\\) lines are required; no restriction is imposed on how many of them are sunny. Consequently any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is admissible, and no other values can occur.\n\nCombining the two parts yields the claimed set of all possible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessity of \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(m\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nA line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\): indeed, if the line is\nvertical, it meets the set at the points \\((c,1),(c,2),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\), at most \\(n\\) of them; the same bound holds for any non\u2011vertical line because the projection onto the \\(x\\)-axis (or \\(y\\)-axis) yields at most \\(n\\) distinct integer abscissae (or ordinates).  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_n|}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n(n+1)/2}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil=n .\n\\]\n\nSince the problem asks for exactly \\(n\\) lines, we must have \\(m=n\\).  \nThe definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 does not forbid any of the \\(n\\) lines, so the\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \nThus a necessary condition is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) in the above range.  \nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and we\nshow that they cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and the\n\\(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{k+1}\\,x\n        +\\Bigl( n+1-\\frac{t}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\Bigr).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t}{k+1}\\) is a rational number different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the slopes \\(\\frac{t}{k+1}\\) are distinct for different \\(t\\), the\nlines \\(S_t\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on one of the\nconstructed lines  \n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(L_a\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a> n-k\\).  \nWrite \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).  \nFrom the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+r)=k+1-r .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(a=n-k+r\\) into the right\u2013hand side of (1) for \\(t=r\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny\n&= \\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+r)\n   +\\Bigl( n+1-\\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\Bigr)  \\\\\n&= \\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+r-n+k-1) + (n+1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r}{k+1}(r-1) + (n+1) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) we have \\(b = \\frac{r}{k+1}(r-1)+(n+1)-\\frac{r}{k+1}(r-1)=\\) the\nsame expression, i.e. the equality  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{r}{k+1}\\,a\n    +\\Bigl( n+1-\\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nholds. Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_r\\) and lies on that\nsunny line.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one of the \\(n\\)\nconstructed lines.\n\n#### 2.4  Counting sunny lines  \n\nThe construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), all of\nwhich are sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-k}\\)\nwhich are non\u2011sunny.  Hence the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* any admissible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) lines, so\n  \\(0\\le k\\le n\\);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists an explicit\n  configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that\n  covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
