ber\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nDefine the following family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny (vertical) lines*  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{k+1}\\,x\n        +\\Bigl( n+1-\\frac{t}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\Bigr),\n        \\qquad t=1,2,\\dots ,k . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(\\frac{t}{k+1}\\) are rational numbers different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_t\\) is sunny, and distinct \\(t\\) give distinct\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Claim  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n\\(\\{L_i\\}\\cup\\{S_t\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n1. If \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(L_a\\).\n\n2. Suppose \\(a>n-k\\).  Write \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).\n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a = k+1-r . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(a=n-k+r\\) into (4) with \\(t=r\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   y\n   &=\\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+r)\n     +\\Bigl( n+1-\\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\Bigr)\\\\\n   &=\\frac{r}{k+1}\\bigl[(n-k+1)+(r-1)\\bigr]\n     +\\Bigl( n+1-\\frac{r}{k+1}(n-k+1) \\Bigr)\\\\\n   &=\\frac{r}{k+1}(r-1)+(n+1). \\tag{6}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (5) we have \\(b = (k+1-r) - (k+1-r-b) =\\frac{r}{k+1}(r-1)+(n+1)\\),\n   which coincides with the right–hand side of (6).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\n   the equation of \\(S_r\\) and therefore lies on \\(S_r\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting sunny lines\n\nThe construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\), all of which\nare sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-k}\\), none of\nwhich is sunny.  Hence the number of sunny lines in the configuration is\nprecisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* any feasible configuration must consist of \\(n\\) lines, so \\(0\\le k\\le n\\);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists an explicit\n  configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all required points and\n  containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
