d Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1.  A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-c,\n\\]\n\nso the possible values of \\(a\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them because \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nThe vertical case is symmetric.  \n\nIf the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\).  \nSince points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we must have \\(d\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\), the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) are \\((1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\); there are \\(d-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.  A sunny line contains at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with rational slope \\(m\\neq 0,-1\\) and rational intercept \\(q\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+q\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad ma=b-q.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, the left‑hand side is an integer, hence \\(m\\) must be a rational number whose denominator (in lowest terms) divides the integer \\(a\\).  \nWrite \\(m=\\frac{p}{r}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,r)=1\\) and \\(r\\ge 2\\) (the case \\(r=1\\) would give an integer slope, which would be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) only if the line were non‑sunny).  \