\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove that **every** integer satisfying (2 can be attained**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A family of non‑sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x=c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).\nThus the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{L_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole set \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Modifying the family to obtain any prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(T_n\\); for definiteness choose  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define a line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-1 = m_i\\,(x-i),\n\\qquad m_i=i+2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m_i=i+2\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is\nsunny.  Moreover \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\(P_i\\in T_n\\), and the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct because they have different\nslopes.\n\nNow remove from the family \\(\\mathcal V\\) the vertical lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\nThe remaining vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V'=\\{L_c\\mid k+1\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nare still non‑sunny, and there are exactly \\(n-k\\) of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification of the covering condition\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\):* the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) (by construction\n\\(S_a\\) contains \\((a,1)\\) and, because it