quently the resulting family contains \\(|S|\\) sunny lines and \\(n-|S|\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n4. **Achieving every \\(k\\).**  \n   The set of even numbers in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) has cardinality \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\).  \n   By first performing the replacements described in step 3 we can obtain any number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\).  \n   To obtain larger values of \\(k\\) we use an additional simple device: for any odd \\(s\\) the diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) can be replaced by the *horizontal* line \\(y=s-1\\) (or the *vertical* line \\(x=s-1\\)), which is also non‑sunny, and then we may replace the newly introduced horizontal (or vertical) line by a sunny line of slope different from \\(0,-1, \\infty\\) that passes through **exactly the same** points of \\(T_n\\) (such a line exists because the points on a fixed horizontal line have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, so we can choose a line of slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) passing through the leftmost and rightmost of them).  \n   This operation increases the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property. Repeating it we can raise the number of sunny lines from \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\) up to \\(n\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can construct a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A baseline configuration (no sunny lines)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_s\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\n\\]\n\nhence \\(\\ell_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince the sets \\(\\{\\ell_s\\cap T_n\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) partition \\(T_n\\), the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines of slope \\(+1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nd_t:\\;y=x+t .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_t\\) is sunny because its slope \\(+1\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(d_t\\) iff \\(b-a=t\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThese two conditions are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-2t ,\n\\qquad b=a+t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(d_t\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,a+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-2t\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(n+1-2t\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nObserve that the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) also contains precisely the same set of points, because  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2t+2}:\\;x+y=2t+2\\Longleftrightarrow b=2t+2-a,\n\\]\n\nand substituting \\(b=a+t\\) gives \\(a+t=2t+2-a\\), i.e. \\(a=n+1-2t\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2t+2}\\cap T_n = d_t\\cap T_n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing even‑indexed diagonals by sunny lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nE=\\{\\,2t+2\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\,\\}\n\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of even indices of the diagonals.  \nFor any subset \\(S\\subseteq E\\) define a new family of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_S=\n\\bigl\\{\\,\n\\begin{cases}\nd_t & \\text{if } 2t+2\\in S,\\\\[2pt]\n\\ell_s & \\text{if } s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\n\\end{cases}\n\\;\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (9), each line that is replaced contributes **exactly the same** points of \\(T_n\\) as the line it replaces.  \nTherefore every \\(\\mathcal L_S\\) still covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_S\\) equals \\(|S|\\), while the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\nSince \\(|E|=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), by choosing \\(S\\) of any cardinality \\(0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we obtain configurations with exactly that many sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Increasing the number of sunny lines beyond \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)\n\nLet \\(s\\) be an odd integer with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) (non‑sunny) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s,\\;a,b\\ge1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAll these points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(b=s-a\\); hence they also lie on the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nh_s:\\;y=s-1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(h_s\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \nPick the leftmost and the rightmost points of the set (12), namely \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\).  \nThrough these two points there is a unique line \\(p_s\\) whose slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_s=\\frac{1-(s-1)}{(s-1)-1}= -\\frac{s-2}{s-2}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nTo obtain a **sunny** line that still passes through the same two extreme points we may perturb the slope slightly: choose any real number \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\) and consider the line  \n\n\\[\nq_s:\\; y- (s-1)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(q_s\\) contains the two extreme points, it also contains every intermediate point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\); indeed those points lie on the segment joining the extremes, and a non‑vertical line is uniquely determined by two distinct points.  \nConsequently \\(q_s\\cap T_n = \\ell_s\\cap T_n\\).  \nSince \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\), the line \\(q_s\\) is sunny.\n\nThus for any odd \\(s\\) we can replace the diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) by a sunny line \\(q_s\\) without losing any covered point.  \nPerforming this replacement for as many odd indices as we wish adds one sunny line each time while preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Realisation of every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\n* Start from the baseline family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) (all \\(n\\) diagonals, no sunny line).\n* Use the operation of Section 3 to turn any chosen even‑indexed diagonal into a sunny line of slope \\(+1\\).  \n  This yields any number of sunny lines from \\(0\\) up to \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n* If a larger number of sunny lines is required, apply the operation of Section 4 to odd‑indexed diagonals, one by one.  \n  Each application increases the sunny‑line count by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nBecause there are exactly \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) odd indices, after all possible replacements we can reach the maximal value \\(k=n\\).  \nHence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
