ain at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Moreover, from the explicit description of \\(d_t\\) we see that a sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the inequality follows from the condition \\(a\\le n+1-2t\\) in (7)).  \n   Therefore the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining points, whose cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Each non‑sunny line is one of the diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\); the diagonals are pairwise disjoint on \\(T_n\\) and together they contain exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n   Consequently the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most the sum of the sizes of any \\(n-k\\) distinct diagonals, which is precisely the right–hand side of (2).  \n   This is possible only if the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(2i)\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   holds.  Simplifying (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no family satisfying the two conditions can contain more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   E=\\{\\,2t+2\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of even indices among \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n   For any subset \\(S\\subseteq E\\) define a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_S=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,d_t\\mid 2t+2\\in S\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\ell_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the e