th  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nd_t:\\;y=x+t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_t\\) is sunny because its slope \\(+1\\) differs from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(d_t\\) iff \\(b-a=t\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThese two conditions are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-2t,\\qquad b=a+t .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nd_t\\cap T_n=\\{(a,a+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-2t\\},\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n+1-2t\\) points.\n\nNow consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2t+2}:\\;x+y=2t+2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) iff \\(a+b=2t+2\\).  \nCombining this with (6) gives \\(a+(a+t)=2t+2\\), i.e. \\(a=n+1-2t\\).  \nThus the set of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) is exactly the set described in (7). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_{2t+2}\\cap T_n = d_t\\cap T_n\\;}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nso the sunny line \\(d_t\\) can replace the even‑indexed diagonal \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) without losing any required point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### 3.1 Intersection property of a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Its equation can be written  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c ,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) on \\(L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b=(1+m)a+c .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq-1\\), the right–hand side is an affine function of \\(a\\) with non‑zero slope; therefore two distinct points of the same diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) cannot both satisfy (11).  \nHence **\\(L\\) meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) in at most one point**.\n\n#### 3.2 Counting points that sunny lines can cover  \n\nFrom (6) we see that a sunny line \\(d_t\\) contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{t}\\bigl(n+1-2t\\bigr)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\npoints