l \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\) with \\(s_1\\neq s_0\\), then the two points would lie on distinct diagonals, contradicting (1) because the set of points of any diagonal is exactly the set of points with a fixed sum \\(a+b\\). Therefore **any sunny line can cover points belonging to at most one diagonal**.\n\n   *Counting diagonals.*  \n   The set \\(T_n\\) is partitioned by the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \n   Among these diagonals, those with even index are precisely \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) for \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\); there are \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of them.  \n   Because a sunny line can replace at most one diagonal, the total number of sunny lines cannot exceed the number of even‑indexed diagonals, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let  \n   \\[\n   E=\\{\\,2t+2\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}\n   \\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For any subset \\(S\\subseteq E\\) define the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_S=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,d_t\\mid 2t+2\\in S\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\ell_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   By (1) each diagonal \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) that is removed from the family is replaced by a sunny line \\(d_t\\) covering exactly the same points of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L_S\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The family contains \\(|S|\\) sunny lines and \\(n-|S|\\) non‑sunny lines, and all its members are distinct.  \n   Since \\(|E|=\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), by choosing \\(S\\) of any cardinality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\},\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombining the constructive lower bound with the rigorous upper bound (3) yields the required characterisation.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_s\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\ell_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince the sets \\(\\{\\ell_s\\cap T_n\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) partition \\(T_n\\), the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that duplicate an even‑indexed diagonal  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nd_t:\\;y=x+t .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_t\\) is sunny because its slope \\(+1\\) differs from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(d_t\\) iff \\(b-a=t\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThese two conditions are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-2t,\\qquad b=a+t .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nd_t\\cap T_n=\\{(a,a+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-2t\\},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n+1-2t\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2t+2}:\\;x+y=2t+2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) iff \\(a+b=2t+2\\).  \nCombining this with (10) gives \\(a+(a+t)=2t+2\\), i.e. \\(a=n+1-2t\\).  \nThus the set of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) is exactly the set described in (11). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_{2t+2}\\cap T_n = d_t\\cap T_n\\;}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nso the sunny line \\(d_t\\) can replace the even‑indexed diagonal \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) without losing any required point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### 3.1  Intersection property of a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line; write it as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) on \\(L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b=(1+m)a+c .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq-1\\), the right–hand side is an affine function of \\(a\\) with non‑zero slope; therefore two distinct points of the same diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) cannot both satisfy (14).  \nHence **\\(L\\) meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) in at most one point**.\n\n#### 3.2  A sunny line can replace at most one diagonal  \n\nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains a point of diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_0}\\). By the property above, \\(L\\) contains no other point of \\(\\ell_{s_0}\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) also contained a point of a different diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\) (\\(s_1\\neq s_0\\)), then the two points would have different sums \\(a+b\\), contradicting the fact that all points of a single line \\(L\\) satisfy a linear relation of the form (14) with a fixed slope \\(m\\neq-1\\). A more direct argument is that the set of points of any diagonal is characterised solely by the value of \\(a+b\\); a line cannot simultaneously realise two distinct values of \\(a+b\\) while also satisfying (14) with a fixed \\(m\\neq-1\\). Therefore **each sunny line can contain points from at most one diagonal**.\n\n#### 3.3  Counting diagonals  \n\nThe diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) with \\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) partition \\(T_n\\).  \nAmong them, the even‑indexed diagonals are \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) for \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\); there are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nsuch diagonals.  \nSince a sunny line can replace at most one diagonal, the total number of sunny lines in any admissible family cannot exceed the number of even‑indexed diagonals. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nE=\\{\\,2t+2\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}\n\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any subset \\(S\\subseteq E\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_S=\n\\bigl\\{\\,d_t\\mid 2t+2\\in S\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nBy (13) each diagonal \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) that is omitted from the family is replaced by the sunny line \\(d_t\\) covering exactly the same points of \\(T_n\\). Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_S\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe family contains \\(|S|\\) sunny lines and \\(n-|S|\\) non‑sunny lines, and all its members are distinct.  \n\nSince \\(|E|=\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), by choosing \\(S\\) with any cardinality  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\},\n\\]\n\nwe obtain a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe constructions show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is realizable, and the argument in §3 proves that no larger \\(k\\) is possible. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
