            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_s\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\ell_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince the sets \\(\\{\\ell_s\\cap T_n\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) partition \\(T_n\\), the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that duplicate an even‑indexed diagonal  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nd_t:\\;y=x+t .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_t\\) is sunny because its slope \\(+1\\) differs from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(d_t\\) iff \\(b-a=t\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThese two conditions are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-2t,\\qquad b=a+t .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nd_t\\cap T_n=\\{(a,a+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-2t\\},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n+1-2t\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2t+2}:\\;x+y=2t+2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) iff \\(a+b=2t+2\\).  \nCombining this with (10) gives \\(a+(a+t)=2t+2\\), i.e. \\(a=n+1-2t\\).  \nThus the set of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) is exactly the set described in (11). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_{2t+2}\\cap T_n = d_t\\cap T_n\\;}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nso the sunny line \\(d_t\\) can replace the even‑indexed diagonal \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) without losing any required point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### 3.1  Intersection property of a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line; write it as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) on \\(L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b=(1+m)a+c .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq-1\\), the right–hand side is an affine function of \\(a\\) with non‑zero slope; therefore two distinct points of the same diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) cannot both satisfy (14).  \nHence **\\(L\\) meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) in at most one point**.\n\n#### 3.2  A sunny line can replace at most one diagonal  \n\nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains a point of diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_0}\\). By the property above, \\(L\\) contains no other point of \\(\\ell_{s_0}\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) also contained a point of a different diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\) (\\(s_1\\neq s_0\\)), then the two points would have different sums \\(a+b\\), contradicting the fact that all points of a single line \\(L\\) satisfy a linear relation of the form (14) with a fixed