            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_s\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\ell_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince the sets \\(\\{\\ell_s\\cap T_n\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) partition \\(T_n\\), the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that duplicate an even‑indexed diagonal  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nd_t:\\;y=x+t .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(d_t\\) is sunny because its slope \\(+1\\) differs from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(d_t\\) iff \\(b-a=t\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThese two conditions are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-2t,\\qquad b=a+t .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nd_t\\cap T_n=\\{(a,a+t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-2t\\},\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n+1-2t\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2t+2}:\\;x+y=2t+2 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nA point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) iff \\(a+b=2t+2\\).  \nCombining this with (11) gives \\(a+(a+t)=2t+2\\), i.e. \\(a=n+1-2t\\).  \nThus the set of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) is exactly the set described in (12). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_{2t+2}\\cap T_n = d_t\\cap T_n\\;}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nso the sunny line \\(d_t\\) can replace the even‑indexed diagonal \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) without losing any required point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### 3.1  Intersection of a sunny line with a diagonal  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line; write it as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+c ,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(L\\) then \\(b=ma+c\\), whence  \n\n\\[\na+b=(1+m)a+c . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed diagonal \\(\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\\) the right–hand side of (16) is an affine function of the integer variable \\(a\\) with slope \\(1+m\\neq0\\) (because \\(m\\neq-1\\)).  \nTherefore the equation  \n\n\\[\n(1+m)a+c=s\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nhas **at most one integer solution** \\(a\\); consequently a sunny line meets each diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) in **at most one point** of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  What “replacing a diagonal’’ means  \n\nIn a covering family we need **all** points of a diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) (i.e. the whole set \\(\\ell_s\\cap T_n\\)) to be present on some line of the family.  \nBecause a sunny line meets any diagonal in at most one point, the only way a sunny line can replace a diagonal is when that diagonal consists of a single point.  \nThe diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points; the only single‑point diagonals are \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\).  \nBoth are odd‑indexed when \\(n\\ge3\\), so the only diagonals that can be replaced by a sunny line are the **even‑indexed** ones \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).  \nFor each such diagonal we have exhibited the sunny line \\(d_t\\) that contains **exactly** the same set of points, as shown in (14).\n\n#### 3.3  Each sunny line can replace at most one diagonal  \n\nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) were to replace two distinct diagonals \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{s_2}\\) with \\(s_1\\neq s_2\\).  \nBoth \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{s_2}\\) are even‑indexed, hence each contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) replaced \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\) it would have to contain **all** points of \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\cap T_n\\); similarly for \\(\\ell_{s_2}\\).  \nThus \\(L\\) would meet the diagonal \\(\\ell_{s_1}\\) in at least two points, contradicting the result of §3.1 that a sunny line meets any diagonal in at most one point.  \nHence **any sunny line can replace at most one diagonal**.\n\n#### 3.4  Counting replaceable diagonals  \n\nAmong the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) (\\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)) the even‑indexed ones are  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{2t+2}\\qquad (t=0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1),\n\\]\n\nand there are  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nof them.  \nBecause each sunny line can replace at most one diagonal, the total number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in any admissible family satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nE=\\{\\,2t+2\\mid 1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}\n\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any subset \\(S\\subseteq E\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_S=\n\\bigl\\{\\,d_t\\mid 2t+2\\in S\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nBy (14) each diagonal \\(\\ell_{2t+2}\\) that is omitted from the family is replaced by the sunny line \\(d_t\\) covering exactly the same points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore \\(\\mathcal L_S\\) still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe family contains \\(|S|\\) sunny lines and \\(n-|S|\\) non‑sunny lines, and all its members are distinct.  \n\nSince \\(|E|=\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), by choosing \\(S\\) of any cardinality  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\},\n\\]\n\nwe obtain a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe constructions show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is realizable, and the argument in §3 proves that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
