                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the critical errors pointed out in the bug report and corrected the argument.  \nThe complete, rigorous solution is that the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Structure of the required point set.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) with\n   \\(a+b=s\\) lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\{\\ell_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   none of which is sunny (each is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), and it covers\n   every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n2. **How a sunny line can intersect the diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\).**  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(L:\\;y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n   If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(L\\) then \\(b=ma+c\\) and therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b=(1+m)a+c . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) the right–hand side of (2) is an affine\n   function of the integer variable \\(a\\) with non‑zero slope\n   \\(1+m\\neq0\\).  Consequently the equation \\((1+m)a+c=s\\) has **at most one**\n   integer solution \\(a\\); thus **any sunny line meets each diagonal\n   \\(\\ell_s\\) in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).**  (In particular a sunny\n   line can never contain the whole set \\(\\ell_s\\cap T_n\\) when that set\n   has more than one element.)\n\n3. **Consequences for covering \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n   *The only diagonal that can be covered by a sunny line alone* is the\n   diagonal that consists of a single lattice point.  Among the diagonals\n   \\(\\ell_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) the only one of size 1 is \\(\\ell_2\\),\n   whose unique point is \\((1,1)\\).  All other diagonal