t one\nadditional line can be present; this line may be sunny or may be the\nremaining diagonal \\(\\ell_2\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_2\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).  That point can\nbe covered either by \\(\\ell_2\\) (non\u2011sunny) or by any sunny line passing\nthrough \\((1,1)\\).  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions attaining the bounds\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The family  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n  \\]\n  consists of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and clearly covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Choose any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for concreteness let  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Replace \\(\\ell_2\\) by \\(L\\) and keep the other diagonals:\n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_1=\\{L\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The set \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) contains exactly one sunny line, still has \\(n\\)\n  distinct lines, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\) lies\n  on \\(L\\), all other points lie on the appropriate \\(\\ell_s\\)).\n\nBoth values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are therefore realizable, and (8) shows that\nno larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]"
