; therefore it has **at most one**\ninteger solution.  Consequently:\n\n> **Lemma 1.** For any sunny line \\(L\\) and any \\(s\\) the set\n> \\(L\\cap D_s\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Necessity of the non‑sunny diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) for \\(s\\ge3\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every\npoint of \\(T_n\\).  Suppose \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsider a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge3\\).  Since \\(|D_s|\\ge2\\), at least two\ndifferent points of \\(D_s\\) must belong to lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\nBy Lemma 1 a single sunny line can contribute **at most one** of those points,\nso the two points cannot be covered solely by sunny lines.  Hence at least\none line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain **both** points of \\(D_s\\); the only\nline that contains the whole set \\(D_s\\) is \\(\\ell_s\\) itself (it is the line\n\\(x+y=s\\)).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell_s\\in\\mathcal L\\qquad\\text{for every }s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains the \\(n-1\\) distinct non‑sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_3,\\ell_4,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).  Because \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), at most one\nadditional line can be present; this line may be sunny or may be the\nremaining diagonal \\(\\ell_2\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_2\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).  That point can\nbe covered either by \\(\\ell_2\\) (non‑sunny) or by any sunny line passing\nthrough \\((1,1)\\).  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions attaining the bounds\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The family  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n  \\]\n  consists of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines and clearly covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Choose any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for concreteness let  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Replace \\(\\ell_2\\) by \\(L\\) and keep the other diagonals:\n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_1=\\{L\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The set \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) contains exactly one sunny line, still has \\(n\\)\n  distinct lines, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\) lies\n  on \\(L\\), all other points lie on the appropriate \\(\\ell_s\\)).\n\nBoth values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are therefore realizable, and (8) shows that\nno larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
