            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_s\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is\nsunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Diagonal decomposition\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigsqcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s ,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand from the definition  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and for every \\(s\\ge3\\) we have \\(|D_s|\\ge2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines intersect a diagonal in at most one lattice point  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis,\nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Thus a sunny line can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=mx+c,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAssume \\((a,b)\\in L\\cap D_s\\).  Then \\(b=ma+c\\) and \\(a+b=s\\); eliminating\n\\(b\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n(1+m)a+c=s .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1+m\\neq0\\), equation (7) is a linear equation in the integer\nvariable \\(a\\) with non‑zero coefficient; therefore it has **at most one**\ninteger solution.  Consequently:\n\n> **Lemma 1.** For any sunny line \\(L\\) and any \\(s\\) the set\n> \\(L\\cap D_s\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Any line containing two points of a diagonal must be \\(\\ell_s\\)\n\nLet a (not necessarily sunny) line \\(L\\) contain two distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) of the same diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(s\\ge2\\)).\nBoth points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i=s\\) for \\(i=1,2\\).  The equation of the line\nthrough them is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-b_1}{x-a_1}= \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\; .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities \\(a_1+b_1=s\\) and \\(a_2+b_2=s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_1-a_2)+(b_1-b_2)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (9) into (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-b_1}{x-a_1}= \\frac{-(a_2-a_1)}{a_2-a_1}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(y-b_1 = -(x-a_1)\\), i.e. \\(x+y = a_1+b_1 = s\\).  Therefore the line\nthrough the two points is exactly \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\n> **Lemma 2.** If a line contains two distinct points of a diagonal\n> \\(D_s\\) then the line coincides with \\(\\ell_s : x+y=s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Consequences for a covering family of lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every\npoint of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFor any \\(s\\ge3\\) the diagonal \\(D_s\\) has at least two points.\nBy Lemma 1 a single sunny line can contribute at most one of them, so the\ntwo points of \\(D_s\\) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines.\nHence some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain **both** points of \\(D_s\\);\nby Lemma 2 that line is \\(\\ell_s\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\ell_s\\in\\mathcal L\\qquad\\text{for every }s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) already contains the \\(n-1\\) distinct non‑sunny lines\n\\(\\ell_3,\\ell_4,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).  Since \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), at most one\nadditional line can be present; this extra line may be sunny or may be the\nremaining diagonal \\(\\ell_2\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) consists of a single point, so it can be\ncovered either by \\(\\ell_2\\) (non‑sunny) or by any sunny line passing through\n\\((1,1)\\).  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Realising the two possible values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the family  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  It consists of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines and clearly covers every point of\n  \\(T_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Choose any sunny line through the point \\((1,1)\\); for concreteness let  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Replace \\(\\ell_2\\) by \\(L\\) and keep the other diagonals:\n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_1=\\{L\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  This set contains exactly one sunny line, still has \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n  and covers all points of \\(T_n\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\), all\n  other points lie on the appropriate \\(\\ell_s\\)).\n\nBoth values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable, and (11) shows that no larger\nvalue is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
